\subsubsection{Soft control condition}

When the control condition is soft so that the division probability follows a normal distribution with a mean $a =T$ and variance $\sigma^2$, the division probability is expressed as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:timersoft}
\phi(a) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma} e^{-\frac{(s-T)^2}{2\sigma^2}}.
\end{equation}
Under this soft control condition, the governing equations become:
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
&\dfrac{\partial f(s, a, t)}{\partial t} + \dfrac{\partial f(s, a, t)}{\partial a} + \dfrac{\partial (v(s) f(s, a,t))}{\partial s} = -(\alpha(t) + \phi(a)) f(s, a, t),\\
&\alpha(t) = \int_0^{+\infty} f(s, 0, t) \mathrm{d} s - \int_0^{+\infty}  \int_0^{+\infty} (\phi(a) f(s, a, t)) \mathrm{d} s \mathrm{d} a,\\
&f(s, 0, t) = 2 \int_0^{+\infty} \int_0^{+\infty} p(s, s')\phi(a) f(s', a, t) \mathrm{d} s' \mathrm{d} a,\\
&f(0, a, t) = f(+\infty, a, t) = 0.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}

To explore the effects of the soft control condition, we assumed a constant cell growth model, $v(s) = 0.2$. Using individual-cell-based stochastic simulations, we investigated the cell size distribution under both the timer and soft control conditions (Fig. \ref{fig:7}a). The two controls yield similar size distributions; however, the soft control condition yields smaller sizes than the standard timer control. Fig. \ref{fig:7}b shows the density distributions of both birth size and division size under the soft control condition.

