\emph{Universal spin models are universal approximators}.---
Let us now show that universal spin models are universal approximators of probability distributions. This means that universal spin models, via their Boltzmann distributions, can approximate arbitrary probability distributions over spin configurations to arbitrary precision.

Observe first that any probability distribution $p$ on $\{0,1\}^V$ can be simulated by a spin system $T$.
To this end, construct $T$ such that configurations $\mathbf{t}$ with non-zero probability $p(\mathbf{t})>0$ are assigned low energy, 
and those with zero probability are assigned high energy. Specifically, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ham prob}
        H_T(\mathbf{t}) = \begin{cases}
            -\log(p(\mathbf{t})) 
            & \text{if } p(\mathbf{t})>0 \\
            \Delta             & \text{if }  p(\mathbf{t})=0 .
        \end{cases}
\end{equation} 
Details are provided in \cref{lem:sim prob canonical}.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:univ approx}
Universal spin models are universal approximators.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Consider any probability distribution $p$ and the corresponding spin system $T$ (\cref{eq:ham prob}).
    By assumption a universal spin model can simulate $T$ for arbitrary cut-offs. 
    Simulations preserve Boltzmann distributions (\cref{lem:sim approx density}), hence the claim. 
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:univ approx thm}
    Both RBMs and DBMs 
    are universal approximators. 
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The claim follows from \cref{thm:RBM univ}, \cref{thm:DBM univ} and  \cref{thm:univ approx}. 
\end{proof}

This is a UAT for RBMs and DBMs. What is more interesting is that the unravelling of the proofs of \cref{thm:RBM univ} and \cref{thm:DBM univ} provides a new construction of the universal RBM or DBM.
While existing proofs of UATs for RBMs and DBMs were quite different (cf.\ \cite{LR08,Mo15}), 
ours emanate from a common framework and hence are similar.   
Moreover, both our proofs are similar to the original RBM construction.  

\emph{Deep belief networks}.---
Let us extend the characterization of universal approximators to deep Belief Networks (DBNs) and thus beyond energy based models.
Similar to DBMs, DBNs are defined on layerwise fully connected graphs.  However, their output is not given by their Boltzmann distribution but is  the marginal of 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DBN Ham}
    q(\mathbf{h}_0, \ldots, \mathbf{h}_k) = 
    \left( \prod_{i=0}^{k-2} p_i(\mathbf{h}_i \, \vert \,  \mathbf{h}_{i+1})  \right) p_{k-1}(\mathbf{h}_{k-1},\mathbf{h}_k)  
\end{equation}
over visible spins, 
where $p_i(\mathbf{h}_i,\mathbf{h}_{i+1})$ is the Boltzmann distribution of the $i$-th RBM and $p_i(\mathbf{h}_i \, \vert \, \mathbf{h}_{i+1})$ is the corresponding conditional distribution (cf. \cref{eq: Ham DBM}).
For $k=2$ and $S=R_0+R_1$ this marginal can be expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2 DBN}
    q_{V_0}[S](\mathbf{h}_0) = \sum_{\mathbf{h}_1}p[R_0](\mathbf{h}_0 \, \vert \, \mathbf{h}_1) p_{V_1}[R_1](\mathbf{h}_1).
\end{equation}
Following \cite{Mo15}, the corresponding DBM output equals
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2 DBM}
     p_{V_0}[S](\mathbf{h}_0) = \sum_{\mathbf{h}_1}p[R_0](\mathbf{h}_0 \, \vert \, \mathbf{h}_1) (p_{V_1}[R_0]\ast p_{V_1}[R_1])(\mathbf{h}_1),
\end{equation}
where $(p\ast p')(x)=\frac{1}{N}p(x)p'(x)$ denotes the pointwise, normalized product of probability distributions with normalization constant $N$.
Thus, the difference between the DBN and DBM output is the  factor $p_{V_1}[R_0]$, namely the top-level marginal of $R_0$, appearing in the latter. In \cite{Mo15} this factor is termed backwards signal of $R_0$, as it distinguishes the directional information flow of DBNs from the undirected one of DBMs.

Under certain assumptions, the backwards signal of $R_0$ can be absorbed in a change of couplings of $R_1$, yielding a system $S'=R_0+R_1'$ whose DBN output approximates the DBM output of $S$ \footnote{By changing couplings from $R$ to $R'$ we mean that $R$ and $R'$ are defined on the same interaction hypergraph and thus act on the same spins, but differ in their local energy functions and specifically the parameters that define them.}.
More precisely, when the top-level marginal of $R_0$ is \emph{approximately spiked}, i.e.\ up to $\mathcal{O}(e^{-\Delta})$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:top level marg assumption}
        p_{V_1}[R_0](\mathbf{h}_1) = \begin{cases}
            a  \ &\text{if} \ \mathbf{h}_1 = \mathbf{x} \\ 
             b \ &\text{else},
        \end{cases}
\end{equation}
for some configuration $\mathbf{x}$ and real numbers $a,b$, 
then changing couplings of $R_1$ allows to define systems $R_1'$ and $S'=R_0+R_1'$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dbn dbm sim}
          q_{V_0}[S'](\mathbf{h}_0) = p_{V_0}[S](\mathbf{h}_0) + \mathcal{O}(e^{-\Delta}).
\end{equation}
This is proven in \cref{lem:DBN DBM 2}. 
For $k>2$ and $S=R_0+\ldots + R_{k-1}$, 
backwards signals appear for all RBMs except for $R_{k-1}$. 
In some cases, they can be absorbed into changes of couplings by applying \cref{lem:DBN DBM 2} iteratively, as we will see in \cref{lem:DBM-DBN sim}.
This means that some DBMs can be simulated by DBNs. 
We shall call such DBMs \emph{effectively directional}.


We now construct a class of effectively directional DBMs which are  universal approximators, as we will prove by means of  \cref{thm:univ approx}. Since they can be simulated by DBNs, this will entail a UAT for DBNs, which is our final destination, \cref{thm:DBN UAT}.

To this end, we consider DBMs whose top-level RBM is a linear combination of two flag RBMs for configurations $\mathbf{z}$ and $\mathbf{x}$, 
