\section{Conclusion}
We introduce \textbf{3D-Generalist}, a framework that formulates text-driven 3D world creation as a sequential decision-making problem.
The framework can be extended to integrate with other generative methods, such as image-to-3D reconstruction~\cite{sun2024partial,xu2024instantmesh}, image-to-PBR material generation~\cite{lopes2024material} or 3D asset generation models~\cite{nvidia2024edify3d}, enabling a fully generative graphics pipeline. Beyond content creation, the use of explicit 3D representations also facilitates generative simulation—enabling the automatic generation of environments for training RL policies~\cite{sun2024factorsim,wang2023robogen}.
Limitations of \method are further discussed in the supplementary material.

%%% include for arxiv version
Despite the demonstrated efficacy of \method in scaling up the generation of data grounded in 3D environments, \method has its limitations in generating \textit{functional} layouts. An important future direction is to address how we can better generate layouts of a 3D space and consider the functional and contextual use of objects in a space based on the given prompt.

%Despite the demonstrated efficacy of \method in scaling up the generation of data grounded in 3D environments, \method has limitations. More specifically, the layouts generated by \method tend to overlook the functional use of a space given the prompt, which presents another important direction for future improvements.
%, improving its ability to generate more context-aware layouts.
% in structured, context-aware 3D scene generation.

