\begin{abstract}
Portfolio optimization faces challenges due to the sensitivity in traditional mean-variance models. The Black-Litterman model mitigates this by integrating investor views, but defining these views remains difficult. 
This study explores the integration of large language models (LLMs) generated views into portfolio optimization using the Black-Litterman framework.
Our method leverages LLMs to estimate expected stock returns from historical prices and company metadata, incorporating uncertainty through the variance in predictions. 
We conduct a backtest of the LLM-optimized portfolios from June 2024 to February 2025, rebalancing biweekly using the previous two weeks of price data.
As baselines, we compare against the S\&P 500, an equal-weighted portfolio, and a traditional mean-variance optimized portfolio constructed using the same set of stocks.
Empirical results suggest that different LLMs exhibit varying levels of predictive optimism and confidence stability, which impact portfolio performance.
The source code and data are available at \textcolor{hotpink}{\url{https://github.com/youngandbin/LLM-MVO-BLM}}.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

% ### **Background and Motivation (연구 배경과 필요성)**

Portfolio optimization is a fundamental problem in finance, traditionally approached using mean-variance optimization \cite{markowitz1952jf}.
However, a well-known limitation of mean-variance optimization is its sensitivity to input parameters, such as expected returns and covariance estimates. 
The Black-Litterman asset allocation model \cite{black1992global} was introduced to mitigate these problems by incorporating investor views into expected returns through a Bayesian framework. 
% Unlike traditional mean-variance optimization, which relies purely on historical data, the Black-Litterman model blends prior market equilibrium returns with subjective investor views, weighted by confidence levels. 
% This approach not only stabilizes return estimates but also allows for a more flexible and systematic integration of expert knowledge and quantitative signals. 
Although the Black-Litterman model remains widely used in asset management industry, including Goldman Sachs, a key challenge lies in how to define and quantify these views in a systematic and reliable manner. 
% Traditionally, these views have been derived from qualitative assessments or ad hoc quantitative models, limiting scalability and objectivity. 
Recent advances in LLMs have shown promise in processing large amounts of financial data and generating insights that could potentially be used to formulate investor views \cite{zhao2024revolutionizing} and optimized portfolios \cite{hwang2025decision}.
This raises the question: can we leverage LLMs to generate structured and data-driven views that enhance portfolio construction while maintaining the robustness of the Black-Litterman framework?

% ### **Existing Approaches and Their Limitations (기존 접근법 및 한계)**

Traditional investor views are based on subjective judgment, expert analysis, or historical trends \cite{idzorek2007step,kara2019hybrid}.
Some approaches use quantitative forecasting models \cite{feng2019temporal,wang2022mg}, but these require extensive feature engineering and assumptions.
A major limitation is the lack of an automated, scalable method to generate investor views that dynamically adapt to market conditions.

% ### **Contribution of This Study (본 연구의 기여점)**

We propose a novel approach that integrates LLM-generated views into the mean-variance optimization using the Black-Litterman model.
Our approach leverages LLMs to predict expected stock returns based on historical performance and company metadata, systematically incorporating uncertainty quantification as a variance of LLM predictions into the model.
This method allows for a data-driven, automated, and scalable way to generate views, reducing human bias in portfolio construction.

% ### **Research Objective and Methodology (연구 목표 및 실험 개요)**

We evaluate recent open source models including \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}  \cite{dubey2024llama}, \texttt{Gemma-7B} \cite{team2024gemma}, and \texttt{Qwen-2-7B} \cite{yang2024qwen2technicalreport}, as well as the proprietary \texttt{GPT-4o-mini} \cite{openai2024gpt4o} \footnote{Although the exact size of \texttt{GPT-4o-mini} has not been officially disclosed, \cite{abacha2024medec} estimate it to be within the 8 billion parameter range, making it comparable to the open-source models used in our study.}, in generating views for S\&P 500 constituents.
All selected models are of comparable size, enabling a fair evaluation under similar computational constraints.
The predicted returns are integrated into the Black-Litterman model, with view confidence quantified using the variance of multiple responses from LLMs.
The resulting optimized portfolio is backtested from June 2024 to February 2025 against the S\&P 500 benchmarks, with performance evaluated using various metrics including Sharpe ratio, cumulative return, and maximum drawdown.

\section{Data and Methodology}

\subsection{Data Description}

We use historical stock price data from the top 50 S\&P 500 constituents by market capitalization, as of March 26, 2025, sourced from Yahoo Finance to evaluate our proposed methodology. The dataset spans from \texttt{June 2024 to February 2025}. To ensure adaptability to changing market conditions, the portfolio is rebalanced every two weeks. At the beginning of each two-week period, the LLM is provided with the past two weeks of stock price data to generate average return predictions for the following two weeks, and a new portfolio is constructed accordingly. This process is repeated iteratively, ensuring that each rebalancing is based on the most recent available data. As a result, for the evaluation of portfolio performance, we assess the outcomes of trading over an 8-month period, using portfolios constructed with views generated by the LLM.
In addition, we incorporate company metadata (detailed in Figure \ref{fig:prompt}) to improve the contextual understanding of the LLM and improve return predictions.

\subsection{Selection of Large Language Models}

To incorporate predictive views, we employ four models with the most recent versions available, each with a knowledge cutoff prior to the evaluation period of our dataset. This ensures that the models do not have direct exposure to future stock performance, preserving the validity of the evaluation. We used instruction-tuned models to ensure consistency in the generated return predictions.

