\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{\texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}}: A scalable version of the \texttt{LLaMA-3.1} family, optimized for text comprehension, reasoning, and numerical tasks. Released in July 2024 by Meta.

    \item \textbf{\texttt{Gemma-7B}}: A lightweight yet high-performance model designed for general natural language processing tasks. Released in February 2024 by Google DeepMind.

    \item \textbf{\texttt{Qwen-2-7B}}: A scalable and efficient model with a particular focus on fine-grained reasoning and multi-turn interactions. Released in June 2024 by Alibaba.

    \item \textbf{\texttt{GPT-4o-mini}}: A lightweight version of \texttt{GPT-4o}, optimized for cost-effective reasoning and fast inference. Released in July 2024 by OpenAI. 

\end{itemize}

We used vLLM \cite{kwon2023efficient} for optimized inference. Each model was prompted to predict the average daily return for the next two weeks based on the past two weeks of stock returns and company metadata. To quantify confidence in the predictions, each model was queried 10 times per stock, and the variance of the responses was used as a measure of confidence. 

\subsection{LLM Prompt Templates}

To extract predictive views from LLMs, we employ a structured prompt designed to estimate expected stock returns based on historical data and company metadata, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:prompt}. To assess the LLMs’ ability to predict future stock performance, we provide essential inputs for return forecasting: a time series of past daily returns and company metadata for each stock. These inputs are included in the user prompt alongside the system prompt, allowing the LLMs to generate informed return estimates based on relevant historical and contextual data.


\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
    \begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=blue!80!black, colback=blue!10!white, coltitle=white, title=System prompt for making views, width=1\textwidth]
    \small
    You are providing analysis on \texttt{{\{\{DATE\}\}}}. Predict the average daily return for the next two weeks based on the information provided about a stock's past performance  
    You will receive the following inputs:  
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Daily Returns}: The stock's daily returns, a time-series from the past two weeks.  
        \item \textbf{Company Information}: 
            \begin{itemize}
                \item Ticker: The stock symbol.  
                \item Company Name: The name of the company. 
                \item GICS Sector: The Global Industry Classification Standard sector.
                \item GICS Sub-Industry: The sub-industry classification.    
            \end{itemize}
    \end{itemize}

    \# Steps 

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Analyze the Time-Series Data: Review the historical daily returns to identify patterns, trends, or anomalies that may affect future performance.  
        \item Incorporate Company Information: Use the details from the GICS sector and sub-industry, along with the company symbol and name, to contextualize the predicted performance within its industry.  
        \item Predict Future Returns: Estimate the average daily returns for the next two weeks based on the analysis of available data.  
    \end{enumerate}

    \# Output Format 

    \begin{quote}
    Return a single float value that represents the predicted average daily return for the stock over the next two weeks, without any additional commentary or explanation.  
    \end{quote}

    \# Notes

    \begin{itemize}
    \item Ensure the prediction considers the quantified data from the time-series  
    \item Make calculations based on statistical trends from daily returns data  
    \item Make calculations without additional interpretation or commentary  
    \end{itemize}

    \end{tcolorbox}
    \caption{System prompt for making views}
