%         Picking matrix     & $\mathbf{P} \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times n}$            & Identity matrix $\rightarrow$ one view per asset \\
%         Confidence matrix  & $\boldsymbol{\Omega} \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times k}$   & Diagonal matrix from prediction variances (uncertainty) \\
%         \hline
%     \end{tabular}
%     \caption{LLM-generated inputs for the Black-Litterman model.}
%     \label{tab:llm_views}
% \end{table}


\paragraph{View Vector ($\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^{k}$)}
indicates \(k\) views. For each stocks, the LLM is queried 30 times with an identical prompt, instructing it to predict the stock's average daily return for the next period. The expected return for each stock is then defined as the mean of the 10 responses. Here, \(k\) represents the number of views (predictions) and the vector $\mathbf{q}$ contains the expected return for each view.

\paragraph{Picking matrix ($\mathbf{P} \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times n}$)}
specifies which of the \(n\) stocks correspond to each of the \(k\) views. Since we generate an independent expected return for every stock, we use an identity matrix. This structure ensures that each LLM-generated view directly corresponds to a single asset, rather than a linear combination of multiple stocks.

\paragraph{Confidence Matrix ($\boldsymbol{\Omega} \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times k}$)}
represents uncertainty of \(k\) views. To quantify the reliability of LLM-generated views, we compute the variance of the 10 responses for each stock. This variance serves as an uncertainty measure and is used to construct the diagonal elements of the confidence matrix $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$. A higher variance indicates greater uncertainty in the LLM's predictions, resulting in a lower weight assigned to that view in the optimization process.

By incorporating these elements into the Black-Litterman framework, we adjust the market equilibrium returns to reflect the predictive insights provided by LLMs. The resulting posterior expected returns are then used in the portfolio optimization process.


