The comparative performance of all portfolios is presented in Table~\ref{tab:vertical_performance_comparison} and Figure~\ref{fig:cumulative_returns}. This analysis highlights how portfolios optimized using LLM-generated return forecasts within the Black-Litterman framework compare against traditional baselines, including the S\&P500 index, an equally weighted (EW) portfolio, and a mean-variance optimized (MVO) portfolio.

\textbf{BLM-Llama} achieved the highest cumulative return (CAGR: 0.6731) and demonstrated remarkable performance throughout the test period. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:cumulative_returns}, the BLM-Llama consistently outperformed all other strategies across the entire backtest period. These results suggest that the return forecasts generated by Llama were both optimistic and stable, enabling consistently favorable asset allocations.


\textbf{EW} exhibited robust performance, recording the lowest volatility (std: 0.0074) and the highest annualized Sharpe ratio (2.5075) among all strategies. The EW portfolio served as a strong benchmark, offering stable returns with minimal risk. In contrast, the \textbf{MVO} portfolio, delivered the weak performance (CAGR: -0.0189, Sharpe: 0.0087), reflecting its limitations, such as its sensitivity to estimation errors in expected returns and covariances, and the tendency to produce concentrated and unstable portfolios.

\textbf{BLM-Gemma} and \textbf{BLM-GPT} significantly underperformed. Both recorded negative cumulative returns, indicating that their forecasts led to poor asset allocations. \textbf{BLM-Qwen} yielded modest but unremarkable results.

Overall, these findings demonstrate that LLM-generated views can substantially enhance portfolio performance. Among the tested models, BLM-Llama proved the most effective, while Gemma and GPT illustrate the potential pitfalls of unstable.




