To further investigate how LLMs formulated asset views, we analyzed the cross-sectional distribution of the predicted daily returns that were actually used in our portfolio construction. Each LLM generated 10 return predictions (views) per stock, and we previously used the average of these 10 predictions as the final view for each stock. In this analysis, we collected those averaged views across all 50 stocks at each rebalancing point and visualized their distribution. Figure~\ref{fig:boxplot} displays time-series boxplots constructed from these 50 averaged views, with the y-axis clipped to a range of -2\% to 2\% for clearer visual comparison.

Compared to other models, BLM-Llama produced a larger number of extreme outliers in its views, particularly on the negative side. These outliers often led to portfolios heavily concentrated in a few selected stocks—a pattern observable in Figure \ref{fig:total_compare}. The model consistently assigned highly negative expected returns to less promising stocks, which likely acted as a strong filtering mechanism. This decisive separation appears to have contributed positively to its superior portfolio performance.

In contrast, BLM-Gemma exhibited greater variability and a downward bias in its return forecasts. Its medians often trended below zero, and the overall spread was wider, reflecting unstable or pessimistic assessments over time.

BLM-Qwen showed relatively neutral and cautious behavior. Its median predictions were typically centered near zero with narrower dispersion than Gemma or GPT. This conservative view generation may explain the moderate but stable portfolio results of the model.

