Let us, finally, try to correct for one of our calculation's limitations, the truncated single-particle space.
To extrapolate our results to an infinite number of harmonic oscillator shells ($\eMax = \infty$),  we repeat the calculations with $\eMax =8, 10$ and $14$. 
The final values for the structure factors $a_\alpha$ in \nuclide[129]{Xe}, \nuclide[199]{Hg}, and \nuclide[255]{Ra} appear in Table~\ref{tab:Qs_mu_S43}.  
The physics we've already discussed makes the structure factors in \nuclide[225]{Ra} several orders of magnitude  larger than those in the other two nuclei.  
Because the calculation in that nucleus clearly includes all the excited states that are important, it is presumably more reliable as well.  

We compare our final results with those of other calculations in Fig.~\ref{fig:ai_comparison_methods}(a)-(c).  The figure shows that our results for \nuclide[225]{Ra} are consistent with, though slightly smaller than, those of the Skyrme Hartree-Fock calculations constrained by the data on intrinsic octupole moments~\cite{Dobaczewski:2018PRL}.   In the near-spherical isotopes \nuclide[199]{Hg} and \nuclide[129]{Xe}, differences in predictions are much larger.  We have already pointed out that our  mean-field configurations containing only quadrupole-octupole deformed one-quasiparticle states omit the isovector giant-dipole resonance and much of the isoscalar giant-dipole resonance. Such states and other multiquasiparticle states can be included in the MR-CDFT framework, but at a nontrivial cost that we postpone until a later paper. 




 \paragraph{\textbf{Conclusions.}}
We have used MR-CDFT to carry out the first beyond-relativistic-mean-field studies of nuclear Schiff moments, in the diamagnetic atoms $\nuclide[129]{Xe}$, $\nuclide[199]{Hg}$, and $\nuclide[225]{Ra}$. 
We have treated the beyond-mean-field correlations generated by shape fluctuations and symmetry restoration in the generator coordinate method, with projection onto states with good parity, particle number, and angular momentum. 
Our results reveal a strong correlation between the contributions of nuclear intermediate states to the Schiff moment structure factors and the electric dipole transition strengths from those states to the ground state, suggesting that experimental measurements of these transitions can constrain the models used to predict Schiff moments.  
Beyond-mean-field correlations turn out to modify Schiff moments significantly; in \nuclide[225]{Ra}, for which we consider our results more reliable than in the other two isotopes, they weaken the enhancement from octupole deformation.  
These findings underscore the importance of an accurate treatment of correlations for understanding CP violation at low energies.

 It is worth noting in closing that in non-relativistic chiral effective-field theory, a CP-violating contact interaction between nucleons occurs at leading order~\cite{deVries:2020l};  it is not clear to us whether the same is true in the relativistic version of the effective theory.  
 And we have not attempted to quantify either the systematic or statistical uncertainties associated with our predicted Schiff moments. 
 The systematic uncertainty arises mainly from the choice to use CDFT, the choice of the density functional, and the particular states that we have added through the GCM.  
 We expect these uncertainties to be larger in \nuclide[129]{Xe} and \nuclide[199]{Hg} than in \nuclide[225]{Ra}.  
 Statistical uncertainties can be assessed in future work within the recently developed subspace-projected CDFT approach~\cite{Zhang:2024_Letter}, which has already demonstrated its effectiveness in quantifying the uncertainty in nuclear matrix elements for neutrinoless double-beta decay. 
 %We anticipate the statistical uncertainty to be small. In short, the present work represents a significant step toward precision studies of nuclear Schiff moments at the beyond-mean-field level. 
 %We do not expect that any of these future extensions will substantially alter the main conclusions of this study.


