The constraint is absent in the limit of $\eta\to\infty$ and the complete network is globally uniformly adaptive. Choosing $\alpha=0, b = 1, \beta =0$ and small~$\epsilon$, we expect to observe the dynamics studied in Ref.~\onlinecite{cestnik2025continuum}, and, in particular, states of partial synchronization for $\sigma = 0.7068$ and $ a=1$, $\epsilon = 0.1$.
Numerical simulations carried out for $\eta=10^4$ produce order parameters consistent with partial synchronization, see Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1}a).  (See Appendix~\ref{app:initial_conditions} for initial conditions).
While the edge weights fluctuate due to the adaptive nature of the system (Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1}b)), they tend to accumulate near $w_{kl}\approx 1,2$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1} panels c) and f)). 
We can explain this as follows.
Since most phases for partial synchrony are approximately phase synchronized, $\cos(\phi_j-\phi_k)\approx 1$, most edge weights assemble near $w_{kj}\approx a+b =2 $. Drifting oscillators, on the other hand, result in $\cos(\phi_j-\phi_k)\approx 0$, with fluctuation amplitudes scale with the value of the (small but fixed) parameter~$\epsilon$, implying that $w_{kj}\approx b = 1$.

For the constrained dynamics ($\eta \to 0$), the order parameter~$Z(t)$ will eventually come arbitrarily close to zero,
consistent with incoherent oscillations.
The constraint enforces the decoupling of the two subpopulations with $w_{kj} \to 0$ as $\eta \to 0$, while edge weights connecting vertices within each subpopulation remain non-zero, adaptive and co-evolve with their associated phases. While a few oscillator remain locked for non-zero $\eta=0.1$, resulting in a minor accumulation of weights near $w\approx a + b = 2$, the majority of oscillators undergo incoherent oscillations so that the associated phase differences average to zero, implying that associated weights accumulate near $w\approx a = 1$.

The subgraph structure becomes increasingly prominent in the weights $w_{kj}$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1}, panels d) to f)) as~$\eta$ decreases. Enforcing the two-subpopulation subgraph constraint effectively halves the coupling strength, leading to the loss of coherent oscillations; 
on the other hand, augmenting~$\eta$, the constraint is weakened so that an increasing number of edges may co-evolve with their associated phases. Thus, it becomes possible for two oscillators~$i$ and~$j$ to lock, and given that $\beta=0$, associated edge weights are up-regulated. In effect, this increases the average coupling strength of the network so that more and more oscillators are recruited into a locked / partially synchronized state with increasing~$\eta$. The subgraph constraint effectively controls the level of ``accumulated'' coupling. 

At a specific value of $\eta$ the minima of the order parameter $|Z|$ detaches from zero, clearly indicating a transition from incoherent oscillations to partial synchrony.
Note that edge weights consistent with incoherence ($w=0,1$) and partially synchronized states ($w=1,2$) are prominent features of the histograms in Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1} panel c) for a large range of $\eta $ values); however, weights also accumulate near values in between as they gradually assemble in new (quasi-)stationary configurations before and after this incoherence-coherence transition.

Simulations using varying initial conditions indicate that the incoherence-coherence transition does not occur for a unique critical value of~$\eta$. In fact, it is possible to continue an incoherent solution from left to right throughout the range of $\eta$ values shown in the bifurcation diagram. This observation is explained by the high multi-stability observed for the adaptive Kuramoto model (see also Ref.~\onlinecite{cestnik2025continuum}). (This is also reflected in the observation that the order parameters~$|Z_1|, |Z_2|$ are slightly asymmetric due to asymmetrically chosen initial conditions). Thus, one expects that the system may begin to nucleate to partial synchrony for a whole range of $\eta$ values. 
Regardless, the structure of the histogrammatic bifurcation diagram for the edge weights~$w$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:subgraph_constraint_example_1} panel~(c)) remains similar, the actual distributions of edge weights~$w_{kj}$ (away from the values $0,1$, and $2$) depends on the various phase configurations the system settles on, given a specific initial condition for the quasi-continuation in $\eta$. 
We leave the question whether this transition is restricted to a certain interval for the constraint parameter $\eta$ for a future study.

