%\||g|\wedge |h|\|=
\|g\wedge h\|=2\sup\{(g\wedge h)(\bm{1}_A): A\subseteq \omega\}=0. 
\end{split}
    \end{displaymath}   
Therefore $f=g-h$ for some $g,h\in \ell_\infty^{\,\prime}$ such that $g\wedge h=0$. We conclude by \cite[Theorem 1.5(b)]{MR2262133} that $g=f^+$ and $h=f^-$, hence the decomposition is unique (note that the definition of $g$ coincides with the one provided in \eqref{eq:definitiong}, hence also the definition of $h:=g-f$).

