    Lastly, suppose $\|f\|<2$ and that $\mathcal{I}$ is not maximal, so that $1-\|f\|/2>0$ and there exist two distinct maximal ideals $\mathcal{J}_1$, $\mathcal{J}_2$ which extend $\mathcal{I}$. Then it is easy to see that the function $g$ defined in \eqref{eq:definitiong} with $f_0(x):=\mathcal{J}_1\text{-}\lim x$ is different from the one choosing $f_0(x):=\mathcal{J}_2\text{-}\lim x$ (note that they are both $\mathrm{S}_\mathcal{I}$-limits, as it follows by Theorem \ref{thm:secondrepresenation}). Hence there exist at least two decompositions of $f$. %This completes the proof. 
