\subsection{Evaluation Protocols and Implementation Details}\label{sec:protocol_implement}
To evaluate the performance of current advanced LLMs on the proposed \method{}, we conduct a fair comparison across 9 open-source models (\llmname{C2S-Pythia-160M} \cite{levine2024cell2sentence}, \llmname{C2S-Pythia-410M} \cite{levine2024cell2sentence}, \llmname{C2S-Pythia-1B} \cite{rizvi2025scaling}, \llmname{Qwen-2.5-7B} \cite{yang2024qwen2}, \llmname{Qwen-2.5-32B} \cite{yang2024qwen2}, \llmname{Qwen-2.5-72B} \cite{yang2024qwen2}, \llmname{Llama-3.3–70B} \cite{grattafiori2024llama}, \llmname{DeepSeek-V3} \cite{liu2024deepseek}, and \llmname{DeepSeek-R1} \cite{guo2025deepseek}) and 5 closed-source models (\llmname{GPT-4} \cite{achiam2023gpt}, \llmname{GPT-4o-mini} \cite{openaigpt4omini}, \llmname{GPT-4o} \cite{openaigpt4o}, \llmname{GPT-4.1-mini} \cite{openaigpt41}, and \llmname{GPT-4.1} \cite{openaigpt41}).
Among these, only the \llmname{C2S-Pythia} series models were trained specifically on single-cell analysis tasks and thus considered specialist models, while all others are generalist models.
We perform inference using the vLLM framework \cite{kwon2023efficient} for open-source models and official APIs for closed-source models.
During preliminary testing, we observed that all models struggle to make reasonable decisions under open-ended question settings. Therefore, we convert all questions into multiple-choice format by including a list of candidate answers directly in the prompt. 
The experiments are conducted under both zero-shot and few-shot settings.
Model performance is then evaluated using standard metrics: precision score, recall score, F1 score, and overall accuracy.

