\title[]{Physical Formalism Of Directional Quantum Evolution Theory}

\author{\fnm{Tarek} \sur{Yehia}}
\email{202228364@std.sci.cu.edu.eg}
\affil{\orgdiv{Faculty of Science}, \orgname{Cairo University}, Cairo, Egypt}

\abstract{Here, we introduce the Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET), a covariant reformulation of quantum mechanics where evolution takes place along a four-vector-defined arbitrary timelike direction. This method restores space-time symmetry and provides a geometric interpretation of energy as a frame-dependent projection by substituting a directional derivative for the traditional time derivative. DQET establishes a conserved probability current, supports proper-time evolution, and recovers the Schrödinger in suitable bounds. It provides a covariant solution to the quantum twin conundrum and predicts observable phase discrepancies between systems traveling along distinct trajectories. With encouraging extensions to curved spacetime, the theory offers a cohesive framework for relativistic quantum evolution.

}
\keywords{Quantum Mechanics, Special Relativity, General Relativity}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
The role of time in quantum mechanics has long been a subject of foundational debate. Unlike position, momentum, or energy, time enters standard quantum theory not as an observable but as an external classical parameter. This asymmetry manifests starkly in the time-dependent Schrödinger equation, where evolution proceeds with respect to a fixed coordinate time \( t \), which is treated as absolute and non-dynamical.
The asymmetry between time and space in quantum mechanics has long been a conceptual issue \cite{Butterfield2002}.
This treatment of time is at odds with the principles of special and general relativity, which treat space and time on equal footing as components of a unified spacetime manifold. The tension becomes particularly acute in quantum gravity and relativistic quantum information theory, where the notion of a global, observer-independent time is either ambiguous or absent altogether. Pauli famously objected to the possibility of defining a self-adjoint time operator canonically conjugate to the Hamiltonian, highlighting the deeper incompatibility between time and quantum observables\cite{Pauli1933}.
Over the decades, several approaches have attempted to address this asymmetry. The Page–Wootters formalism\cite{PageWootters}, for instance, sought to model time relationally within entangled states. Canonical quantum gravity and the Wheeler–DeWitt equation\cite{DeWitt1967} suggest a timeless perspective where dynamics emerges from correlations. In this work, we propose a reformulation of quantum dynamics, the Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET), that directly addresses these foundational issues. In DQET, time is no longer an external background parameter but is replaced by an intrinsic directional parameter
\[
s = v_\mu x^\mu
\]
defined by a fixed, future-directed timelike unit vector \( v^\mu \). This vector specifies the direction of evolution in spacetime and plays the role of a local "arrow of time". The fundamental evolution equation becomes
\[
i\hbar\,v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi(x) = \mathcal{E}\,\psi(x)
\]
where \( \mathcal{E} = p^\mu v_\mu \) is the directional energy: a Lorentz scalar associated with motion along \( v^\mu \). This formulation preserves Lorentz covariance and reduces to the standard Schrödinger equation when \( v^\mu = (1, 0, 0, 0) \).
The aim of DQET is to construct a covariant quantum theory in which:
Evolution is expressed with respect to intrinsic, observer-dependent hypersurfaces.
Energy is interpreted geometrically as the projection of four-momentum.
The time–energy uncertainty relation acquires a rigorous covariant meaning.
The framework remains valid even in the absence of a global foliation.
This paper develops the physical formalism of DQET in flat spacetime with constant \( v^\mu \), derives its core predictions, and demonstrates its consistency with known quantum limits. We also outline how the theory provides a natural platform for generalization to curved spacetime and non-inertial observers, potentially offering a path toward resolving the conceptual tension between time, causality, and quantum coherence.
Attempts to model time measurements using ideal quantum clocks have led to fundamental bounds on spatial and temporal resolutions, arising from the interplay between quantum mechanics and gravity~\cite{SaleckerWigner1958}.
\section{Main Equation and Physical Interpretation}
We propose that quantum evolution proceeds along an arbitrary timelike direction in spacetime, specified by a future-directed unit four-vector \( v^\mu \). The evolution of the wavefunction \( \psi(x) \) is governed by the covariant equation:
\begin{equation}
    i\hbar\, v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi(x) = \mathcal{E}\, \psi(x)
    \label{eq:DQET}
\end{equation}
Here:
\( \psi(x) \) is the quantum wavefunction defined over spacetime.
\( v^\mu \) is a fixed timelike unit four-vector defining the direction of evolution.
\( \partial_\mu \) denotes the spacetime gradient.
\( \mathcal{E} = p^\mu v_\mu \) is the directional energy: the projection of the system’s four-momentum onto the direction \( v^\mu \)
This equation generalizes the conventional time-dependent Schrödinger equation by replacing the partial derivative with respect to coordinate time \( t \) with a directional derivative along \( v^\mu \). The affine parameter \( s = v^\mu x_\mu \) defines internal time along the system's worldline.
\textbf{Physical Interpretation:}
The term \( i\hbar v^\mu \partial_\mu \) generates translations in the internal (directional) time parameter \( s \), rather than external coordinate time.
The energy observable \( \mathcal{E} \) represents the Lorentz-invariant scalar projection of the system’s four-momentum \( p^\mu \) onto the direction of evolution \( v^\mu \) (the energy measured in the direction of evolution.)
The formulation restores spacetime symmetry and enables a covariant, observer-dependent description of quantum evolution.
We can rewrie it by defining a generalization of the standard time derivative into a directional derivative along \( v^\mu \):
\begin{equation}
D_v := v^\mu \partial_\mu
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the core equation of Dynamical Quantization along Evolutionary Time (DQET) takes the covariant form:
\begin{equation}
i \hbar D_v \psi(x) = \mathcal{E} \psi(x)
\end{equation}
This formulation formally treats time and space on equal footing by avoiding any special role for a fixed coordinate time \( t \). However, the explicit introduction of the timelike vector \( v^\mu \) selects a preferred foliation of spacetime via the hypersurfaces
\begin{equation}
\Sigma_s := \{ x^\mu \mid v^\mu x_\mu = s \}
\end{equation}
where \( s \in \mathbb{R} \) labels the foliation parameter (evolution time).
The scalar quantity \( \mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu \), obtained as the projection of the system's four-momentum onto the evolution direction, plays the role of energy in this framework. It reduces to the usual Hamiltonian in the frame where \( v^\mu = (1, 0, 0, 0) \), recovering the standard Schrödinger equation.
To demonstrate Lorentz covariance of the formulation, consider a Lorentz transformation \( \Lambda^\mu_{~\nu} \), under which the coordinates and evolution vector transform as:
\begin{equation}
x'^\mu = \Lambda^\mu_{~\nu} x^\nu, \qquad v'^\mu = \Lambda^\mu_{~\nu} v^\nu
\end{equation}
The gradient transforms covariantly:
\begin{equation}
\partial'_\mu = \frac{\partial}{\partial x'^\mu} = \Lambda_\mu^{~\nu} \partial_\nu
\end{equation}
Then, the directional derivative becomes:
\begin{equation}
D'_v = v'^\mu \partial'_\mu = (\Lambda^\mu_{~\rho} v^\rho)(\Lambda_\mu^{~\sigma} \partial_\sigma)
= v^\rho \delta^\sigma_\rho \partial_\sigma = v^\mu \partial_\mu = D_v
\end{equation}
So, \( D_v \) is invariant under Lorentz transformations, and the evolution equation retains its form:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar D_v \psi'(x') = \mathcal{E} \psi'(x')
\end{equation}
Therefore, the DQET equation is manifestly Lorentz covariant  and
This covariant structure ensures that quantum evolution is compatible with the relativistic geometry of spacetime and allows for a flexible, observer-dependent formulation of quantum dynamics.
\section*{Proof: $D_v = v^\mu \partial_\mu$ is the Directional Derivative Along the Curve $x^\mu(s)=v^\mu s$}
We consider the one‐parameter family of points in spacetime defined by the integral curve
\begin{equation}
x^\mu(s) \;=\; v^\mu\,s
\end{equation}
where \(v^\mu\) is a fixed timelike four‐vector.  We wish to show that, for any smooth scalar field \(f(x)\),
\begin{equation}
D_v f \;\equiv\; v^\mu\,\partial_\mu f(x)
\end{equation}
coincides with the ordinary derivative of \(f\) along the curve \(x^\mu(s)\):
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{ds}\bigl[f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)\bigr]
\end{equation}
\subsection*{Parametric Derivative Along the Curve}
By definition, the composition \(f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)\) is a function of the single variable \(s\)and Its total derivative with respect to \(s\) is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{ds}f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)
&= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^\nu}\,\frac{d x^\nu(s)}{ds}
\\
&= \partial_\nu f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)\,\frac{d}{ds}\bigl(v^\nu s\bigr)
\\
&= \partial_\nu f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)\,v^\nu
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since \(x(s)\) is an arbitrary integral curve of \(v^\mu\), we may drop the explicit argument \(x(s)\) and write simply
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{ds}\bigl[f(x)\bigr]
= v^\nu\,\partial_\nu f(x)
\end{equation}
\subsection*{ Identification with $D_v$}
By definition in DQET, the directional derivative operator along \(v^\mu\) is
\begin{equation}
D_v \;=\; v^\mu\,\partial_\mu
\end{equation}So, acting on any scalar \(f(x)\),
\begin{equation}
D_v f(x) \;=\; v^\mu\,\partial_\mu f(x)
\;=\;\frac{d}{ds}\bigl[f\bigl(x(s)\bigr)\bigr]
\end{equation}
We have shown that \(D_v\) exactly generates the rate of change of fields along the worldline \(x^\mu(s)=v^\mu s\).  In other words, \(D_v\) is the covariant directional derivative along the integral curves of \(v^\mu\).  
\begin{equation}
{D_v f = \frac{d}{ds}\bigl[f(x(s))\bigr]}
\end{equation}
\subsection*{Resolving the Time Tension}
Time functions as an external quantity \( t \) in standard quantum physics, unrelated to the symmetry of spacetime and unrelated to any observable or operator. On the other hand, special relativity views time and space as one and the same dimensions of spacetime, which are symmetrically defined by Lorentz transformations and four-vectors.
It has long been acknowledged that this asymmetry is a conceptual weakness, particularly when trying to make relativistic frameworks and quantum theory work together. 
DQET resolves this tension by replacing the external time parameter with an intrinsic, observer-dependent direction in spacetime \( v^\mu \). Evolution is redefined not with respect to a global time variable, but along the affine parameter \( s \equiv \int v^\mu dx_\mu \), which corresponds to the proper-time evolution experienced by an observer.
The directional Schrödinger equation:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi = \mathcal{E} \psi
\end{equation}
is manifestly covariant, removing coordinate time's prominent role and reestablishing the harmony between space and time. In this way, rather than being absolute, time in DQET is relational, internal, and dynamic.
This reformulation creates fresh avenues for their unification and offers a natural link between the relativistic and quantum conceptions of time. 
\section{Physical Interpretation of the Observer–Velocity Vector \(v^\mu\)}
In DQET, the four–vector \(v^\mu\) plays a dual role: it both labels the ‘‘direction of evolution’’ in spacetime and encodes the kinematic relation between the quantum system and the chosen frame of reference.  A consistent physical interpretation can be given as follows:
\textbf{External Observer Frame.}  
In scattering or clock–comparison experiments, one may identify \(v^\mu\) with the true four–velocity of the laboratory apparatus (or ‘‘observer’’) carrying the quantum system.  In this picture,  
    \begin{equation}
      v^\mu = \frac{dx^\mu_{\rm lab}}{d\tau_{\rm lab}}
    \end{equation}
and \(\mathcal{E}=p_\mu v^\mu\) is simply the energy measured in that frame.
\textbf{Intrinsic Evolution Direction.}  
 For an isolated free particle, one can equivalently view \(v^\mu\) as an \emph{internal} dynamical label namely, the direction in which the system’s own phase coalesces.  In this sense, \(v^\mu\) becomes a property ‘‘carried’’ by the particle’s phase structure (much like a ‘‘clock pointer’’) and need not coincide with any physical laboratory’s motion.
\textbf{Choice by Physical Context}  
In any concrete setup, \(v^\mu\) must be fixed by experimental or dynamical boundary conditions:
In time–of–flight or tunneling–time measurements, one takes \(v^\mu\) to be the four–velocity of the detector or clock circuit.
In cosmological or curved spacetime applications, \(v^\mu\) may be aligned with a preferred time–like Killing field or the local comoving frame.
When studying intrinsic phase evolution of a wavepacket, \(v^\mu\) can be chosen proportional to the group–velocity four–vector of that packet.
\textbf{Not Always a Physical Observer}  
Importantly, \(v^\mu\) need not correspond to a real detector worldline: it may be regarded as an \emph{auxiliary} geometric object that selects the ‘‘split’’ between spatial and temporal evolution in the theory.  Physical predictions (for example: phase differences) remain invariant under any consistent choice of \(v^\mu\) so long as the corresponding \(\mathcal{E}\) is kept fixed.
\section{Unification of \(\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu\) and \(\mathcal{E} = mc^2\)}
Let a particle of rest mass \(m\) have four‑momentum \(p^\mu\) satisfying the mass‐shell condition
\begin{equation}
p^\mu p_\mu = m^2c^2
\end{equation}
and let \(v^\mu\) be an arbitrary timelike evolution vector with normalization
\begin{equation}
v^\mu v_\mu = c^2
\end{equation}
We define the directional energy by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} \;\equiv\; p_\mu\,v^\mu
\end{equation}
\subsection{General Frame: \(\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu\)}
By construction, \(\mathcal{E}\) is the Lorentz‑invariant projection of the four‑momentum along \(v^\mu\).  In an inertial frame in which the observer’s four‑velocity is
\begin{equation}
u^\mu = \frac{v^\mu}{c},\qquad u_\mu u^\mu = 1
\end{equation}
one often writes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = p_\mu\,v^\mu = c\,p_\mu\,u^\mu
\end{equation}
so that \(p_\mu u^\mu\) is the energy measured by that observer (divided by \(c\))
\subsection*{Rest Frame: \(\mathcal{E} = mc^2\) as a Special Case}
In the particle’s own rest frame, the four‑momentum takes the form
\begin{equation}
p^\mu_{\rm rest} = \bigl(m c,\,\mathbf{0}\bigr)
\end{equation}
and a natural choice for the evolution vector is
\begin{equation}
v^\mu_{\rm rest} = \bigl(c,\,\mathbf{0}\bigr)
\end{equation}
so that \(u^\mu_{\rm rest}=v^\mu_{\rm rest}/c=(1,\mathbf{0})\).  Hence
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}
= p_\mu^{\rm rest}\,v^\mu_{\rm rest}
= (m c)\,(c)
= m c^2
\end{equation}
Thus, the familiar rest‐energy relation \(E=mc^2\) emerges as the special case of the general definition \(\mathcal{E}=p_\mu v^\mu\) when \(v^\mu\) is chosen parallel to the particle’s own four‑momentum.
\subsection*{Mathematical Consistency}
More formally, decompose \(p^\mu\) into components parallel and perpendicular to \(v^\mu\):
\begin{equation}
p^\mu
= \frac{p_\nu v^\nu}{v_\lambda v^\lambda}\,v^\mu \;+\; p_\perp^\mu
= \frac{\mathcal{E}}{c^2}\,v^\mu + p_\perp^\mu
\end{equation}
with \(v_\mu p_\perp^\mu=0\).  Squaring both sides and using \(p^2=m^2c^2\) and \(v^2=c^2\) gives
\begin{equation}
m^2c^2
= \frac{\mathcal{E}^2}{c^2} + p_{\perp}^2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\mathcal{E}^2 = m^2c^4 - c^2\,p_{\perp}^2
\end{equation}
In the special case \(p_\perp^\mu=0\) (i.e.\ \(p^\mu\parallel v^\mu\)), this reduces to
\(\mathcal{E}^2=m^2c^4\) and hence \(\mathcal{E}=mc^2\).
\subsection*{Physical Interpretation: Klein–Gordon Equation And DQET}
From a physical perspective, it is important to distinguish between two roles played by the equations under consideration:
The DQET equation,
  \begin{equation}
  i\hbar\, v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi = \mathcal{E}\, \psi
  \end{equation}
  governs the evolution of the wavefunction \(\psi\) along a chosen timelike direction \(v^\mu\). It defines how phase accumulates as the system moves through spacetime, and it is primarily an equation of motion.
  \begin{equation}
  \Box \psi + \left( \frac{m c}{\hbar} \right)^2 \psi = 0
  \end{equation}
In contrast, the Klein–Gordon equation imposes a mass–shell condition, ensuring the spectral consistency of free relativistic particles. It does not serve as an evolution equation in time but rather constrains the physical states allowed in the theory (as extensively discussed in \cite{GreinerRelQM}). Consequently, It serves as a consistency condition instead, indicating that only DQET evolution law solutions that also meet the Klein-Gordon equation correspond to free particles that have physical significance.
To summarize, KG limits the permissible structure of \(\psi\), whereas DQET offers a rule for quantum phase evolution along a reference direction. Although they coexist, the two equations serve distinct conceptual purposes.
\section{Reduction to Known Quantum Equations}
\subsection{Non-Relativistic Limit: Schrödinger Equation}
We show that, upon choosing the evolution vector 
\(\displaystyle v^\mu = (1,0,0,0)\), the DQET evolution equation
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v^\mu\partial_\mu \psi(x^\nu) \;=\;\mathcal{E}\,\psi(x^\nu)
\end{equation}
reduces to the standard nonrelativistic Schrödinger equation 
\(\,i\hbar\,\partial_t\psi = \hat H\,\psi\)
\subsection*{Specializing the Evolution Vector}
Let
\begin{equation}
v^\mu = (1,\,0,\,0,\,0)
\end{equation}
so that the directional derivative becomes
\begin{equation}
D_v \;=\; v^\mu\partial_\mu 
\;=\; \underbrace{1}_{v^0}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x^0}
+ 0\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial x^i}
\;=\; \frac{\partial}{\partial t}
\end{equation}
where we identify \(x^0 = t\).
So, the DQET equation becomes
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t} = \mathcal{E}\,\psi
\end{equation}
\subsection*{Interpreting \(\mathcal{E}\) as the Hamiltonian}
In the nonrelativistic limit, the directional energy
\(\mathcal{E}=p_\mu v^\mu\) for \(v^\mu=(1,0,0,0)\) reduces to
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = p_0 
= E_{\rm total}
= H \;=\; -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\,\nabla^2 + V(\mathbf{x})
\end{equation}
where \(H\) is the usual Schrödinger Hamiltonian operator,  
\(\nabla^2 = \delta^{ij}\partial_i\partial_j\), and \(V(\mathbf{x})\) is any scalar potential
\subsection*{Emergence of the Schrödinger Equation}
Substituting \(\mathcal{E}\to \hat H\) into the time‐derivative equation, we obtain
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,\frac{\partial\psi(t,\mathbf{x})}{\partial t}
= \hat H\,\psi(t,\mathbf{x})
\end{equation}
which is precisely the time‐dependent Schrödinger equation.
\subsection*{Consistency with the Probability Current}
Under this reduction, the DQET probability current
\(\,J^\mu = \hbar\,v^\mu\,|\psi|^2\) becomes
\begin{equation}
J^0 = \hbar\,|\psi|^2,\quad
J^i = 0
\end{equation}
so that the conserved density \(\rho = J^0/\hbar = |\psi|^2\) and spatial current
\(\mathbf{j} = \frac{\hbar}{m}\,\Im(\psi^*\nabla\psi)\)
emerge in the usual way once one re‐defines
\(\mathbf{j} = \rho\,\mathbf{v}_{\rm group}\)
\section{Geometric Interpretation of Energy}
The Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET) treats energy as a geometric quantity that depends on the direction along which evolution is specified, rather than as an absolute scalar connected with a preferred time coordinate. In particular, the Lorentz-invariant scalar provides the energy observable in DQET.:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu
\end{equation}
where \( v^\mu \) is the unit timelike vector indicating the direction of evolution in spacetime, and \( p^\mu \) is the system's four-momentum.
One way to read this statement is as the four-momentum of the system projected onto the evolution vector. Like proper time, which is measured along a timelike trajectory, geometrically it corresponds to the component of momentum “along the worldline” described by \( v^\mu \). In this context, the energy observed by an observer co-moving with the evolution direction, that is, in the local rest frame described by \( v^\mu \), is represented by \( \mathcal{E} \).
This interpretation leads to several physical consequences:
In a frame where \( v^\mu = (1, 0, 0, 0) \), the projection yields \( \mathcal{E} = p^0 = E_{\text{standard}} \), recovering the usual notion of energy as the time component of momentum.
The evolution vector changes for a moving observer with velocity \( \mathbf{v} \), and \( \mathcal{E} \) becomes the energy measured in that frame, taking relativistic motion and time dilation into account.
\( \mathcal{E} \) offers a natural tool for examining systems from various observer perspectives within a coherent quantum framework because it is frame-dependent but Lorentz-invariant under simultaneous transformation of \( p^\mu \) and \( v^\mu \). In DQET, the statement \( \mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu \) generates evolution along \( v^\mu \), generalizing this idea to any directions in spacetime. It is fundamentally related to the classical Hamiltonian as a generator of motion along time.
This geometric view unifies the concepts of energy, observer motion, and quantum evolution into a single covariant framework. It also offers a path toward a re-interpretation of time itself and not as a universal parameter, but as an emergent, directional flow associated with the information content and structure of the quantum state.
\section*{Proof: Noether Current from the DQET Lagrangian}
By Noether’s theorem \cite{Noether1918}, any continuous symmetry of the action corresponds to a conserved current.
We begin with the Lagrangian density for the scalar field \(\psi : \mathbb{R}^{1,3} \to \mathbb{C}\) in the DQET framework:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}[\psi, \partial_\mu \psi] = \psi^* \left( i\hbar\,v^\mu \partial_\mu - \mathcal{E} \right) \psi
\end{equation}
where \(v^\mu\) is a fixed timelike unit four-vector (\(v^\mu v_\mu = -c^2\)) and \(\mathcal{E}\) is a constant.
Under a global \(U(1)\) phase transformation:
\begin{equation}
\psi(x) \longrightarrow e^{i\alpha} \psi(x), \quad \psi^*(x) \longrightarrow e^{-i\alpha} \psi^*(x)
\end{equation}
The Lagrangian remains invariant:
\begin{equation}
\delta\mathcal{L} = 0
\end{equation}
By Noether's theorem, the associated conserved current is:
\begin{equation}
J^\mu = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi)}\,\delta \psi
+ \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi^*)}\,\delta \psi^*
\end{equation}
For an infinitesimal transformation:
\begin{equation}
\delta\psi = i\alpha\,\psi, \qquad \delta\psi^* = -i\alpha\,\psi^*
\end{equation}
Therefore,
\begin{equation}
J^\mu = i\alpha \left[
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi)}\,\psi
- \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi^*)}\,\psi^*
\right]
\end{equation}
We compute the functional derivatives from the Lagrangian:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi)} = i\hbar\,\psi^* v^\mu,
\qquad
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_\mu \psi^*)} = 0
\end{equation}
since the Lagrangian depends linearly on \(\partial_\mu \psi\) , but not on \(\partial_\mu \psi^*\)
Thus, the current becomes:
\begin{equation}
J^\mu = i\alpha\, (i\hbar\,\psi^* v^\mu)\, \psi = \alpha\,\hbar\,v^\mu\,|\psi|^2
\end{equation}
Dropping the overall factor \(\alpha\), we conclude that the conserved Noether current is:
\begin{equation}
{
J^\mu = \hbar\,v^\mu\,|\psi|^2
}
\end{equation}
as claimed
\section*{Proof of Conservation of the Probability Current}
We define the current:
\begin{equation}
J^\mu = \hbar\, v^\mu\, |\psi|^2
\end{equation}

Then its divergence is:
\begin{equation}
\partial_\mu J^\mu = \hbar\, \partial_\mu \left( v^\mu\, |\psi|^2 \right)
= \hbar\, (\partial_\mu v^\mu)\, |\psi|^2 + \hbar\, v^\mu\, \partial_\mu |\psi|^2
\end{equation}

In flat spacetime (Minkowski) with \(v^\mu = \text{const}\), we have:
\begin{equation}
\partial_\mu v^\mu = 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
\partial_\mu J^\mu = \hbar\, v^\mu\, \partial_\mu |\psi|^2
\end{equation}

Now recall the DQET equation of motion:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\, v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi = \mathcal{E} \psi
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar} \psi
\end{equation}

We take the derivative of \(|\psi|^2 = \psi^* \psi\):
\begin{equation}
v^\mu \partial_\mu |\psi|^2 = v^\mu \partial_\mu (\psi^* \psi) = 
v^\mu \left( \psi^* \partial_\mu \psi + \psi\, \partial_\mu \psi^* \right)
\end{equation}

From the equation and its complex conjugate:
\begin{equation}
v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar} \psi
\quad \text{and} \quad
v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi^* = -\frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar} \psi^*
\end{equation}

Substitute into the expression:
\begin{equation}
v^\mu \partial_\mu |\psi|^2 = \psi^* \cdot \frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar} \psi + 
\psi \cdot \left( -\frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar} \psi^* \right) = 0
\end{equation}

Therefore:
\begin{equation}
\partial_\mu J^\mu = \hbar\, v^\mu\, \partial_\mu |\psi|^2 = 0
\end{equation}
The probability current \(J^\mu\) is conserved in flat spacetime when \(v^\mu\) is constant.
\begin{equation}{ \partial_\mu J^\mu = 0 }
\end{equation}
Similar probability currents have appeared in the context of quantum time-of-arrival theory~\cite{Muga2008}.
\section{Directional Evolution of Observables }
In the standard Heisenberg picture of quantum mechanics, the time evolution of an operator \(\hat{A}(t)\) is given by:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}}{dt} = \frac{1}{i\hbar}[\hat{A}, \hat{H}] + \left( \frac{\partial \hat{A}}{\partial t} \right)
\end{equation}
where the Hamiltonian operator is represented by \(\hat{H}\), and any explicit time dependence of the operator is taken into account by the second term.
According to the Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET), evolution occurs along a selected timelike direction \(v^\mu\) rather than with respect to coordinate time \(t\). Thus, the directional derivative along \(v^\mu\) is the natural generalization of the time derivative.:
\begin{equation}
D_v \equiv v^\mu \partial_\mu
\end{equation}
Let \(\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v\) denote the directional energy operator that generates evolution along \(v^\mu\), defined as:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v = -i\hbar v^\mu \partial_\mu
\end{equation}
Then, for an observable operator \(\hat{A}(x)\), we define its evolution along the affine parameter \(s\) (parametrizing the direction \(v^\mu\)) as:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}}{ds} = D_v \hat{A} \equiv v^\mu \partial_\mu \hat{A}
\end{equation}
Now, suppose the quantum state \(\psi(s)\) evolves as:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar \frac{d}{ds} \psi(s) = \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v \psi(s)
\end{equation}
We now move to the Heisenberg picture by defining the Heisenberg-evolved operator:
\begin{equation}
\hat{A}_H(s) = U^\dagger(s)\, \hat{A}(x)\, U(s)
\end{equation}
where the unitary evolution operator is:
\begin{equation}
U(s) = \exp\left( -\frac{i}{\hbar} \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v s \right)
\end{equation}
Differentiating \(\hat{A}_H(s)\) with respect to \(s\):
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}_H}{ds} = \frac{dU^\dagger}{ds} \hat{A} U + U^\dagger \frac{d\hat{A}}{ds} U + U^\dagger \hat{A} \frac{dU}{ds}
\end{equation}
Using:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dU}{ds} = -\frac{i}{\hbar} \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v U, \quad \frac{dU^\dagger}{ds} = \frac{i}{\hbar} U^\dagger \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v
\end{equation}
we find:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}_H}{ds} = \frac{i}{\hbar} U^\dagger \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v \hat{A} U + U^\dagger (v^\mu \partial_\mu \hat{A}) U - \frac{i}{\hbar} U^\dagger \hat{A} \hat{\mathcal{E}}_v U
\end{equation}
Simplifying:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}_H}{ds} = U^\dagger \left( \frac{1}{i\hbar} [\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v, \hat{A}] + v^\mu \partial_\mu \hat{A} \right) U
\end{equation}
So, in the Heisenberg picture, the directional evolution equation for observables becomes:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{A}_H}{ds} = \frac{1}{i\hbar} [\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v, \hat{A}_H] + v^\mu \frac{\partial \hat{A}_H}{\partial x^\mu}
\end{equation}
This formulation mirrors the Heisenberg picture of quantum mechanics, where observables evolve via commutators with the Hamiltonian \cite{Sakurai2017}.
This equation shows that the evolution of observables is generated by the directional energy operator\(\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v\), and it reduces to the conventional Heisenberg equation when \(v^\mu = (1, 0, 0, 0)\).
Operators that commute with \(\hat{\mathcal{E}}_v\) are conserved along the direction \(v^\mu\).
This formulation provides a covariant generalization of quantum dynamics in the Heisenberg picture.
It unifies the algebraic and geometric perspectives by encoding both commutator evolution and explicit spacetime dependence in a single covariant equation.
\section{Proper Time and Intrinsic Evolution}
In relativistic physics, proper time \( \tau \) plays a fundamental role as the invariant time experienced along a particle's worldline. Unlike coordinate time, which depends on the observer's frame, proper time provides an intrinsic parameterization of motion. The DQET framework naturally accommodates evolution with respect to proper time, offering a reformulation of quantum dynamics that aligns more closely with the relativistic structure of spacetime.
(see \cite{Rindler2006} for a comprehensive discussion of proper time and four-velocity in special relativity)
Consider a particle following a worldline \( x^\mu(\tau) \), with four-velocity \( u^\mu = dx^\mu/d\tau \), satisfying \( u^\mu u_\mu = c^2 \). In DQET, we may identify the evolution direction \( v^\mu \) with the normalized four-velocity \( u^\mu / c \), so that evolution proceeds along the proper time of the system itself:
\begin{equation}
v^\mu = \frac{dx^\mu}{cd\tau}
\end{equation}
Substituting this into the DQET equation yields:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar \frac{d}{d\tau} \psi(\tau) = \mathcal{E} \psi(\tau)
\end{equation}
where the directional derivative becomes a total derivative along the worldline, and \( \mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu \) retains its geometric interpretation as the projection of four-momentum onto the particle's path.
This formulation implies that quantum systems can be described as evolving with respect to their own intrinsic clocks, rather than relying on an external coordinate time. The result aligns with classical relativistic mechanics, where proper time governs the equations of motion for massive particles.
The implications are far-reaching:
DQET provides a consistent description of single-particle quantum evolution along curved trajectories in spacetime, including in accelerated frames or gravitational fields (with appropriate generalization).
The theory allows for a unified treatment of ensembles of particles, each evolving along their respective proper times, offering a natural language for relativistic statistical mechanics and field theory.
This intrinsic evolution opens potential pathways toward resolving long-standing conceptual tensions between the “timeless” nature of some quantum gravity proposals and the emergence of time in classical relativity.
By grounding evolution in proper time, DQET offers a formulation of quantum mechanics that is not only covariant, but also intrinsic to the system, extending the reach of quantum theory into the regime of relativistic dynamics and observer-dependent structures.
\section{Application: Quantum Twin Paradox and Directional Evolution}
In standard quantum mechanics the evolution parameter is a universal time \(t\), shared by all systems in a given inertial frame.  Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET) replaces this single time with an \emph{observer‐dependent} affine parameter
\begin{equation}
s \;=\; v_\mu x^\mu
\end{equation}
and associates to each evolution direction \(v^\mu\) a corresponding \emph{directional energy}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} \;=\; p_\mu\,v^\mu
\end{equation}
which generates translations in \(s\).  Thus two quantum systems following different timelike worldlines \(\gamma_1\), \(\gamma_2\) will in general accumulate different phases, even if they share the same four‐momentum magnitude.
To illustrate this, consider the familiar \emph{twin paradox} setup adapted to DQET:
System~1 (the “stationary twin”) moves with four‐velocity 
\(v_1^\mu = (1,0,0,0)\), so that \(s_1 = t\) is the laboratory time.
System~2 (the “traveling twin”) moves with four‐velocity 
\(\displaystyle v_2^\mu = \gamma\,(1, \beta,0,0)\), \(\beta = v/c\)
and thus experiences an internal time
\(\displaystyle s_2 = \gamma\,(t - \beta x)\)
Along each worldline, the wavefunction obeys
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v_i^\mu\partial_\mu \psi_i = \mathcal{E}_i\,\psi_i,
\qquad i=1,2
\end{equation}
with \(\mathcal{E}_i = p_\mu v_i^\mu\).  Between two reunion events at the same spacetime point, the total accumulated phase for system \(i\) is
\begin{equation}
\phi_i \;=\;\frac{\mathcal{E}_i\,\Delta s_i}{\hbar}
\end{equation}
where \(\Delta s_i\) is the difference of the affine parameters along \(\gamma_i\).  The resulting phase difference
\(\Delta\phi=\phi_1-\phi_2\) provides a direct, operational measure of the observer–dependence of quantum evolution in DQET.
\subsection{Covariant Construction of Two Worldlines}
We model two quantum “twins” as point–like systems whose histories in Minkowski spacetime are given by timelike integral curves
\begin{equation}
\gamma_i : s_i \;\mapsto\; x_i^\mu(s_i)
\,,\qquad i=1,2,
\end{equation}
satisfying the differential equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{dx_i^\mu}{ds_i} \;=\; v_i^\mu,
\end{equation}
with constant, normalized four–velocities
\begin{equation}
v_i^\mu v_{i\mu} = -c^2\,.
\end{equation}
By integration, and choosing a common initial event \(x_i^\mu(0)=x_0^\mu\), one obtains the affine parametrizations
\begin{equation}
x_i^\mu(s_i) \;=\; x_0^\mu \;+\; v_i^\mu\,s_i,
\qquad
s_i \;=\;\frac{1}{c^2}\,v_{i\mu}\bigl[x_i^\mu(s_i)-x_0^\mu\bigr].
\end{equation}
Equivalently, the invariant “internal time” along each worldline is
\begin{equation}
s_i = \frac{1}{c^2}\,v_{i\mu}\,x_i^\mu\,,
\end{equation}
so that \(dx_i^\mu\,v_{i\mu} = c^2\,ds_i\).  In particular:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
v_1^\mu &= (c,0,0,0)\,, 
&& x_1^\mu(s_1) = x_0^\mu + (c\,s_1,0,0,0)\,,\\
v_2^\mu &= \gamma\,(c,\;v,\;0,0)\,, 
&& x_2^\mu(s_2) = x_0^\mu + \bigl(\gamma c\,s_2,\;\gamma v\,s_2,0,0\bigr)\,,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
with \(\gamma=1/\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}\).  The reunion event \(X^\mu\) is defined by
\(\gamma_1(s_1^R)=\gamma_2(s_2^R)=X^\mu\), yielding two proper–time intervals
\(\Delta s_i = s_i^R - s_i^0\).  These will underlie the subsequent computation of relative directional phases. 
\subsection{Directional Phase Accumulation}
Along each worldline \(\gamma_i: s_i\mapsto x_i^\mu(s_i)\), the solution of the DQET evolution equation
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v_i^\mu\partial_\mu\psi_i(x_i(s_i)) \;=\;\mathcal{E}_i\,\psi_i(x_i(s_i))
\end{equation}
acquires a phase factor
\begin{equation}
\psi_i(s_i) \;=\; \exp\!\biggl(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\!\int_{s_i^0}^{s_i}\!\mathcal{E}_i\,ds_i'\biggr)\,\psi_i(s_i^0).
\end{equation}
Since \(\mathcal{E}_i=p_\mu v_i^\mu\) is constant along \(\gamma_i\), the integral reduces to
\begin{equation}
\phi_i \;=\;\frac{1}{\hbar}\,\mathcal{E}_i\,(s_i^R - s_i^0)
\;=\;\frac{\mathcal{E}_i\,\Delta s_i}{\hbar},
\end{equation}
where \(\Delta s_i=s_i^R-s_i^0\) is the total change in affine parameter between the initial event \(s_i^0\) and the reunion event \(s_i^R\).  
Hence the relative phase accumulated by the two twins upon reunion is
\begin{equation}
\Delta\phi = \phi_1 - \phi_2
= \frac{1}{\hbar}\bigl(\mathcal{E}_1\,\Delta s_1 - \mathcal{E}_2\,\Delta s_2\bigr).
\end{equation}
In the special case where both reunions occur at the same spacetime point and proper–time separation \(\Delta\tau\), one may write
\begin{equation}
\Delta\phi
= \frac{(\mathcal{E}_1 - \mathcal{E}_2)\,\Delta\tau}{\hbar},
\end{equation}
making explicit the dependence on the observer–dependent directional energies \(\mathcal{E}_i\).  
\subsection{Physical Interpretation}
In the framework of DQET, the evolution of a quantum system is governed not by an external universal time but by an internal, frame–dependent parameter \(s = v_\mu x^\mu\), which reflects the system’s motion through spacetime. The directional energy \(\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu\) associated with this motion determines the rate at which the system accumulates phase along its worldline.
Under this interpretation, each quantum system can be thought of as carrying a “phase clock” that ticks at frequency \(\mathcal{E}/\hbar\), where the notion of time is replaced by the directional parameter \(s\),(see also \cite{Mermin2005} for an intuitive discussion of proper time and the twin paradox in special relativity)
As the system moves through spacetime along a path defined by its velocity \(v^\mu\), the wavefunction evolves as
\begin{equation}
\psi(s) = e^{-i \mathcal{E} s / \hbar} \, \psi(0)
\end{equation}
analogous to a free particle in conventional quantum mechanics, but now governed by a generalized, observer–dependent evolution.
The twin paradox, in this context, is not interpreted in terms of time dilation or acceleration, but rather as a differential accumulation of quantum phase due to differing values of \(\mathcal{E}\) and \(s\) along each trajectory. The system with the larger \(\mathcal{E} \cdot \Delta s\) will accumulate more phase, and this difference is directly observable through interference or phase–sensitive measurements.
This reformulation provides a covariant, phase–centric resolution to the paradox: quantum systems moving on different worldlines evolve differently—not because of time–keeping discrepancies, but because their intrinsic directional evolution carries distinct energetic and geometric imprints.
Hence, DQET reframes the relativistic twin effect as a fundamentally quantum phase phenomenon, deeply tied to the observer’s state of motion.
