\subsection{Relativistic Doppler Shift from Directional Energy}
We illustrate how the DQET definition of directional energy
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu
\end{equation}
naturally reproduces the relativistic Doppler effect observed when a photon is detected by an observer in motion relative to the source.
Consider a massless particle (e.g., a photon) with four–momentum
\begin{equation}
p^\mu = (\hbar \omega,\, \hbar \vec{k})
\end{equation}
Satisfying the dispersion relation for light:
\begin{equation}
|\vec{k}| = \frac{\omega}{c},
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
p^\mu = \hbar \omega\, \left(1,\, \frac{\hat{\mathbf{k}}}{c}\right)
\end{equation}
where \(\hat{\mathbf{k}}\) is a unit vector in the direction of propagation.
Now consider an observer moving with constant velocity \(v\) in the direction of the photon (say, along the \(x\)-axis). The observer’s four–velocity is given by:
\begin{equation}
v^\mu = \gamma\, (c,\, v,\, 0,\, 0),
\quad \text{with} \quad
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - \beta^2}},\; \beta = \frac{v}{c}
\end{equation}
We now compute the directional energy \(\mathcal{E}\) measured by this observer.
\subsubsection*{Computation of \(\mathcal{E}\)}
Assume the photon propagates in the \(+x\) direction, so its 4–momentum becomes:
\begin{equation}
p^\mu = \left(\hbar \omega,\, \frac{\hbar \omega}{c},\, 0,\, 0\right)
\end{equation}
Contracting with the observer’s 4–velocity:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{E}
&= p_\mu\,v^\mu \\
&= \eta_{\mu\nu}\,p^\mu\,v^\nu \\
&= -\hbar\,\omega\;\gamma\,c
  \;+\;\frac{\hbar\,\omega}{c}\;\gamma\,v \\
&= \hbar\,\omega\;\gamma\,(-c + \beta\,c) \\
&= -\,\hbar\,\omega\;\gamma\,c\,(1 - \beta)\,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Taking absolute value (since energy is positive for forward propagation):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = \hbar \omega\, \gamma c (1 - \beta)
\end{equation}
Hence, the frequency observed by the moving observer is:
\begin{equation}
\omega' = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}
= \omega\, \gamma (1 - \beta)
\end{equation}
This is precisely the relativistic Doppler shift formula for a receding light source.
\subsubsection*{Blueshift Case}
If the photon is propagating in the \( -x \) direction (i.e., the source is approaching), the spatial momentum reverses:
\begin{equation}
p^\mu = \left(\hbar \omega,\, -\frac{\hbar \omega}{c},\, 0,\, 0\right)
\end{equation}
and we obtain:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu = \hbar \omega\, \gamma c (1 + \beta)
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
\omega' = \omega\, \gamma (1 + \beta)
\end{equation}
The standard relativistic blueshift corresponds to this.
Therefore, the observer-dependent directional energy \(\mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu\) directly leads to the Doppler effect in DQET. This proves the DQET formalism's ability to reproduce well-known relativistic phenomena from a unified quantum–geometric viewpoint and confirms its physical consistency.
\section{Time–Energy Uncertainty Relation in DQET}
The time–energy uncertainty relation has remained conceptually ambiguous due to the lack of a time operator in standard quantum mechanics~\cite{Hilgevoord1996}\cite{Ballentine1998}.
In the Directional Quantum Evolution Theory (DQET), the wavefunction evolves along an affine parameter \(s\) defined by:
\begin{equation}
s = \int v_\mu\, dx^\mu
\end{equation}
which plays the role of intrinsic (proper-like) time for a given evolution direction \(v^\mu\). The evolution equation reads:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\, \frac{d}{ds}\psi(s) = \mathcal{E}\, \psi(s)
\end{equation}
where \( \mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu \) is the directional energy measured along \(v^\mu\).
\subsection*{Fourier Duality}
We consider normalized wavefunctions \( \psi(s) \in L^2(\mathbb{R}) \), and define their Fourier transform with respect to \( \mathcal{E} \) as:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\psi}(\mathcal{E}) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\hbar}} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \psi(s)\, e^{i\mathcal{E}s/\hbar} ds
\end{equation}
with inverse:
\begin{equation}
\psi(s) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\hbar}} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \tilde{\psi}(\mathcal{E})\, e^{-i\mathcal{E}s/\hbar} d\mathcal{E}
\end{equation}
This expresses the state as a superposition of eigenstates of the directional energy \( \mathcal{E} \), each oscillating along the affine time \( s \).
\subsection*{Uncertainty Relation}
We define the statistical variances:
\begin{equation}
(\Delta s)^2 = \langle s^2 \rangle - \langle s \rangle^2, \quad
(\Delta \mathcal{E})^2 = \langle \mathcal{E}^2 \rangle - \langle \mathcal{E} \rangle^2
\end{equation}
with:
\begin{equation}
\langle s \rangle = \int s\, |\psi(s)|^2 ds, \quad
\langle \mathcal{E} \rangle = \int \mathcal{E}\, |\tilde{\psi}(\mathcal{E})|^2 d\mathcal{E}
\end{equation}
By the Fourier uncertainty principle for conjugate variables \( (s, \mathcal{E}) \), we obtain:
\begin{equation}
\Delta s \cdot \Delta \mathcal{E} \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\end{equation}
This inequality follows purely from the Fourier duality between the affine time \(s\) and the directional energy, exactly as the position–momentum uncertainty relation arises from the duality between \(x\) and \(p\).

\subsection*{Interpretation in DQET}
This inequality follows purely from the Fourier duality between the affine time \(s\) and the directional energy \( \mathcal{E} \), exactly as the position–momentum uncertainty relation arises from the duality between \(x\) and \(p\). However, the interpretation is fundamentally different:
In DQET, \(s\) is the internal time experienced by the system, determined by its direction of evolution \(v^\mu\).
\( \mathcal{E} = p_\mu v^\mu \) is the generator of translations along \(s\), analogous to how \(p\) generates translations in \(x\).
Thus, the uncertainty relation
\begin{equation}
{
\Delta \mathcal{E} \cdot \Delta s \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
}
\end{equation}
It represents a true time–energy uncertainty relation, grounded in the same mathematical framework that supports canonical uncertainty pairs.
\section{Resolution of Pauli’s Objection in DQET}
\textit{Pauli’s theorem} states that a self‑adjoint time operator \(\hat{T}\) satisfying
\([\hat{T},\hat{H}]=i\hbar\) cannot exist if the Hamiltonian \(\hat{H}\) is bounded from below.
In DQET, “time’’ is not introduced as an operator but arises as the affine parameter
\begin{equation}
s \;=\;\int v_\mu\,dx^\mu
\end{equation}
associated with a chosen timelike four‑vector \(v^\mu\).The generator of translations in \(s\) is the directional energy
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} \;=\; p_\mu v^\mu
\end{equation}
which governs the evolution via
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,\frac{d}{ds}\,\psi(s) \;=\; \mathcal{E}\,\psi(s)
\end{equation}
Because \(s\) remains a parameter intrinsic to the wavefunction—not an operator on the Hilbert space—the spectral argument underlying Pauli’s objection does \emph{not} apply. Instead, \((s,\mathcal{E})\) form a Fourier‑conjugate pair, obeying the uncertainty relation
\begin{equation}
\Delta s\,\Delta\mathcal{E}\;\ge\;\frac{\hbar}{2}\,
\end{equation}
Thus, DQET consistently incorporates a rigorous time–energy uncertainty relation without evoking a problematic time operator.
\subsection{Reconciliation of the Dual Roles of Directional Energy \(\mathcal{E}\)}
The directional energy \(\mathcal{E}\) appears in DQET both as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E} \;=\; p_\mu \, v^\mu
\end{equation}
and as the constant parameter in the Lagrangian
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} \;=\; \psi^*\bigl(i\hbar\,v^\mu\partial_\mu - \mathcal{E}\bigr)\psi
\end{equation}
These two facets are reconciled as follows:
\textbf{\(\mathcal{E}\) as Eigenvalue of Proper–Time Translations.}  
In the variational derivation, \(\mathcal{E}\) enters as the Lagrange multiplier (or Hamiltonian eigenvalue) enforcing first–order evolution in the internal parameter \(\tau\).  Varying \(\psi^*\) yields
    \begin{equation}
    i\hbar\,v^\mu\partial_\mu\psi = \mathcal{E}\,\psi
    \end{equation}
so \(\mathcal{E}\) is interpreted as the spectral value associated with translations in \(\tau\), not as a dynamical field.
\textbf{\(p_\mu\) as Operator vs.\ Classical 4–Velocity \(v^\mu\)}
Here \(p_\mu\) is promoted to the usual quantum operator \(-\,i\hbar\,\partial_\mu\), while \(v^\mu\) labels the observer’s fixed inertial frame.  Acting on an on–shell eigenstate \(\psi\), one finds
    \begin{equation}
    \hat{p}_\mu\,v^\mu\,\psi = \mathcal{E}\,\psi
    \end{equation}
    so \(\mathcal{E}\) also coincides with the expectation (or eigenvalue) of the operator \(p_\mu v^\mu\).
\section*{Proof: Causal Consistency of the Internal Parameter}
We work in a spacetime with metric signature \((-+++)\).  Let \(v^\mu\) be a fixed timelike unit vector,
\begin{equation}
v^\mu v_\mu = -1
\end{equation}
and define the scalar parameter
\begin{equation}
s(x) \;=\; v_\mu x^\mu
\end{equation}
We show that (i) each hypersurface \(s=\mathrm{const}\) is spacelike, and (ii) evolution in \(s\) cannot violate causality.
\subsection*{ Normal to the Hypersurface}
The hypersurface 
\(\Sigma_s\colon s(x)=s_0\) has normal covector
\begin{equation}
n_\mu \;=\; \partial_\mu s = v_\mu
\end{equation}
Its norm is
\begin{equation}
n^\mu n_\mu \;=\; v^\mu v_\mu = -1 < 0
\end{equation}
so \(n^\mu\) is timelike.  A hypersurface whose normal is timelike is itself spacelike. 
\begin{equation}
{\Sigma_s\;\text{is spacelike for every fixed }s}
\end{equation}
\subsection*{No Causal (Backward) Evolution}
Consider any future‐directed causal (timelike or null) curve \(x^\mu(\lambda)\).  Its tangent \(u^\mu = dx^\mu/d\lambda\) satisfies
\begin{equation}
u^\mu u_\mu \le 0,
\quad
u^0 > 0
\end{equation}
Along this curve,
\begin{equation}
\frac{d s}{d\lambda}
= v_\mu \frac{dx^\mu}{d\lambda}
= v_\mu u^\mu
\end{equation}
Since both \(v^\mu\) and \(u^\mu\) are future‐directed timelike or null, their inner product is strictly positive:
\begin{equation}
v_\mu\,u^\mu < 0 \times 0 \;=\; 0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad v_\mu u^\mu > 0
\end{equation}
Hence
\begin{equation}
\frac{d s}{d\lambda} > 0
\end{equation}
so \(s\) \emph{monotonically increases} along every future‐directed causal curve.  One cannot go “backwards” in \(s\) without reversing the causal direction.
1. Each level set \(s=\mathrm{const}\) is a spacelike hypersurface.  
2. \(s\) increases strictly along all future‐directed causal curves.
Therefore, using \(s\) as the evolution parameter \emph{preserves causality} and does not permit any backward‐in‐time propagation.
\section{Proof: Conservation of Directional Energy $\mathcal{E}=p_\mu v^\mu$}
We wish to show that along the DQET evolution flow,
\begin{equation}
v^\mu \partial_\mu \mathcal{E} \;=\; 0
\qquad\text{when}\quad v^\mu=\text{const}
\end{equation}
\subsection*{Definition of Directional Energy}
By hypothesis,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}(x) \;=\; p_\mu(x)\,v^\mu
\end{equation}
where \(p_\mu(x)\) is the local four‐momentum density associated with the wavefunction \(\psi\), and \(v^\mu\) is a fixed timelike unit vector.
\subsection*{DQET Equation of Motion}
The DQET evolution equation reads
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v^\mu\partial_\mu\psi = \mathcal{E}\,\psi
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
v^\mu\partial_\mu\psi = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar}\,\psi
\end{equation}
Taking the gradient with respect to \(x^\nu\) gives
\begin{equation}
v^\mu\partial_\mu\bigl(\partial_\nu\psi\bigr)
=\partial_\nu\!\Bigl(\tfrac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar}\psi\Bigr)
=\frac{1}{i\hbar}\Bigl[(\partial_\nu\mathcal{E})\,\psi + \mathcal{E}\,\partial_\nu\psi\Bigr]
\end{equation}
\subsection*{Definition of \(p_\mu\) and Its Evolution}
Recall the de Broglie identification
\begin{equation}
p_\mu = \hbar\,\Im\bigl[\psi^*\partial_\mu\psi\bigr]
\end{equation}
so that
\begin{equation}
\partial_\nu p_\mu
= \hbar\,\Im\Bigl[\partial_\nu\psi^*\;\partial_\mu\psi
+\psi^*\;\partial_\nu\partial_\mu\psi\Bigr]
\end{equation}
Contracting with \(v^\mu\), and using the above evolution for \(\partial_\nu\partial_\mu\psi\), one finds
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
v^\mu\,\partial_\nu p_\mu
&= \hbar\,\Im\Bigl[
      \partial_\nu\psi^*\,v^\mu\partial_\mu\psi
    + \psi^*\,v^\mu\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\psi
  \Bigr] \\
&= \hbar\,\Im\Bigl[
      \partial_\nu\psi^*\,\frac{\mathcal{E}}{i\hbar}\,\psi
    + \psi^*\,\frac{1}{i\hbar}\bigl(
        \partial_\nu\mathcal{E}\,\psi
      + \mathcal{E}\,\partial_\nu\psi
      \bigr)
  \Bigr]
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The imaginary parts cancel except for the term containing \(\partial_\nu\mathcal{E}\), yielding
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
v^\mu\,\partial_\nu p_\mu
&= -\,\partial_\nu \mathcal{E}
   \;\underbrace{\Im\bigl[\psi^* \psi\bigr]}_{=0}
   \;+\;\Im\!\Bigl[\psi^*\,\frac{\partial_\nu \mathcal{E}}{i}\,\psi\Bigr]
\\
&= \Im\!\Bigl[\psi^*\,\frac{\partial_\nu \mathcal{E}}{i}\,\psi\Bigr]
\\
&= v_\nu\,\bigl(v^\alpha \,\partial_\alpha \mathcal{E}\bigr)\,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where we used \(v_\nu\,v^\nu=-c^2\) and \(\Im[\psi^*\psi]=0\)
\subsection{Conservation Law}
Now compute:
\begin{equation}
v^\nu\partial_\nu\mathcal{E}
= v^\nu\,v^\mu\,\partial_\nu p_\mu
= v^\nu\,v_\nu\,\frac{\partial_\alpha\mathcal{E}\,v^\alpha}{ }
= -c^2\,v^\alpha\partial_\alpha\mathcal{E}
\end{equation}
Rearranging gives
\begin{equation}
\bigl(1 + \tfrac{c^2}{ }\bigr)\,v^\alpha\partial_\alpha\mathcal{E} = 0
\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
v^\alpha\partial_\alpha\mathcal{E} = 0
\end{equation}
Thus the directional energy \(\mathcal{E}=p_\mu v^\mu\) is conserved along the flow of \(v^\mu\) whenever \(v^\mu\) is constant.  
\section{Toward General Relativity}
While DQET is formulated in the context of flat Minkowski spacetime, its structure invites a natural extension to curved spacetimes as described by general relativity. In such settings, spacetime is equipped with a metric \( g_{\mu\nu}(x) \) and the notion of direction and evolution must be treated geometrically. We now sketch the path toward generalizing DQET to such backgrounds.
In curved spacetime, the directional derivative \( D_v = v^\mu \partial_\mu \) generalizes to a covariant form \( D_v = v^\mu \nabla_\mu \), consistent with the geometric structure of general relativity \cite{Misner1973}.
where \( \nabla_\mu \) is the covariant derivative compatible with the metric. The fundamental evolution equation becomes:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar v^\mu \nabla_\mu \psi(x) = \mathcal{E}(x) \psi(x)
\end{equation}
where both \( v^\mu \) and \( \mathcal{E}(x) = p_\mu(x) v^\mu(x) \) are local quantities defined with respect to the curved geometry.
The construction follows the standard treatment of motion and conserved quantities in curved spacetime \cite{Schutz2009}.
In this formulation, evolution proceeds along a timelike curve \( x^\mu(\tau) \) with tangent vector \( v^\mu = dx^\mu/d\tau \), and the proper-time \( \tau \) retains its role as an invariant parameter. The wavefunction evolves locally under the influence of both the geometry and the observer's trajectory.
Importantly, the conservation law also generalizes. A conserved current in curved spacetime takes the form:
\begin{equation}
J^\mu = \hbar v^\mu |\psi(x)|^2, \qquad \nabla_\mu J^\mu = 0
\end{equation}
This continuity equation ensures probability conservation across curved spacetime volumes, respecting the underlying geometric structure.
This opens the possibility of describing quantum systems in strong gravitational fields, such as near black holes, cosmological backgrounds, or accelerating frames, with evolution governed not by an external coordinate time, but by intrinsic geometry-aligned directions. For example:
Near a Schwarzschild black hole, DQET can describe how quantum information evolves along infalling geodesics.
In an expanding universe, the evolution vector \( v^\mu \) can align with comoving observers, allowing a consistent description of quantum states over cosmological scales.
In rotating or shearing spacetimes, different local observers may experience different phase accumulation, offering a potential handle on quantum effects of spacetime anisotropy.
Thus, DQET may serve as a stepping stone toward a fully covariant, observer-dependent formulation of quantum mechanics in curved spacetime — bridging the conceptual gap between general relativity and quantum theory from the bottom up.
\section{Explicit Plane Wave Solutions of the DQET Equation}
We conclude by presenting the explicit solution of the DQET evolution equation:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi(x) = \mathcal{E}\,\psi(x)
\end{equation}
where \(v^\mu\) is a fixed timelike four–vector and \(\mathcal{E}\) is a real constant.
We seek solutions of the form
\begin{equation}
\psi(x) = A\,e^{-i k_\mu x^\mu}
\end{equation}
with constant amplitude \(A\) and four–wavevector \(k^\mu\). Substituting into the equation gives:
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\,v^\mu \partial_\mu \psi(x) = \hbar\,v^\mu k_\mu\,\psi(x)
\end{equation}
Therefore, the plane wave is a solution if and only if
\begin{equation}
{v^\mu k_\mu = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}}
\end{equation}
Thus, any wavevector \(k^\mu\) lying on the hyperplane defined by this constraint yields a valid solution. The general solution is then a superposition over all such wavevectors:
\begin{equation}
\psi(x) = \int d^4k\,\tilde\psi(k)\,e^{-i k_\mu x^\mu}
\quad \text{subject to} \quad v^\mu k_\mu = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}
\end{equation}
This defines a 3-dimensional constraint surface in momentum space, analogous to an energy shell. The phase fronts of the solution are orthogonal to \(v^\mu\), confirming that the natural parameter of propagation is \(s = v_\mu x^\mu\).
Note: Since the equation does not constrain \(k^\mu k_\mu\), additional physical input (e.g., a mass shell condition) may be imposed to restrict the solution to physical wave packets if desired.
\subsection*{Construction of Localized Wavepackets}
While plane wave solutions of the form
\begin{equation}
\psi_k(x) = A_k\, e^{-i k_\mu x^\mu}
\quad \text{with} \quad v^\mu k_\mu = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}
\end{equation}
solve the DQET equation, they are spatially delocalized and not normalizable. To construct physically realistic states, we form wavepackets as superpositions of such solutions:
\begin{equation}
\psi(x) = \int d^3k\, \tilde{\psi}(k)\, e^{-i k_\mu x^\mu}
\end{equation}
with the constraint
\begin{equation}
v^\mu k_\mu = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}
\end{equation}
imposed on the domain of integration.
This constraint defines a three-dimensional hyperplane in momentum space. The function \(\tilde{\psi}(k)\) may be chosen as a smooth, localized distribution (e.g., a Gaussian centered at some \(k_0^\mu\)) supported entirely on the hyperplane. In this way, the wavepacket is built from admissible modes that evolve consistently with the directional equation.
Such wavepackets are localized in spacetime and exhibit standard quantum behavior such as group velocity and dispersion (depending on the shape of \(\tilde{\psi}(k)\)). The condition
\begin{equation}
v^\mu k_\mu = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{\hbar}
\end{equation}
remains satisfied for each component, ensuring the full wavepacket satisfies the DQET evolution law.
