\begin{remark}
 If $\{v_i,v_j\}$ is an edge and $v_i<v_j$, then $[v_i,[v_i,\dots,[v_i,v_j]\dots]]$, with $k-1$ times $v_i$, is a basis element lying in $V_k$.
\end{remark}

Let $m=|V|$ be the number of vertices of $G$. 
Each basis element $\omega$ has a multidegree $d(v)$, the $m$-tuple of multiplicities of each vertex in the underlying word.
In the example above, $d([[v_1,v_2],v_2])=(1,2,0)$ and $d([[v_1, [v_1, v_3]], v_2])=(2,1,1)$.
Given two basis elements $\omega_i$ and $\omega_j$, their bracket is
\[ [\omega_i,\omega_j]=\sum_k c_k \omega_k, \]
where $c_k\ne 0$ only if 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:multidegree}
	d(\omega_k)= d(\omega_i)+d(\omega_j).
\end{equation}



