In both cases, their second nil-cohomology vanishes ($H^2_{2-\operatorname{nil}}(\mu,\mu)=0$).
Since the rigidity of the first one has already been observed in \cite[Proposition 2.4]{A}, we focus on the second one. 

Let's consider the graph that is a square. That is, \(G=(V,E)\), with \(V=\{v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4\}\) and \(E=\{\{v_1,v_2\},\{v_2,v_3\},\{v_3,v_4\},\{v_4,v_1\}\}\). 
Let $\g$ be the 2-step nilpotent Lie algebra associated to $G$ and recall that (see \eqref{eqn:H2-nil})
\[ H^2_{2-\nil}(\mu,\mu)=\frac{\ker(\eta_2)}{\Im(\delta^1)}. \]
If \(\sigma\in\ker(\eta_2)\), then $\sigma([v_i,v_j],v_k)+[\sigma(v_i,v_j),v_k]=0$. This implies that \(\sigma(v_1,v_3),\sigma(v_2,v_4)\in Z(\g)\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
	\sigma(v_1,v_3)&=a[v_1,v_2]+b[v_2,v_3]+c[v_3,v_4]+d[v_4,v_1]\\
	\sigma(v_2,v_4)&=e[v_1,v_2]+f[v_2,v_3]+g[v_3,v_4]+h[v_4,v_1],
\end{aligned}
\]
for some constants $a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h\in\C$.
The linear function $f:\g\rightarrow \g$ defined by
\[
\begin{aligned}
	f(v_1)&=bv_2-cv_4\\
	f(v_2)&=-hv_1+gv_3\\
	f(v_3)&=av_2-dv_4\\
	f(v_4)&=-ev_1+fv_3\\
	f([v_1,v_2])&=[f(v_1),v_2]+[v_1,f(v_2)]-\sigma(v_1,v_2)\\
	f([v_2,v_3])&=[f(v_2),v_3]+[v_2,f(v_3)]-\sigma(v_2,v_3)\\
	f([v_3,v_4])&=[f(v_3),v_4]+[v_3,f(v_4)]-\sigma(v_3,v_4)\\
	f([v_4,v_1])&=[f(v_4),v_1]+[v_4,f(v_1)]-\sigma(v_4,v_1)\\
\end{aligned}
\]
satisfies \(\delta^1(f)=\sigma\).
Therefore, $H^2_{2-\nil}(\mu,\mu)=0$.

\bigskip 

