\section{\bf Introduction}
  Let $G$ be a connected (real) Lie group and ${\rm Sub}_G$ be the space of (closed) subgroups of $G$ equipped with the Chabauty topology introduced by Claude Chabauty in \cite{CC}. The Chabauty topology on Sub$_G$ is generated by the subsets $\{ H \in {\rm Sub}_G \mid H\cap K = \emptyset \}$, $\{ H\in {\rm Sub}_G \mid H \cap U \not = \emptyset \}$, 
where $K \subset G$ is compact and $U \subset G$ is open.  With respect to the
Chabauty topology, $\text{Sub}_G$ is a compact metrizable space (cf. Lemma E.1.1 of \cite{BaP}).  Moreover, a sequence $H_n\in\text{Sub}_G$ converges
to $H\in \text{Sub}_G$ if and only if for any $h\in H$ there is a sequence $h_n\in H_n$ such that $h_n$ converges to $h$ and for
any sequence $x_{k_n}\in H_{k_n}$, with $k_{n+1}>k_n$ and $x_{k_n}\to x$, we have $x\in H$ (cf. Lemma E.1.2 of \cite{BaP}). 

The group $G$ acts on Sub$_G$ by conjugation: $(g, H) \mapsto gHg^{-1}$.  
An invariant random subgroup (hereafter IRS) is a probability measure $\mu$ on Sub$_G$ that is invariant under the conjugation action of $G$ on Sub$_G$.  We say that a Borel subset $\cO$ of Sub$_G$ supports an IRS $\mu$ if $\mu ({\cO}) =1$:  a Borel subset ${\cO}$ 
supporting an IRS $\mu$ need not be the support of $\mu$ but 
$\overline {\cO}$ contains the support of $\mu$. Dirac measure supported on a closed normal subgroup of a connected Lie group is an example of an IRS. Suppose $\Gamma$ is a lattice inside the connected Lie group $G$, i.e., $\Gamma$ is a discrete subgroup of $G$ such that $G/\Gamma$ carries a probability measure $\mu$ that is invariant under the natural action of $G$ on $G/\Gamma$. Then the map from $G$ to $\text{Sub}_G$ sending $g$ to $g\Gamma g^{-1}$ factors through $G/\Gamma$, and the push forward of $\mu$ by this map becomes a conjugation-invariant probability measure supported on conjugacy classes of $\Gamma$. So, invariant random subgroups can be thought of as a generalization of both normal subgroups and lattices. 


Invariant random subgroups arise naturally as stabilizers of measure preserving actions of $G$ on a probability space $(X,\mu)$. By a result of Varadarajan (\cite{Va1}), the stabilizer of almost every point of this action is closed in $G$. Moreover, the stabilizer map from $X$ to $\text{Sub}_G$ given by $x\to G_x$ is measurable. Then, one can push the measure $\mu$ forward to an IRS of $G$. In fact, every IRS can be obtained this way (see \cite{A7s}, \cite{AGV} for more details). 

In recent times, IRS has attracted many studies (see for examples \cite{A7s} and \cite{AGV}). In this article, we are interested in the existence of invariant random subgroups supported on certain orbits of the conjugation action of $G$ on $\text{Sub}_G$.  We start with the orbit of a semisimple Levi subgroup.  Let $G$ be a connected Lie group with a closed semisimple Levi subgroup $L$; it may be noted that semisimple Levi subgroups of connected Lie groups 
need not be closed. But, Levi subgroups of linear groups are closed.  Let $L_G$ be the set of all semisimple Levi subgroups of $G$.  Then $L_G = \{xLx^{-1} \mid x \in N \}$ 
where $N$ is the nilradical of $G$ (cf. Theorem 3.18.3 of \cite{Va}).   
An IRS $\mu$ is called a Levi IRS if $\mu (L_G)=1$.


\begin{theorem}\label{irsl}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group with nilradical $N$, and $L$ be a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$.  Then the following are equivalent: 
\begin{enumerate}

 \item[(1)] there is an IRS supported on the set of all semisimple Levi subgroups;

\item[(2)] there is a $N$-invariant probability measure supported on semisimple Levi subgroups;

\item[(3)] the semisimple Levi subgroup $L$ is normal in $G$.
\end{enumerate}

In particular, the set of semisimple Levi subgroups $L_G$ is closed in Sub$_G$ if and only if $L$ is normal. 
\end{theorem}
\begin{corollary}
Any Levi IRS of a connected Lie group $G$ is a Dirac measure supported on a Levi subgroup of $G$.
\end{corollary}
So, any connected Lie group $G$ admitting a Levi IRS is of the form $G=L\times S$ where $L$ is semisimple and $S$ is solvable. In particular, $G=\SL(2,\R)\times \R^2$ admits a Levi IRS, whereas $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$ does not admit a Levi IRS.


As in the case of Levi subgroups, the set of all maximal compact subgroups of a connected Lie group is also the orbit of a maximal compact subgroup under the conjugation action. We next provide a necessary and sufficient condition for the set of maximal compact subgroups to support an IRS.

\begin{theorem}\label{irsK}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group and $K$ be a maximal compact subgroup of $G$. Also, let $K_G$ be the set of all maximal compact subgroups of $G$. Then,  
there is an IRS supported on $K_G$ if and only if $K$ is normal in $G$.
\end{theorem}

We now assume that $G$ is a real algebraic group, that is, $G$ is the group of $\R$-points of an algebraic group defined over $\R$. Let $B$ be a Borel subgroup of $G$, i.e., $B$ is a maximal connected solvable subgroup of $G$. Also, let $B_G$ be the set of all Borel subgroups of $G$. Then $B_G$ is the orbit of $B$ under the conjugation action. Suppose $H$ is a locally compact group which acts continuously on a locally convex topological vector space $V$. Then, every $H$-invariant, compact, convex subset of $V$ is called a compact, convex $H$-space. We call $H$ to be amenable if $H$ has a fixed point in every compact, convex $H$-space. Abelian, compact and solvable Lie groups are examples of amenable groups. The readers are referred to Chapter $12$ of \cite{MDW} for more details regarding amenable groups.  The next theorem is about the existence of an IRS supported on $B_G$.  

\begin{theorem}\label{irsB}
Let $G$ be a connected real algebraic group and $B_G$ be the set of all Borel subgroups of $G$. Then $B_G$ is closed in Sub$_G$ and there is an IRS supported on $B_G$ if and only if $G$ is amenable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
Let $G= SO(3, \R) \ltimes \R ^3$.  We identify $O(2, \R)$ as a subgroup of $SO(3, \R)$ by the map 
$\alpha \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & 0 \cr 0 & \det (\ap ) \end{pmatrix}$.  Then $B= SO(2, \R) \ltimes R^3$ is a Borel subgroup of $G$ and the normalizer of $B$ is $O(2, \R)\ltimes \R^3$.  Thus, $SO(3, \R)$-invariant measure on the quotient space $SO(3, \R)/O(2, \R)$ induces an IRS on the set of Borel subgroups $B_G$ of $G$.  
\end{example}

We next consider the orbit of a maximal diagonalizable subgroup $D$. Here, by diagonalizable subgroup, we mean diagonalizable over $\R$, that is, an $\R$-split torus. The set of all maximal diagonalizable subgroups then consists of conjugates of $D$, we denote this set by $D_G$.

\begin{theorem}\label{irsD}
Let $G$ be a connected real algebraic group and $D_G$ be the set of all maximal diagnolizable subgroups of $G$.  Then there is an IRS supported on $D_G$ if and only if $D$ is central in $G$.
\end{theorem}

We have seen that the group $\SL(2,\R)\times \R^2$ admits a Levi IRS, whereas, the group $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$ does not admit a Levi IRS. In particular, the orbit of a semisimple Levi subgroup is not closed in $\text{Sub}_G$ when $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$. This leads to a natural question of studying the closure of the orbit under the conjugation action of a semisimple Levi subgroup. The same question can be asked for other orbits discussed above as well. In this article, we address these questions in the case of the Lie group $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$. Note that, studying these questions is the same as investigating about conjugacy limits of various subgroups of $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$. The study of conjugacy limits is an interesting problem in its own right and has been an active area of research aimed at understanding the space $\text{Sub}_G$. For example, in \cite{L3}, \cite{Ln}, the author has studied the conjugacy limits of diagonal subgroups in $\SL(3,\R)$ and $\SL(n,\R)$ respectively. One may also look at the references cited in these two papers for related works and their relation to geometry.

 Given a closed subgroup $H$ of $G$, by a conjugacy limit of $H$ in $\text{Sub}_G$, we mean the limit of $(g_nHg_n^{-1})$ for a sequence $(g_n)$ in $G$. Note that $(g_nHg_n^{-1})$ always has a convergent subsequence as $\text{Sub}_G$ is compact.
%Our primary motivation to study conjugacy limits inside $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$ comes from the fact that the Levi subgroup $\SL(2,\R)$ is not normal, hence 
%by Theorem \ref{irsl} the set of all Levi subgroups $L_G$ is not closed. So, what is the closure of $L_G$, can we identify all the limit points of $L_G$?
The following theorem gives a complete description of all the limit points of the set $L_G$. We write any element of $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$ as $(g,v)$ with $g\in\SL(2,\R)$ and $v\in\R^2$. Also, for any two elements $(g,v)$ and $(g',v')$, the group operation is defined as follows:
\[
(g,v)(g',v')=(gg',v+g(v')).
\]
We identify $\SL(2,\R)$ and $\R ^2$ as closed subgroups of $G$ by the maps $g \mapsto (g, (0,0))$ 
and $v\mapsto (I,v)$ where $I$ denotes the $2\times 2$ identity matrix.  For any $c\in \R$, we 
define a unipotent subgroup $N^+ _c$ by 
\[
N^+ _c=\left\{ \big(\begin{pmatrix} 1&x\\ 0&1 \end{pmatrix}, (cx, 0) \big):x\in\R\right\}.
\]

For $c=0$, we denote $N^+_c$ by $N^+$, which is identified (and isomorphic) to 
the group of unipotent upper triangular matrices in $\SL(2,\R)$.  

\begin{theorem}\label{limit-L}
Any subgroup in $\overline {L_G}$ is either a Levi subgroup of $G$ or a conjugate of 
$N^+\ltimes \R^2$. 
\end{theorem}

Next, we consider the conjugacy limit of maximal compact subgroups, that is, the closure of
$K_G= \{ gKg^{-1} \mid g\in G \}$ for any maximal compact subgroup $K$ of $G = SL(2, \R)\times \R ^ 2$. For example, we can take $K= SO(2, \R)$.   
For this purpose, we introduce the following subgroups of $SL(2, \R)\ltimes \R ^2$:

\[
V_c=\{ (I, (t, ct)) \mid t \in \R \},\ c\in\R, ~~
V_\infty=\{ (I, (0, t)) \mid t \in \R \}
\]
and 
\[ \Tilde N^+=\left\{ \pm \begin{pmatrix}
    1&s\\ 0&1
\end{pmatrix}:s\in\R\right\}.
\]
It may be noted that $V_c , c\in \R \cup \{\infty \}$ is the set of all one-dimensional subspaces of $\R ^2$, the projective space $\mP ^1$.  

\begin{theorem}\label{limit-K}
The closure of $K_G$ consists of conjugates of the subgroups $V_c,c\in \R\cup\{\infty\}$ and $\Tilde{N^+}$, in addition to the conjugates of $K$.  
\end{theorem}

The next result identifies limit points of the set of maximal diagonalizable subgroups of $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$. Let $A= \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} a &0 \\ 0& \frac{1}{a} \end{pmatrix} \mid a \in \R^+ \right\}$ and $D_G = \{ gA g^{-1} \mid 
g\in SL(2, \R)\ltimes \R ^2 \}A$.  We now introduce the following one-dimensional subgroups
\[
V_{(a,b,c)}= \left\{ \left( \begin{pmatrix} 1& at \\ 0&1 \end{pmatrix} ,\left( ct+\frac{abt^2}{2}, bt\right) \right ) \mid t\in \R \right\} 
\]
for any $a, b, c \in \R$. It may be noted that $V_{a,b,c}$'s are the one-parameter subgroups in the $3$-dimensional Heisenberg group and $V_{(1,0,c)}= N^+_c$, $V_{(0,1,c)}= V_c$ and $V_{(0,0,1)} =V_\infty$.  


\begin{theorem}\label{limit-D}
The closure of $D_G$ consists of conjugates of $V_{(r_1, r_2, r_3)}$ for any $r_1, r_2, r_3 \in \R$ with $r_1 =1$ or $r_3=1$ or $r_1=0$ and $r_2=1$ in addition to the conjugates of the diagonal group $A$.

\end{theorem}


Let $B$ denote the group of all upper triangular matrices in $\SL(2,\R)$. Then $B$ is a Borel subgroup of $\SL(2,\R)$ and $B\ltimes \R^2$ is a Borel subgroup of $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$. Then, using the Iwasawa decomposition in $\SL(2,\R)$, it is easy to see that, for conjugacy limits of $B\ltimes \R^2$, we only need to consider conjugation by elements of $K=\SO(2,\R)$, hence conjugates of $B$ form a closed subset (but by Theorem \ref{irsB} no IRS is supported on the conjugates of $B$ as $G$ is not amenable).  We therefore consider conjugacy limits of $B$ inside $G$ and identify all possible conjugacy limits of $B$
.
\begin{theorem}\label{limits-B}
The closure of $\{ gBg^{-1} \mid g\in SL(2, \R) \ltimes \R ^2 \}$ consists of conjugates of $B$, $N^+\ltimes \{ (t,0) \mid t\in \R \} ~(\simeq \R ^2)$ and $\R^2$. 
\end{theorem}

The next result is concerned about the conjugacy limits of the unipotent subgroup $N^+$. 
\begin{theorem}\label{limits-U}
The closure of $\{ gN^+g^{-1} \mid g\in SL(2, \R) \ltimes \R ^2 \}$ consists of conjugates of $N^+$ and $V_0$.
\end{theorem}


\section{\bf Preliminary}
In this section, we prove certain technical results which are needed to discuss the existence or non-existence of IRS on orbits of the conjugation action of $G$ on $\text{Sub}_G$. Given a finite-dimensional vector space $V$, let $\Gr _k(V)$ be the be the Grassnannian manifold of $k$-dimensional subspaces of $V$.  Equip $V$ with an inner product, and for a subspace $W$ of $V$, let $P_W$ denote the orthogonal projection onto the subspace $W$. We define a metric on $\Gr _k(V)$ using the operator norm $||.||$ of $P_W$'s as follows: for $W,W'\in \Gr _k(V)$, 
\[
d(W,W')=||P_W-P_{W'}||.
\]
The following Lemma is a comparison between the Grassmannian topology and the Chabauty topology of $\Gr _k(V)$ seen as a subset of $\text{Sub}_V$.

\begin{lemma}\label{irsl2}
Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space and $\Gr_k (V)$ be the Grassmannian manifold of $k$-dimensional subspaces of $V$.   
Let $\mathfrak{i} \colon \Gr _k(V) \to {\rm Sub} _V$ be the canonical inclusion.   
Then $\Gr_k (V)$ is homeomorphic to its image.  In particular, the set of all subspaces of dimension $k$ is a closed set in 
Chaubauty topology.    
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $W_n\to W$ in $\Gr _k(V)$.   This implies that $||P_{W_n}-P_W||\rightarrow 0$ as $n\rightarrow\infty$.
Now let $w\in W$, we want to show that there exist $w_n\in W_n$ such that $w_n\rightarrow w$. Let $w_n=P_{W_n}(w)$. Then
clearly $w_n\in W_n$ and $$||w_n-w||=||P_{W_n}(w)-P_W(w)||\leq ||P_{W_n}-P_W||||w||\rightarrow 0$$ as $n\rightarrow \infty$. On
the other hand assume that there exist $w_n\in W_n$ such that $w_n\rightarrow w$, we want to show that $w\in W$. It is enough to
show that $P_W(w)=w$. Since $w_n\rightarrow w$ and $P_{W_n}\rightarrow P_W$, it follows that $P_{W_n}(w_n)\rightarrow P_W(w)$. But
as $P_{W_n}(w_n)=w_n$, and $w_n\rightarrow w$, it follows that $P_W(w)=w$.  Thus, $W_n \to W$ in Sub$_V$.  This proves that the 
map $\pi$ is continuous.  Since $\Gr _k(V)$ is compact, $\Gr _k(V)$ is homeomorphic to $\mathfrak{i} (\Gr _k(V))$.   
\end{proof}
It is known that if a sequence of closed subgroups $H_n$ of a Lie group $G$ converges to a closed subgroup $H$, then 
dim$(H) \geq \limsup ~ \dim (H_n)$ (cf. Theorem 1 of \cite{Wa}).  However, in the case of closed subgroups of thev same dimension and in the case of exponential Lie groups, we have the following.

\begin{proposition}\label{cs}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group and $H_n \to H$ in Sub$_G$.  Let $\mathcal{ G}$, $\mathcal{H} _n$ and $\mathcal{H}$ be Lie algebras of $G$, $H_n$ and $H$ respectively.    
\begin{enumerate}
 \item [(1)] If $\dim (H_n) = \dim (H)$ for all $n$, then $\mathcal{H} _n \to \mathcal{H}$ in Sub$_\mathcal{G}$. 
 \item [(2)] If $G$ is an exponential Lie group and $H_n$'s are connected, then $H$ is also connected, $\mathcal{H}_n \to \mathcal{H}$ in Sub$_\mathcal{G}$ and $\limsup ~\dim (H_n) = \dim (H)$.   

\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
\begin{enumerate}


\item The map sending a closed subgroup to its Lie algebra is known to be a measurable map (cf. proof of Lemma 6 in \cite{Zi}) but here we prove continuity under some restrictions 
though measurability is sufficient for our purpose.  


\item It may be noted that $H$ need not always be connected even when $H_n$ are all connected and have the same dimension: e.g., cases $1$ and $2$ of Section $5.3$ provide a sequence of conjugate subgroups whose limit point has two connected components. 

\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}
Let $V$ be a limit of $\mathcal{H}_n$ in Sub$_\mathcal{G}$.  By passing to a subsequence, we may assume that $\mathcal{H}_n \to V$ in 
Sub$_\mathcal{G}$. Then $V$ is a vector subspace. In is easy to see that it is a Lie subalgebra of $\mathcal{G}$ as well.  For $v\in V$, there are $v_n \in \mathcal{H} _n$ such that 
$v_n \to v$, hence $tv_n \to tv$ for all $t\in \R$.  Let $\exp \colon \mathcal{G} \to G$ be the exponential map.  
Then $\exp (tv_n) \to \exp (tv)$ for all $t\in \R$.  Since $H_n$ converges to $H$, $\exp (tv) \in H$ for all $t\in \R$.  This implies 
that $v\in \mathcal{H}$.  Thus, $V \subset \mathcal{H}$.

Assume that $\dim (H_n) = \dim (H)$ for all $n$.  Then $\dim (\mathcal{H} _n) = \dim (\mathcal{H}) $ for all $n$.  By Lemma \ref{irsl2}, 
$\dim (V) = \dim (\mathcal{H})$.  Since $V\subset \mathcal{H}$, we get that $V = \mathcal{H}$.   Thus, $\mathcal{H} _n \to \mathcal{H}$ in Sub$_\mathcal{G}$.  

Assume that $\exp \colon \mathcal{G} \to G$ is a diffeomorphism and $H_n$ are connected.  Then $\exp \colon \mathcal{H} _n \to H$ is also a diffeomorphism for all $n$.  
Let $h \in H$.  Since $H_n \to H$, there are $h _n \in H_n$ such that $h _n \to h$.  
Let $X_n \in \mathcal{H} _n$ and $X\in \mathcal{H}$ be such that $\exp (X_n) = h_n$ and $\exp (X) =h$.  Since $\exp$ is a diffeomorphism, we get that 
$X_n \to X$.  Since $\mathcal{H} _n \to V \subset \mathcal{H}$, we get that $X\in \mathcal{H}$.  Thus, $\exp (\mathcal{H} ) =H$.  Therefore, $H$ is connected.  Since $\exp$ is a diffeomorphism, we also get that $\mathcal{H} _n \to \mathcal{H}$ 
and hence $V=\mathcal{H}$.  Thus, $\limsup ~\dim (H_n) = \dim (H)$.    


\end{proof} 





The result of the following kind is known as Furstenberg's Lemma (cf. Section 5, \cite{GL}) and it has been proved for 
minimally almost periodic groups in Lemma 3 of \cite{Fu} and when $\Gamma$ (as in Lemma \ref{irsl1}) is certain semisimple analytic group in Proposition 5.1 of \cite{GL}.   Here we provide the following using a result of \cite{Da}.  


\begin{lemma}\label{irsl1}
Let $V$ be a finitedimensional vector space and $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $GL(V)$. Also, let $U$ be a subgroup of $\Gamma$ consisting of unipotent transformations.  
Let $\Gr _k(V)$ be the Grassmannian manifold of $k$-dimensional subspaces of $V$ and $\lambda$ be a probability measure on 
$\Gr _k(V)$ which is invariant under the induced action of $\Gamma$ on $\Gr _k(V)$. Also, let $I_\lambda = \{ \tau \in GL(V) \mid  \tau ~~{\rm is ~~ trivial ~~ on ~} S \}$ where $S$ is the support of $\lambda$. Then $\Gamma$ is contained in an algebraic group $\tilde \Gamma$ such that $\tilde \Gamma / I_\lambda $ is a compact group, and $S$ consists of $U$-invariant subspaces. 

\end{lemma}



\begin{proof}

Let $\lambda $ be the $\Gamma$-invariant probability measure on $\Gr _k(V)$. 
Let $G_\lambda = \{\tau \in GL(V) \mid \tau (\lambda ) = \lambda \}$ and 
$I_\lambda = \{ \tau \in GL(V) \mid  \tau ~~{\rm is ~~ trivial ~~ on ~} S \}$ where $S$ is the support of $\lambda$. 
Then $I_\lambda $ is a normal algebraic subgroup of $G_\lambda $.  Since $\Gr _k(V)\simeq GL(V)/H$ where $H$ is the stablizer of 
a $k$-dimensional subspace, by Corollary 2.6 of \cite{Da} we get that $G_\lambda$ is a real algebraic 
group and $G_\lambda /I_\lambda $ is compact.  Thus, any unipotent transformation in $G_\lambda $ is in $I_ \lambda$.  
This proves the result.  
\end{proof}



\begin{proposition}\label{irsp1}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group with a closed connected Lie subgroup $S$.  Let $\Gamma$ be a $\sigma$-compact subgroup of Aut$(G)$ and 
$U$ be a subgroup of $\Gamma$ consisting of unipotent automorphisms.   
Suppose there is a $\Ga$-invariant probability measure $\mu$ on ${\rm Sub}_G$ such that $\mu (\{ \tau (S) \mid \tau \in \Gamma \}) =1$.  
Then $S$ is $N$-invariant where $N$ is the smallest normal subgroup of $\Gamma$ containing $U$.

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ and $\mathcal{S}$ be the Lie algebras of $G$ and $S$ respectively.   
Let $\mathcal {O} = \{ \tau (S) \mid \tau \in {\rm Aut}~(G ) \}$.  
Let $X= \Gr _k (\mathcal{G})$ be the Grassmannian manifold of $k$-dimensional subspaces of $\mathcal{G}$ 
where $k$ is the dimension of $\mathcal{S}$.  Let
$\phi \colon  \mathcal{O} \to X$ be defined by $\phi (\tau (S) )= {\rm d}\tau (\mathcal{S})$.  Then it follows from Proposition \ref{cs} and Lemma \ref{irsl2} that $\phi$ is continuous.

Let $\lambda = \phi (\mu )$.  Consider the canonical action of $\Gamma$ on $X$ given by: $(\tau , V) \mapsto {\rm d}\tau (V)$
for $\tau \in \Gamma$ and $V\in X$.  Then $\lambda$ is a $\Gamma$-invariant probability measure on $X$.  By Lemma \ref{irsl1}, $\lambda$ is supported on $U$-invariant subspaces.  This proves that $\mathcal{S}$ is $N$-invariant.  Since $S$ is a connected Lie subgroup, $S$ is $N$-nvariant.    
\end{proof}

\section{\bf IRS on orbits}
In this section, we show the existence and non-existence of invariant random subgroups supported on various orbits of the conjugation action of a connected Lie group $G$ on Sub$_G$. In other words, we discuss the proofs of Theorems \ref{irsl}, \ref{irsK}, \ref{irsB} and \ref{irsD}. We also discuss certain structural results related to these theorems. 
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{irsl}]
It is sufficient to prove that (2) implies (3).  
Let $L$ be a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ and $N$ be the nilpotent radical of $G$ as in the statement of the theorem.  Then $L$ is a connected Lie subgroup of $G$.  Suppose that there is an IRS supported on the set $L_G$ of all semisimple Levi subgroups. Since any two semisimple Levi subgroups conjugate in $G$ by an element of $N$, there is a $N$-invariant probability measure $\mu$ such that $\mu (\{ xLx^{-1} \mid x \in N \}) =1$.  Let $\mathcal{G}$ be the Lie algebra of $G$.  Since $N$ is a nilpotent normal subgroup of $G$, Ad$(x)$ is a unipotent transformation on $\mathcal{G}$ for all $x\in N$.  Therefore, by Proposition \ref{irsp1}, $\mu$ is supported on $N$-invariant subspaces of $\mathcal{G}$.  Thus, $L$ is normalized by $N$.  Since any two semisimple Levi subgroups are conjugate by an element of $N$, we get that $L$ is normal in $G$.  

Suppose the set of all semisimple Levi subgroups is closed in Sub$_G$. Then $L_G$ is compact; and, since $N$ is amenable, 
$L_G$ admits an $N$-invariant probability measure.  Therefore, $(2)$ implies $(3)$ proves that $L$ is normal in $G$.

\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{irsK}]
Suppose that there is an IRS $\mu$ such that $\mu (K_G)=1$, where $K_G$ is the set of all maximal compact subgroups of $G$ with $K$ being a maximal compact subgroup of $G$. Then $K$ is connected and $K_G=\{xKx^{-1} \mid x\in G \}$ 
(cf. Theorem 3.1, Chapter XV of \cite{Ho}), hence by Proposition \ref{irsp1}, 
$K$ is normalized by any Ad-unipotent subgroup of $G$.   

Let $L$ be a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ and $K_l$ be a maximal compact subgroup of $L$. Then $K_l \subset gKg^{-1}$ for some $g\in G$.  Replacing $K$ by a conjugate, we may assume that $K$ contains 
$K_l$. This, in particular, implies that $K$ is normalized by compact simple factors of $L$. Let $L_1$ be a noncompact simple factor of $L$.  Then $L_1$ is generated by the Ad-unipotent elements of $L_1$. Therefore, $L_1$ normalizes $K$. Thus, the seimsimple Levi subgroup $L$ normalizes $K$.  

Now, let $N$ be the nilpotent radical of $G$. Then Ad$(x)$ is unipotent for any $x\in N$, hence $N$ normalizes $K$. Thus, $NK$ is a closed connected subgroup of $G$. Since $K$ is a maximal compact subgroup of $G$, $K$ is a maximal compact subgroup of $NK$. Since $N$ normalizes $K$, $K$ is normal in $NK$ and hence contains any compact subgroup of $NK$. Recall that any two maximal compact subgroups of a connected Lie groups are conjugate.     
Let $T=N\cap K$.  Then $T$ is the largest compact normal subgroup of $N$ and hence $N/T$ is a simply connected nilpotent Lie group.  Since $N$ is normal in $G$ and $T$ is the largest compact normal subgroup of $N$, 
$T$ is normal in $G$.  Since $T\subset K$, replacing $G$ by $G/T$, we may assume that $N$ is simply connected.  

Let $\tilde G = G/N$ and $\tilde K$ be a maximal compact subgroup of $\tilde G$ containing $KN/N$.  Then $\tilde K$ is connected and there is a closed 
subgroup $H$ of $G$ containing $N$ such that $H/N = \tilde K$.  Since $N$ and $\tilde K$ are connected, $H$ is also connected (cf. Theorem 7.4 of \cite{HeR}). As $N$ is simply connected, $H$ contains a compact subgroup $M$ such that $H= MN$ and $\tilde K= H/N \simeq M$ (cf. Theorem 3.2, Chapter XII of \cite{Ho}). Also, $K\subset H$ as $H/N =\tilde K$ contains $KN/N$. Since $M$ is a compact subgroup of $H$ and $K$ is a maximal compact subgroup, $M\subset xKx^{-1}$ for some $x\in H=MN$. But $N$ normalizes $K$, hence $M\subset K$. This shows that $\tilde K = KN/N$. 

Now, let $R$ be the solvable radical of $G$.  Then $N\subset R$ and by Corollary 3.16.4 of \cite{Va}, $R/N$ is the center of $\tilde G$, hence $\tilde K$ is normalized by $R/N$.  This shows that $KN$ is normalized by $R$.  Since the semisimple Levi subgroup $L$ normalizes $K$, $L$ normalizes $KN$ as well. It follows that $KN$ is a normal subgroup of $G$.  

As $N$ normalizes $K$, $K$ is a normal subgroup of $KN$.  
Since $N$ is a simply connected nilpotent Lie group, $N\cap K= \{  e\}$ and hence $KN/K~(\simeq N)$ has no compact subgroup.  
This proves that $K$ is a normal subgroup of $G$.  
\end{proof}
The existence of an IRS supported on the set of maximal compact subgroups has certain implications on the structure of the connected Lie group $G$ under consideration as described in the following proposition.
\begin{proposition}\label{s1}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group with solvable radical $R$ and $M$ be a maximal compact subgroup of $G$.  Suppose $M$ is normal in $G$.  
Then we have the following:
\begin{enumerate}

\item solvable radical $R$ admits a compact central subgroup $T$ so that $R/T$ is simply connected; 

\item any closed simple Lie subgroup of $G$ is either compact or has no nontrivial compact connected subgroups.

\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
The universal cover of $SL(2, \R)$ is a simple Lie group without any compact subgroup of positive dimension. Thus the second possibility in 2 of Proposition \ref{s1} may occur.
\end{remark} 


\begin{proof}
Suppose the maximal compact subgroup $M$ of $G$ is normal in $G$.  If $R$ is the solvable radical of $G$, then $R\cap M$ is a 
maximal compact subgroup of $R$. Since $M$ is normal in $G$, $R\cap M$ is normal in $R$. If $T= R\cap M$, then $T$ is abelian and $R/T$ has no compact subgroup. Thus, $R/T$ is simply connected (cf. exercise 3, Chapter XII of \cite{Ho}).   

Let $S$ be any simple Lie subgroup of $G$. Suppose that there exists a non-trivial compact connected subgroup of $S$. Then $M\cap S$ is a compact normal subgroup of positive dimension in $S$.  Since $S$ is simple, $S= S\cap M$.
\end{proof}

Now, we assume that $G$ is a real algebraic group. Also, let $B$ be a Borel subgroup of $G$ and $B_G$ be the set of all Borel subgroups of $G$, i.e., $B_G=\{xBx^{-1}|x\in G\}$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{irsB}]
Since $B$ is a Borel subgroup of $G$, $G/B$ is compact, and hence there is a compact set $C$ in $G$ such that $G=CB$.  
Since any two Borel subgroups are conjugate in $G$, $B_G = \{xBx^{-1} \mid x \in C \}$.  Thus, $B_G$ is a compact subset of 
Sub$_G$.

Suppose that there is an IRS $\mu$ such that $\mu (B_G)=1$. To prove that $G$ is amenable, it is sufficient to prove that any
semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ is compact. Let $R$ be the solvable radical of $G$. Then $R\subset B$, hence any Borel subgroup of $G$ is a lift of a Borel subgroup of $G/R$. Thus, replacing $G$ by $G/R$, we may assume that $G$ is semisimple. Now, let $S$ be a simple factor of $G$. If $S$ is non-compact, then $S$ is generated by unipotent elements. This implies by Proposition \ref{irsp1} that $B$ is normalized by $S$, hence $B\cap S$ is a solvable normal subgroup of $S$ containing a Borel subgroup of $S$.  This is a contradiction to the simplicity of $S$.  
Hence, any simple factor of $G$ is compact. This completes the proof.  
\end{proof}


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{irsD}]
Let $D$ be a maximal diagnolizable subgroup of $G$ and $U$ be the unipotent radical of $G$.  
Then there is a reductive Levi subgroup $L$ of $G$ containing $D$ such that $G= LU$.   
Let $L'= [L, L]$.  Then $L'$ is a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$.   

Suppose that there is an IRS $\mu$ such that $\mu (D_G)=1$. Then, by Proposition \ref{irsp1}, 
$D$ is normalized by any unipotent subgroup of $G$. If $S$ is a noncompact simple algebraic subgroup of $G$, then maximal split diagonalizable subgroup $D_S$ of $S$ is contained in 
a conjugate of $D$. Replacing $S$ by a conjugate of $S$, we may assume that $D_S\subset D$. Since $S$ is noncompact, $S$  is generated by unipotent elements, hence $S$ normalizes $D$. Therefore, $D\cap S$ is a normal subgroup of positive dimension which is a contradiction to the simplicity of $S$. Thus, the semisimple 
Levi subgroup $L'= [L, L]$ of $G$ is compact. This implies that $D \subset Z(L)$ because $L=[L, L] Z(L)$.  

Since $D$ is normalized by the unipotent subgroups, $D$ is normalized by $U$.  Since $D\cap U$ is trivial, $D$ is centralized by $U$ also.  Since $G= LU$, $D$ is central in $G$.   

\end{proof}

The maximal diagonalizable subgroup being normal has certain implications on the structure of $G$ which is noted down below.


\begin{proposition}\label{s2}
Let $G$ be a connected real algebraic group and $D$ be a maximal diagonalizable subgroup of $G$. In addition, suppose $D$ is normal in $G$.  
Then any Levi subgroup of $G$ is a compact subgroup, and the solvable radical is a compact extension of a nilpotent group.
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}
Let $H$ be a noncompact simple factor of a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ and $D_H$ be the maximal diagonalizable subgroup of $H$. Then $D_H$ is contained in a conjugate of $D$. Since $D$ is normal, $D_H\subset D$. This implies that $D\cap H$ is an abelian normal subgroup of positive dimension in $H$, which is a contradiction to the simplicity of $H$. Therefore, any semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ is compact.  

Now, let $R$ be the solvable radical of $G$. Then $R\cap D$ is a normal subgroup $R$, hence $D_RU$ is a nilpotent subgroup of $R$ 
where $D_R = D\cap R$ and $U$ is the unipotent radical of $G$.  Since $R/D_RU$ is a anisotropic torus (that is, without any split torus 
subgroup) we get that $R/D_RU$ is compact. 
\end{proof}  




\section{\bf Application to measure preserving actions}

Let $G$ be a locally compact group and $(X,m)$ be a probability space on which $G$ acts by measure preserving transformations.   
It follows from \cite{Va1} that the stabilizer of almost every point in X is closed in G and the stabilizer map $x\mapsto G_x$ is measurable 
where $G_x = \{g\in G \mid gx =x \}$ for $x\in X$.  Thus, the $G$-invariant probability measure $m$ on $X$ may be realized as an IRS on G 
which leads to the following: 

\begin{corollary}\label{a1} Let $G$ be a connected Lie group and $(X,m)$ be a probability space on which $G$ acts by measure preserving transformations. 
Then almost none of the stabilizer subgroups is a semisimple Levi subgroup (resp. maximal compact subgroup) of $G$ or $m$ has an atom $x$ such that $G_x$ is a semisimple Levi subgroup (resp. maximal compact subgroup) of $G$ and $G_x$ is normal in $G$. In addition, if the action of $G$ is ergodic, then almost none of the stabilizer is a semisimple Levi subgroup of $G$ or the action of $G$ factors through the solvable radical of $G$.
\end{corollary} 

\begin{proof}
Let $L_G$ be the set of all semisimple Levi subgroups of $G$ and $X_L = \{ x\in X \mid G_x  \in L_G \}$. Since $L_G$ is invariant under the conjugation action and $G_{gx}=gG_xg^{-1}$, it follows that $X_L$ is invariant under the action of $G$. Suppose $m(X_L) >0$. Then, replacing $X$ by $X_L$ and $m$ by the restriction of $m$ to $X_L$, we obtain a measure preserving action of $G$ such that almost all stabilizers are Levi subgroups of $G$.  Using the identification of $x\mapsto G_x$, we obtain a IRS supported on the set of semisimple Levi subgroups.  By Theorem  \ref{irsl}, we 
get that $G$ has a unique semisimple Levi subgroup, say L, and $L$ is normal in $G$.  The case of maximal compact subgroups may be proved in a similar way.  

Suppose the action of $G$ is ergodic.  Then $X_L$ has full measure or null.  In case $X_L$ has full measure, any $G_x=G_y$ for all $x, y \in X_L$ and hence the semisimple Levi subgroup acts trivially on $X$, hence the action factors through the solvable radical of $G$.  


\end{proof}

A similar application can also be done for maximal diagonalizable groups and Borel subgroups as in the following: we omit the details as it is same as in Corollary \ref{a1}

\begin{corollary}\label{a10} Let $G$ be a connected real algebraic group and $(X,m)$ be a probability space on which $G$ acts by measure preserving transformations. 
Then almost none of the stabilizer subgroup is a Borel (resp. maximal diagonalizable) subgroup of $G$ or $m$ has an atom $x$ such that $G_x$ is a Borel 
(resp. maximal diagonalizable) subgroup of $G$ and $G_x$ is normal in $G$.  In addition if the action of $G$ is ergodic, then almost none of the stabilizer is a 
Borel (resp. maximal diagonalizable) subgroup of $G$ or the action of $G$ factors through a compact subgroup (resp. compact extension of the unipotent radical) of $G$.
\end{corollary} 




\begin{corollary}\label{ak}
Let $G$ be a connected Lie group and $K$ be a maximal compact subgroup of $G$.  Suppose $G/K$ carries a $G$-invariant probability measure.  
Then $G$ is a compact group. 
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Let $m$ be the $G$-invariant probability measure on $G/K$.  Then $(G/K, m)$ is a probability space on which $G$ acts by measure preserving transformations and stabilizer 
at any $xK\in G/K$ is $xKx^{-1}$.  Therefore by Corollary \ref{a1}, $K$ is normal in $G$, hence $G/K$ is a locally compact group.  Since $G/K$ carries a $G$-invariant 
probability measure, $G/K$ is compact.  Thus, $G$ is compact.  
\end{proof}


\begin{corollary}\label{a2}
Let $G$ be a connected real algebraic group and $D$ be a maximal diagonalizable subgroup of $G$. Suppose $G/D$ carries a $G$-invariant probability measure. Then $G$ is a direct product of $D$ and a compact group. 
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Let $m$ be the $G$-invariant probability measure on $G/D$.  Then $(G/D, m)$ is a probability space on which $G$ acts by measure preserving transformations and stabilizer 
at any $xD\in G/D$ is $xDx^{-1}$.  Therefore by Corollary \ref{a10}, $D$ is normal in $G$.  By Proposition \ref{s2}, the Levi subgroup of $G$ is a compact normal subgroup 
and the solvable radical is nilpotent.  Since $G/D$ has a $G$-invariant probability measure, unipotent radical of $G$ is trivial.  Thus, $G$ is a direct product of $D$ and a compact group.  
\end{proof}


\section{\protect{\bf Conjugacy limits in $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$}}

\subsection{Limits of levi subgroups}
From now onwards, we use the notation $G$ for $\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$, and the notation $L$ for the group $\SL(2,\R)$ inside $G$. Also, let $L_G$ be the set of all semsimple Levi subgroups inside $G$. Then $L_G$ consists of conjugates of $L$ by elements of $G$. We denote elements in $G$ by $(g,v)$ with $g\in\SL(2,\R)$ and $v\in\R^2$. Now, if $(G_n)$ is a sequence of Levi subgroups in $L_G$, then each $G_n$ is given by a conjugate of $L$ by some elements of $G$, i.e.,
\[
G_n=\ (g_n,v_n)\ L\ (g_n,v_n)^{-1},
\]
where $(g_n,v_n)^{-1}$ denotes the inverse of $(g_n,v_n)$ in $G$.

If we conjugate $L$ by elements of the form $(g,(0,0))$, the resultant is $L$ itself. Also, note that $(g,v)=(I,v)\ (g,(0,0))$, where $I$ is the identity element in $\SL(2,\R)$. Therefore, to determine all the limit points of $L_G$, it is enough to consider the conjugacy limits of the form
\[(I,v_n)\ L\ (I,v_n)^{-1}.
\]
The following lemma is a key ingredient for the results in this section and afterwards.
\begin{lemma}\label{el1} 
Let $(g_n)$ and $(v_n)$ be sequences in $\SL (2,\R)$ and $\R^2$ respectively such that $v_n \not \to 0$ and 
\[(I,v_n)\ (g_n,(0,0))\ (I,v_n)^{-1}=(g_n,v_n-g_n(v_n))\rightarrow (g,(0,0)) \] 
for some $g\in\SL(2,\R)$.  Then $g$ is unipotent. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} 
Suppose $(g_n)$ and $(v_n)$ are sequences in $\SL (2,\R)$ and $\R ^2$ respectively such that $v_n\not \to 0$ and 
\[(I,v_n)\ (g_n,(0,0))\ (I,v_n)^{-1}=(g_n,v_n-g_n(v_n))\rightarrow (g,(0,0))\] for some $g\in\SL(2,\R)$.  
Then $g_n \to g $ in $SL_2(\R )$ and  $(I-g_n)(v_n)\rightarrow (0,0)$ as $n\rightarrow\infty$.  
Note that if $I-g$ is invertible, then since $g_n \to g$, $I-g _n \to I-g$.  Since invertible matrices form an open set, 
$I-g_n$ is invertible for all large $n$ and hence $(I-g_n)^{-1} \to (I-g)^{-1}$. As $(I-g_n)(v_n)\rightarrow (0,0)$, we arrive at a contradiction to the assumption that $v_n \not \to 0$. Therefore, $I-g$ is not invertible.  Since $g\in SL( 2, \R )$, $g$ is an unipotent transformation.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{limit-L}]

Assume that $(I,v_n)\ L\ (I,v_n)^{-1}$ converges to $H$ in $\text{Sub}_G$; we want to determine $H$. The proof is divided into following caes.

\noindent {\bf Case $1$.} Suppose $v_n\rightarrow v$ as $n\rightarrow\infty$. In this case, it is easy to see that $(I,v_n)\ L\ (I,v_n)^{-1}$ converges to the closed subgroup
$(I,v)\ L\ (I,v)^{-1}$, and hence the limit $H$ is a Levi subgroup.

\smallskip

We now assume that $(v_n)$ is unbounded. It follows from Lemma \ref{el1} that if $g\in SL(2, \R ) \cap H$, then $g$ is unipotent.\\ 
\noindent {\bf Case $2$.} Suppose $v_n=(\alpha_n,\beta_n)$ and $\alpha_n$ and $\beta_n$ both diverge.   We first show that the subgroup $\R^2$ is contained in $H$. Let $\mathfrak{g}_n=\begin{pmatrix} 1&\frac{-s}{\beta_n}\\ 0&1 \end{pmatrix}$. Then
\[
(I,v_n)\ (\mathfrak{g}_n,(0,0))\ (I,v_n)^{-1}=(\mathfrak{g}_n,v_n-\mathfrak{g}_n(v_n))=(\mathfrak{g}_n,(s,0))\rightarrow (I,(s,0))
\]
as $n\rightarrow\infty$. Similarly, considering
\[
\mathfrak{h}_n=\begin{pmatrix}
    1&0\\ \frac{-t}{\alpha_n}&1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
it is easy to see that
\[
(I,v_n)\ (\mathfrak{h}_n,(0,0))\ (I,v_n)^{-1}\rightarrow (0,t)
\]
as $n\rightarrow\infty$. Then composing $(I,(s,0))$ and $(I,(0,t))$, we see that $(I,(s,t))\in H$ for any $s,t\in\R$.

Since $G= SL(2, \R) \ltimes \R ^2$ and $\R ^2 \subset H$, we get that $H= (H\cap L)\ltimes \R^2$.  But $L\cap H$ consists of unipotent
transformations.  Since $N^+$ and its conjugates in $SL(2, \R)$ are the maximal subgroups with unipotent elements,  $H$ is given by
\[
H = hN^+h^{-1}\ltimes \R^2
\]
for some $h\in\SL(2,\R)$, where
\[
N^+=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
    1&s\\ 0&1
\end{pmatrix}:s\in\R\right\},
\]

\noindent {\bf Case $3$.} The sequence $(v_n)=(\alpha_n,\beta_n)$ diverges but one of $\alpha_n$ or $\beta_n$ is bounded. First suppose, $v_n=(\alpha_n,0)$ with $\alpha_n$ diverging. Then, for any real number $s$, if we take $\mathfrak{g}_n:=\begin{pmatrix} \frac{\displaystyle{\alpha_n-s}}{\displaystyle{\alpha_n}}&0\\ 0&\frac{\displaystyle{\alpha_n}}{\displaystyle{\alpha_n-s}}\end{pmatrix}$, we see that $$(I,v_n)(\mathfrak{g}_n,(0,0))(I,v_n)^{-1} \to (I,(s,0)) .$$ Similarly, considering $\mathfrak{h}_n:=\begin{pmatrix} 1&0\\ \frac{\displaystyle{-s}}{\displaystyle{\alpha_n}}&1 \end{pmatrix} $, we see that $$(I,v_n)(\mathfrak{h}_n,(0,0))(I,v_n)^{-1}\to (0,s).$$ This shows that $\R^2$ is contained in the limit subgroup $H$. Also, since any element of $N^+$ fixes $v_n$, it follows that $N^+$ is contained in $H$ as well. Now it follows, as in {\it Case $2$}, that
\[
H=N^+\ltimes \R^2.
\]
Similarly, if $v_n=(0,\alpha_n)$ with $\alpha_n$ diverging, then
\[
H=N^-\ltimes \R^2.
\]
where
\[
N^-=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
    1&0\\ s&1
\end{pmatrix}:s\in\R\right\}.
\]

Now suppose $v_n=(\alpha_n,\beta_n)$ with $\alpha_n$ diverging and $\beta_n$ bounded. As we are looking for limit points of the set of all Levi subgroups, we may assume in this case that $\alpha_n$ diverges and $\beta_n$ converges to some $\beta$. We can write $(I,(\alpha_n,\beta_n))=(I,(\alpha_n,0))(I,(0,\beta_n))$. So, if $(I,(\alpha_n,0))L(I,(\alpha_n,0))^{-1}$ converges to the subgroup $H$, then\\ $(I,v_n)L(I,v_n)^{-1}$ converges to the subgroup $(I,(0,\beta))H(I,(0,\beta))^{-1}$.\\ Therefore, $H$ is a conjugate of $N^+\ltimes \R^2$. 

Thus, any subgroup in $\overline {L_G}$ is either a Levi subgroup or a conjugate of $N^+\ltimes \R^2$.

\end{proof}

\vspace{0.2cm}

\subsection {Limits of maximal compact subgroups}

\vspace{0.2 cm}
We now identify the limit points of the set of maximal compact subgroups. In other words, we find the closure of the orbit of $K$ under the conjugacy action of $G$ inside $\text{Sub}_G$. 
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{limit-K}]
Let us recall that $K$ is given by  
\[
K:=\ \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}
    \text{cos}\ \theta & \text{sin}\ \theta\\
    -\text{sin}\ \theta & \text{cos}\ \theta
\end{pmatrix}\ :\ \theta\in\R \right\}. 
\]
In order to find the limit points of 
\[
K_G=\{ (g, v) K (g, v) ^{-1}\mid g \in SL (2, \R), v\in \R ^2 \},
\]
it is sufficient to identify the limit points up to conjugation. Using Iwasawa decomposition, i.e.,  $SL(2, \R) = N^+AK$, we may assume that $g\in N^+A$.  Let $H$ be a limit point of $K_G$. Then there exist sequences $$\mathfrak{a}_n = \begin{pmatrix} a_n & 0 \\ 0 & a_n^{-1} \end{pmatrix} \in A, ~ \mathfrak{s}_n  = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & s_n \\ 0 &1 \end{pmatrix} \in N^+ $$ and 
$v_n = (\alpha _n , \beta _n)\in \R ^2$ such that $(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n)K(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n) ^{-1} \to H$. By passing to a subsequence and replacing $\mathfrak{a}_n$ or $\mathfrak{s}_n$ by their conjugates, we may assume that $a_n \to \infty$ or $a_n =1$ and $s_n \to \infty$ or $s_n =0$.  

Let $(g, v)\in H$.  Then there exists $$\Theta _n = \begin{pmatrix}
    \cos \theta_n & \sin \theta_n\\
    -\sin \theta_n & \cos \theta_n
\end{pmatrix} \in K$$ such that $(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n)\Theta _n(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n) ^{-1} \to (g, v)$

Now,
\[
(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n) \Theta _n(\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n, v_n) ^{-1} = (T_n , (x_n, y_n)),
\]
where 
\[
T_n = \begin{pmatrix} \cos \theta _n -\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n  & (\frac{s_n ^2}{a_n^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n \\ -\sin \theta _n / a_n ^2 & \cos \theta _n +\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \end{pmatrix}
\]
and 
\[
x_n = \alpha _n \big(1-\cos \theta _n +{\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}}\sin \theta _n \big) +\beta _n  \big(\frac{s_n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2\big) \sin \theta _n,
\]
\[
y_n = -\alpha _n \frac{\sin \theta _n}{a_n ^2} + \beta _n \big(1-\cos \theta _n -\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \big).
\]

We now assume that $v_n \to \infty$. As $\big(T_n , (x_n , y_n)\big) \to (g, v)$, it follows from Lemma \ref{el1} that $g$ is unipotent.  

\noindent {\bf Case $1$:} We now consider the case when ${\frac{\alpha _n}{\beta _n}} \to 0$.  Since $v_n$ diverges, $\beta _n \to \infty$.  Let $x,y\in \R$ be such that $v= (x,y)$.  Then
\[
x_n = \alpha _n \big(1-\cos \theta _n +\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \big) +\beta _n \big(\frac{s_n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2\big) \sin \theta _n  \to x.
\]
Since $T_n \to g$, $\cos \theta _n -\frac{s_n}{a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n $ converges.  Therefore, 
\[
x_n = \beta _n \Big[\frac{\alpha _n }{ \beta _n}\big(1-\cos \theta _n +\frac{s_n}{ a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \big)
+  \big(\frac{s _n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2\big) \sin \theta _n\Big]
\]
converges implies that $\big(\frac{s_n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2\big) \sin \theta _n \to 0$. Since $g$ is unipotent, it follows that $g= I$. 

Also, for any $t\in \R$, we can choose $\zeta _n \to 0$ so that $ \beta _n (\frac{s_n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \zeta _n  \to t$.  Then $\beta _n\frac{s_n }{a_n ^2}\sin \zeta _n \to 0$
and $\beta _n \sin \zeta _n$ converges (to $0$ or $t$ 
depending on if $s_n ^2 +a_n ^2 \to \infty$ or not).  Since $\beta _n \to \infty$, we also get that 
$(\frac{s_n^2}{a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \zeta _n  \to 0$.  Since $\zeta _n \to 0$ and $\beta _n \to \infty$, we get that $\mathfrak{s}_n \mathfrak{a}_n Z_n \mathfrak{a}_n ^{-1} \mathfrak{s}_n ^{-1} \to I$ for $Z_n = \begin{pmatrix}
    \cos \zeta_n & \sin \zeta_n\\
    -\sin \zeta_n & \cos \zeta_n
\end{pmatrix} \in K$.  Since $\zeta _n \to 0$, $( 1-\cos \zeta _n ) / \sin \zeta _n \to 0$, hence  
$\alpha _n (1-\cos \zeta _n)= (\beta _n \sin \zeta _n)(\frac{\alpha _n }{\beta _n})(\frac{1-\cos \zeta _n }{\sin \zeta _n}) \to 0 $ and 
$-\alpha _n {\sin \zeta _n\over a_n ^2} + \beta _n \big(1-\cos \zeta _n -{s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \zeta _n \big) = {-\alpha _n \over \beta _n}{\beta _n \sin \zeta _n \over a_n^2} 
+\beta _n \sin \zeta _n ({1-\cos \zeta _n \over \sin \zeta _n })- \beta _n {s_n\over a_n ^2} \sin \zeta _n \to 0$.  Thus, 
\[
(\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{a}_n,v_n)\ Z_n\ (\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{a}_n,v_n)^{-1} \to (I, (t,0)).
\]

Now, replacing $\Theta _n $ by $\Theta _n Z _n^{-1}$, we may assume that $x_n \to 0$.   
Since $({s_n^2\over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n \to 0$ and ${s_n ^2\over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2 
\not \to 0$, we get that $\sin \theta _n \to 0$.  Replacing $\theta _n$ by a translate of $2k\pi$, we may assume that $\theta _n\to 0$.  This implies that ${1-\cos \theta_n\ \over \sin \theta _n }\to 0$.  Since $x_n \to 0$, 
we get that $\beta _n ({s_n ^2 \over a_n^2 }+ a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n \to 0$, hence 
$\alpha _n (1-\cos \theta _n +{s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n ) \to 0$.  
Therefore, $$|\alpha _n{- \sin \theta _n \over a_n^2} | \leq |{\alpha _n \over \beta _n}| |
\beta _n ({s_n ^2 \over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n  | \to 0$$ and 
$$\begin{array}{cl}  & |\beta _n (1-\cos \theta _n -{s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n ) |   \\ = &
|{1-\cos \theta _n \over \sin \theta _n }{a_n ^2\over s_n ^2 +a_n ^4} -{s_n\over s_n ^2+a_n ^4} |
|\beta _n ({s_n ^2 \over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n | \to 0. \end{array}  $$
This implies that $H= V_0= \{ (I, (x, 0)) \mid x\in \R\}$.

\noindent {\bf Case $2$: } Suppose ${\beta _n \over \alpha _n} \to 0$.  
Let $F= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1& 0 \end{pmatrix}$.  Since $(g_n , v_n) K (g_n, v_n)^{-1} \to H$, 
conjugating by $F$, we get that $(Fg_n, Fv_n) K (Fg_n, Fv_n)^{-1} \to FHF^{-1}$.  Since $Fv$ satisfies case (i) we get that $FHF^{-1} = V_0$.  Thus, $H= V_\infty = \{ (I, (0,y) \mid y\in \R \}$.


\noindent {\bf Case $3$: } Suppose $c\not =0$ is a limit point of $|{\alpha _n \over \beta _n}|$.  
By passing to a subsequence we may assume that ${\alpha _n \over \beta _n} \to c$.  
There are two cases here:  if one of $a_n $ or $s_n$ diverges to $\infty$, then as in case (i) we can show 
that $H= V_0$.   We now assume that $a_n =1$ and $s_n=0$.  Then, choosing $\zeta _n\rightarrow 0$ such that $\beta_n\sin \zeta_n \rightarrow t$ for any preassigned real number $t$, it is easy to see that 
\[g_nZ_n g_n^{-1}\rightarrow \big(I,\ (t,-ct)\big). \] for suitable $Z_n \in K$.    
Therefore, in this case as in case (i) we may show that 
$H= V_c=\{ (I, (t, ct)) \mid t \in \R \}$.

We now consider the case when $(v_n)$ has a convergent subsequence.  In this case, by passing to a 
subsequence and conjugating by $(I, -v_n)$, we may assume that $(\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{a}_n, 0) K(\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{a}_n ,0)^{-1} \to H$.  
Since $\mathfrak{s}_n , \mathfrak{a} _n \in SL(2, \R)$ and $K\subset SL(2, \R)$, $H\subset SL(2, \R)$.  
Recall that we also have $s_n \to \infty$ or $s_n=0$ and $a_n \to \infty$ or $a_n=1$.  
If both $s_n=0$ and $a_n =1$, we are done.  So we may assume that either $s_n \to \infty $ or $a_n \to \infty$.  
We consider the case that $s_n \to \infty$ and other case that $a_n \to \infty$ is similar.  
 We have $$T_n = \begin{pmatrix} \cos \theta _n -{s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n  & ({s_n ^2\over a_n^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n \\ {-\sin \theta _n / a_n ^2} & \cos \theta _n +{s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \end{pmatrix} \to g = \begin{pmatrix} g_{1,1} & g_{1,2} \\ g_{2,1} & g_{2,2} 
\end{pmatrix}, {~~\rm say}.$$  This in particular, implies that $({s_n^2\over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n$ 
converges.  Since ${s_n\over a_n^2} +a_n^2 \to \infty$, $\sin \theta _n \to 0$ and since 
${\sin \theta _n \over a_n^2 }\to g_{2,1}$, we get that $g_{2,1}=0$.  
Also, for $t\in \R$, we choose 
$\zeta _n\to 0$ such that $({s_n^2\over a_n ^2}+a_n ^2)\sin \zeta _n \to t$.  Then $\cos \zeta _n\to 1 $ and 
${s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \zeta _n \to 0$, hence $g_n Z_ng_n^{-1} \to \begin{pmatrix} 1  & t \\ 
0 & 1\end{pmatrix}  $ for suitable $Z_n\in K$. If, in addition $g_{1,2}=0$, then
$({s_n ^2\over a_n^2}+a_n ^2) \sin \theta _n \to 0$ and hence ${s_n\over a_n ^2}\sin \theta _n \to 0$.  
Thus, $\cos \theta _n$ converges to $g_{1,1}$ as well as $g_{2,2}$ but $g_{1,1} g_{2,2}=1$.  Therefore, 
$g_{1,1}=\pm 1 = g_{2,2}$.  In fact, taking $\theta _n = \pi$, 
we get that 
 \[ 
g_n\Theta_ng_n^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
    \cos \theta_n - {s_n\over a_n^2}\ \sin \theta_n & ({s_n^2\over a_n^2}+a_n^2)\  \sin \theta_n\\
    -\sin \theta_n /a_n^2 & \cos \theta_n + {s_n \over a_n^2} \sin \theta_n
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 &0 \\ 0&-1 \end{pmatrix}. 
\]

Thus, we have $H= \tilde {N^+}$, that is $g_nKg_n^{-1}\rightarrow \tilde {N^+}$.\\\\


\end{proof}

\subsection{Limits of maximal diagonalizable subgroups}

Now we study limit points of the set of maximal diagonalizable subgroups of $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$.
We consider the conjugacy limits $(g_n,v_n)A(g_n,v_n)^{-1}$ of $A$ for different types of $g_n$ and $v_n$. 

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{limit-D}] 
Let $A= \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} a &0 \\ 0 &1/a \end{pmatrix} \mid a\in \R^+ \right\}$ and $H$ be a limit point of 
$\{ (g, v) A (g,v)^{-1} \mid g\in SL(2, \R), ~ v\in \R ^2 \}$. So, there are $g_n \in SL(2, \R)$ and $v_n \in \R ^2$ such that $(g_n, v_n) A (g_n,v_n)^{-1} \to H$. Using Iwasawa decomposition, we may assume that 
$g_n\in N^+$.  Let $g_n = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & s_n \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $v_n = (\alpha _n , \beta _n)$.  
Suppose $(g, v) \in H$. Then, there are $a_n \in \R ^+$ such that $(g_n, v_n) \mathfrak{a}_n(g_n,v_n)^{-1} \to (g, v)$, where $\mathfrak{a}_n = \begin{pmatrix} a_n &0 \\ 0 &1/a_n \end{pmatrix}$.  This implies that 
\[
\begin{pmatrix} a_n & ( {1 \over a_n}-a_n)s_n \\ 0 & {1 \over a_n} \end{pmatrix} \to g, ~~{\rm and} ~~
\]

\[
((1-a_n ) \alpha _n + (a_n - {1 \over a_n})s_n \beta _n, (1-{1 \over a_n})\beta _n ) \to v.
\]
By passing to subsequences and taking conjugates, we may assume that $s_n=0$ or $s_n \to \infty$, $\alpha _n=0$ or 
$\alpha _n \to \infty$ and $\beta _n=0$ or $\beta _n \to \infty$.  

We first consider the case that $s_n\to \infty$.  
If both $\alpha _n=0$ and $\beta _n=0$, then it is easy to see that 
$H = N^+=\left\{ \begin{pmatrix} 1 & t \\ 0&1 \end{pmatrix} \mid t\in \R \right\}$.  So assume that either $\alpha _n \to \infty$ or $\beta _n \to \infty$.  
Then $v_n\to \infty$.  This implies by Lemma \ref{el1} that $g$ is unipotent.  Therefore, $a_n \to 1$. 


Since $g_n \in N^+$, and $B =AN^+$, it follows that $(g_n, v_n)A(g_n,v_n)^{-1} $ are closed subgroups of $B\ltimes \R ^2$.  Since $B\ltimes \R ^2$ is isomorphic to a group of upper triangular matrices with positive diagonal entries, $B\ltimes \R ^2$ is an exponential Lie group. Hence, by Proposition \ref{cs}, $H$ is a connected one-dimensional subgroup of $N^+\ltimes \R ^2$ which is the $3$-dimensional Heisenberg group.  
Therefore, 
\[
H= V_{(a,b,c)}= \left\{ \left ( \begin{pmatrix} 1 & at \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} ,\left( ct+{abt^2\over 2}, bt \right) \right) \mid t\in \R \right\} 
\]
for some 
$a, b, c \in \R$.  Then for each $t\in \R$, there are $b_n$ (depending on $t$) such that 
$$b_n \to 1, ~~ ({1\over b_n} -b_n) s_n \to at ~~{\rm and}~~ (1-{1\over b_n})\beta _n \to bt .$$

\noindent {\bf Case $1$:} Suppose ${s_n \over \beta _n} \to r \in \R$.  Then $a=-2br$ and hence 
$$(1-b_n)\alpha _n - {(1+b_n)\over b_n} (1-b_n)s_n \beta _n \to ct-rb^2t^2 .$$  If $b=0$, then $H= V_0$.  
So, we may assume that $b\not = 0$.  
Also $$2(1-b_n)s_n \beta _n  -{(1+b_n)\over b_n} (1-b_n )s_n \beta _n \to -rb^2 t^2.$$  Therefore, 
$$(1-b_n) \alpha _n -2(1-b_n)s_n \beta _n \to ct .$$  This implies that $2s_n -{\alpha _n \over \beta _n} \to {c\over b}$.  
Let $d= \lim 2s_n -{\alpha _n \over \beta _n}$.  Then $c= db$.  Thus, $H= V_{(r_1b, r_2b, b)}$ for some $r_1, r_2 \in \R$.  

\noindent{\bf Case $2$:} Suppose ${\beta _n \over s_n }\to p \in \R$,  Then $b=- {pa \over 2}$ and hence 
$$(1-b_n)\alpha _n - {(1+b_n)\over b_n} (1-b_n)s_n \beta _n \to ct-{pa^2t^2 \over 4} .$$  
If $a=0$, then $H = V_0$.  So, we may assume that $a\not =0$.  Thus, $$(1-b_n) \alpha _n - 2(1-b_n) s_n \beta _n \to ct.$$  
This implies that ${\alpha _n \over s_n} -2\beta _n \to 2{c\over a}$, hence $c= {da \over 2}$ where 
$d= \lim {\alpha _n \over s_n} - 2 \beta _n$.  Thus, 
$H= V_{(a, r_1a, r_2a)}$ for some $r_1, r_2 \in \R$.  


We now consider the case $s_n=0$.  Then $g= I$ and $$((1-a_n ) \alpha _n, (1-{1 \over a_n})\beta _n ) \to v$$ 
for some $a_n \to 1$. 


\noindent{\bf Case $3$:} Suppose ${\beta _n \over \alpha _n} \to 0$.  Then since $v_n = (\alpha _n , \beta _n ) \to \infty$, 
$\alpha _n \to \infty$ and 
$(1-{1\over a_n})\beta _n = {-1\over a_n}(1-a_n)\alpha _n {\beta _n\over \alpha _n}\to 0$.  Therefore 
$H\subset V_0$.  For any $t\in \R$, taking $b_n = 1-{t\over \alpha _n}$, we get that $b_n \to 1$, $(1-b_n)\alpha _n =t$.  
Therefore, $((1-b_n ) \alpha _n, (1-{1 \over b_n})\beta _n )\to (t,0)$.  Thus, $H=V_0$.  

\noindent{\bf Case $4$:} Suppose ${\alpha _n \over \beta _n} \to 0$.  Then 
$(1-a_n)\alpha _n = -a_n(1-{1\over a_n})\beta _n {\alpha _n \over \beta _n}\to 0$.  Therefore, 
$H\subset V_\infty = \{ (0, t) \mid t\in \R \}$.  For any $t\in \R$, take 
$b_n = {\beta _n \over \beta _n -t}$.  Then $b_n \to 1$ and $((1-b_n ) \alpha _n, (1-{1 \over b_n})\beta _n )\to (0,t)$.  Thus, $H= V_\infty$.

\noindent{\bf Case $5$:} Suppose ${\beta _n \over \alpha _n }\to c \not = 0$.  
Let $x,y \in \R$ be such that $v=(x,y)$.  Then 
$(1-a_n ) \alpha _n \to x$
and $(1-{1 \over a_n})\beta _n \to y$.  
Since $(1-{1 \over a_n})\beta _n = {-1\over a_n} (1-a_n ) \alpha _n  {\beta _n \over \alpha _n}$, we get that 
$y=-cx$.  Therefore $H\subset V_r= \{ (t, rt ) \mid t\in \R \}$ for $r=-c$.  

For $t\in \R$, let $b_n = 1-{t\over \alpha _n}$.  Then $b_n \to 1$ and $(1-b_n)\alpha _n =t$.  
Also, $(1-{1\over b_n})\beta _n = {-t \over \alpha _n -t }\beta _n \to -tc$.  Therefore 
$V_r \subset H$.  Thus, $H= V_r$.

\end{proof}

\vspace{0.2 cm}


\smallskip

\subsection{Limits of unipotent and Borel subgroups}

We now find the conjugacy limits of the Borel subgroup 
\[
B:=\left\{
\left(\begin{pmatrix} a&s\\ 0&1/a\end{pmatrix}:\ a,s\in\R\right)
\right\}
\]
of $\SL(2,\R)$ inside $G=\SL(2,\R)\ltimes \R^2$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{limits-B}]
Clearly, a conjugacy limit of $B$, when conjugated by elements of the type $(g,0)$ with $g\in\SL(2,\R)$, is $B$ itself or a conjugate of $B$ by elements of $K$. For conjugation by more general type of elements, it is enough to consider conjugation by elements of the form $(I,v)$ with $v\in\R^2$. So, let   $\mathfrak{g}_n=(I,v_n)$ and $\mathfrak{b}_n=\begin{pmatrix}a_n&s_n\\0&1/a_n\end{pmatrix}$. Then a simple calculation shows that
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak{g}_n\mathfrak{b}_n\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}=\left(\begin{pmatrix}a_n&s_n\\0&1/a_n\end{pmatrix},\Big((1-a_n)\alpha_n -s_n\beta_n ,\ (1-1/a_n)\beta_n\Big)\right)\\ =\big(\mathfrak{b}_n,(x_n,y_n)\big) ,\  (\text{say})
\end{align*} where $v_n = (\alpha _n , \beta _n)$.
Assume that $v_n \to \infty$ and $\mathfrak{g}_n\mathfrak{b}_n\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}\to (g, v)$.  Then by Lemma \ref{el1}, $g$ is unipotent, hence $a_n\rightarrow 1$ necessarily. 
We shall consider the following cases.

\noindent {\bf Case $1$:} Suppose $\frac{\alpha_n}{\beta_n}\rightarrow 0$. Then $\beta _n \to \infty$.  
If $s_n\rightarrow s$ for some non-zero $s$, then it is easy to see that $x_n$ cannot converge. Hence, $s_n\rightarrow 0$. Given $x,y\in \R$, choose $s_n\rightarrow 0$ and $a_n\rightarrow 1$ in such a way that $-\beta_n s_n \rightarrow x$ and $(1-1/a_n)\beta_n\rightarrow y$.  Then 
$(1-a_n)\alpha _n = -a_n (1-{1\over a_n})\beta _n {\alpha _n \over \beta _n} \to 0$, hence 
$\mathfrak{g}_n \mathfrak{b}_n ' \mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}\rightarrow \big(I,(x,y) \big)$ for suitable $\mathfrak{b}_n '\in B$.  This shows that, in this case, the limit subgroup is $\R^2$.  

\noindent{\bf Case $2$:} Suppose $\frac{\beta_n}{\alpha_n}\rightarrow  0$. Then $\alpha _n \to \infty$.  
Let $x\in \R$.  Then for $\tilde {\mathfrak{b} }_n = \begin{pmatrix} 1- {x\over \alpha _n} & 0 \\ 0 & {\alpha _n \over \alpha _n -x} \end{pmatrix}$, we get that $\mathfrak{g}_n\tilde {\mathfrak{b}_n} \mathfrak{g}_n^{-1} \to (I, (t, 0))$.  
Thus, $\{ (I, (t,0))\mid t \in \R \} \subset H$.  

If $\beta _n$ converges, then $y_n =(1-{1\over a_n})\beta _n\to 0$ and it can be shown that $H=N^+\ltimes \{(t, 0) \mid t\in \R \}$.  So, we may assume that $\beta _n \to \infty$.  Since $\mathfrak{b}_n$ converges, $s_n$ converges, say to $s$.  Since $\beta _n \to \infty$ and $x_n$ converges, we get that $(1-a_n) {\alpha _n \over \beta _n} \to  s $.

If ${\beta _n ^2 \over \alpha _n} \to c \in \R$, then $y_n ={-1\over a_n} (1-a_n) {\alpha _n \over \beta _n } {\beta _n ^2 \over \alpha _n} \to -sc$.  Therefore, by Proposition \ref{cs} we get that 
$H= \{ (\mathfrak{s}, (t,-cs) \mid t, s \in \R \}$ where $\mathfrak{s} = \begin{pmatrix} 1& s \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$.  

If ${\beta _n ^2 \over \alpha _n}\to \infty$, then $(1-a_n) {\alpha _n \over \beta _n} = (1-{1\over a_n})\beta _n 
(-a_n) {\alpha _n \over \beta _n ^2}\to 0$.  Therefore, by Proposition \ref{cs} we get that 
$H= \R ^2 = \{ (I, (t,r) \mid t, r \in \R \}$. 



\noindent{\bf Case $3$}: Suppose $\frac{\alpha_n}{\beta_n}
\rightarrow c$ for some non-zero real number $c$. Note that we are necessarily assuming both $|\alpha_n|$ and $|\beta_n|$ are unbounded. Otherwise, the limit subgroup will be a conjugate of $B$. Now, suppose $x_n\rightarrow x$. Then $\frac{x_n}{\beta_n}\rightarrow 0$. But, 
\[
\frac{x_n}{\beta_n}=\frac{(1-a_n)\alpha_n}{\beta_n}-s_n
\]
which implies that $s_n\rightarrow 0$ in this case as well.  Therefore $H\subset \R ^2$.  Thus, by Proposition \ref{cs}, $H$ is of dimension $2$ and hence $H= \R ^2$.  


\end{proof}
We next determine the conjugacy limits of the unipotent subgroup $N^+$.

\begin{proof}{Proof of Theorem \ref{limits-U}}
Since $N^+$ is normal in $B$, using Iwasawa decomposition in $SL(2,\R)$, it follows that whenever $g_n\in\SL(2,\R)$, the limit of $g_nN^+g_n^{-1}$ is either $N^+$ or its conjugates by elements of $K$. For conjugation by other elements of $G$, without loss of generality, we may consider conjugation by elements of the form $\mathfrak{g}_n=(k_n,v_n)$ with $k_n\in K$ and $v_n=(\alpha_n,\beta_n)\in\R^2$. Also, it is easy to see that if $L$ is a closed subgroup of $G$, $g_nLg_n^{-1}\rightarrow H$ and $k_n\rightarrow k$, then $k_ng_nLg_n^{-1}k_n^{-1}\rightarrow kHk^{-1}$. In view of this, it is enough to consider conjugation of $N^+$ by elements of the form $\mathfrak{g}_n=(I,v_n)$. 

Now, suppose $\mathfrak{g}_n=\big(I,v_n\big)$ with $v_n=(\alpha_n,\beta_n)$. Then for any $\mathfrak{s}_n=\begin{pmatrix}
    1&s_n\\0&1
\end{pmatrix}$, 
\[
\mathfrak{g}_n\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}=\left(\begin{pmatrix}
    1&s_n\\0&1
\end{pmatrix},\big(-s_n\beta_n,0\big)\right).
\]
 If $|\beta_n|\rightarrow\infty$, then choosing $s_n\rightarrow 0$ suitably $\big(I,(t,0)\big)$ can be realized as a limit of $\mathfrak{g}_n\mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}$ for any real number $t$.  Also, if $\mathfrak{g}_n \mathfrak{s}_n\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}$ converges, then 
 $s_n$ as well as $s_n\beta _n$ converges. but, as $|\beta _n| \to \infty$, we have $s_n \to 0$.  This shows that, in this case, the limit of $\mathfrak{g}_nN^+\mathfrak{g}_n^{-1}$ is $V_0$. 

\end{proof}
