% We are motivated by the effectiveness of flow matching policies in robotics control, where they have strong ability at learning complex, continuous policies from expert demonstrations by mapping high-dimensional inputs to sophisticated, sequential actions.










% We aim to train a flow matching neural network to learn the conditional probability path or vector field for mapping a simple prior distribution (Gaussian noise) to an optimal sequence of HFT trading decisions, conditioned on real-time market observations
% ~\cite{frans2024one}. During training (Algorithm \ref{alg:shortcut_training}), the model samples pairs of noisy starting action sequences and corresponding expert action sequences for a given market state. It then creates intermediate, partially noisy action sequences by interpolating between the random start and the expert target. The neural network is trained to predict the "velocity" in the action space needed to move from these intermediate sequences towards the expert target, using targets derived from direct flow matching (connecting noise to expert) and a self-consistency mechanism (ensuring smooth transitions along the learned path). This training minimizes the difference between the network's predicted velocity and the target velocity, effectively teaching it the flow that maps noise to optimal HFT actions conditioned on the market state. 



