	\title{Consistency and Inconsistency in $k$-Means Clustering}


    \author[1]{Mo\"ise Blanchard}
    \author[2]{Adam Quinn Jaffe}
    \author[3]{Nikita Zhivotovskiy}

    \affil[1]{Data Science Institute, Columbia University, New York, NY}
    \affil[2]{Department of Statistics, Columbia University, New York, NY}
    \affil[3]{Department of Statistics, UC Berkeley, Berkeley, CA}


	\date{\today}

	\maketitle

        \begin{abstract}
            A celebrated result of Pollard proves asymptotic consistency for $k$-means clustering when the population distribution has finite variance.
            In this work, we point out that the population-level $k$-means clustering problem is, in fact, well-posed under the weaker assumption of a finite expectation, and we investigate whether some form of asymptotic consistency holds in this setting.
            As we illustrate in a variety of negative results, the complete story is quite subtle; for example, the empirical $k$-means cluster centers may fail to converge even if there exists a unique set of population $k$-means cluster centers.
            A detailed analysis of our negative results reveals that inconsistency arises because of an extreme form of cluster imbalance, whereby the presence of outlying samples leads to some empirical $k$-means clusters possessing very few points.
            We then give a collection of positive results which show that some forms of asymptotic consistency, under only the assumption of finite expectation, may be recovered by imposing some a priori degree of balance among the empirical $k$-means clusters.
        \end{abstract}

    %\setcounter{tocdepth}{1}
	%\tableofcontents

	\section{Introduction}

A celebrated result of Pollard \cite{Pollard} states that the empirical $k$-means cluster centers of independent, identically-distributed (i.i.d.) samples of a random variable $X$  converge almost surely to the population $k$-means cluster centers under the assumption of finite variance $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$.
At the same time, the strong law of large numbers (SLLN) due to Kolmogorov states that the empirical mean of i.i.d. samples converges almost surely to the population mean under the minimal assumption of finite expectation $\mathbb{E}\|X\| < \infty$, and it is easy to see that empirical- and population-level $k$-means clustering reduce to the empirical mean and the population mean, respectively, when $k=1$.
The goal of this paper is to investigate this conspicuous gap in the required moment conditions.
In other words, is $k$-means clustering consistent for all $k\ge 1$ under the assumption $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$?

\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{clustering_plot.pdf}
	\caption{Inconsistency for $k$-means clustering applied to a simulated data set, for $k=2$.
		The samples come from a distribution $X$ which is symmetric and satisfies $\Pbb(|X|\ge t) = t^{-2}$ for all $t \ge 1$ (left).
		The empirical $k$-means cluster centers are not convergent (right).}
	\label{fig:two_sided_pareto}
\end{figure}

Before getting into the details, let us consider the simplest possible version of this problem.
That is, we consider $k$-means clustering in $\Rbb^d$ when $k=2$ and $d=1$, and we suppose that $X$ is symmetric and satisfies $\Pbb(|X|\ge t) = t^{-2}$ for all $t \ge 1$.
(In other words, $|X|$ has a \textit{Pareto distribution with exponent 2}, denoted $\Pareto(2)$.)
Importantly, note that $X$ satisfies $\Ebb|X|^2 = \infty$ but $\Ebb|X|^p<\infty$ for all $1\le p < 2$, so it just barely fails to have finite variance.
In Figure~\ref{fig:two_sided_pareto} we show a simulation of this data and the optimal cluster centers for $n=1,\ldots, 3000$ samples, and we observe some large jumps of the empirical cluster centers.
As we rigorously prove later, $X$ admits a unique set of population $k$-means cluster centers given by $\{-2,2\}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}) but the empirical $k$-means cluster centers are infinitely often of the form $\{-\sqrt{n},0\}$ or $\{0,\sqrt{n}\}$ up to logarithmic factors (Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}).
This example shows that $k$-means clustering can be inconsistent even when all population-level objects are well-defined.

Now we introduce some notation.
Recall that for i.i.d. samples $X_1,\ldots, X_n$ in $\Rbb^d$ from the same distribution as $X$, the \textit{empirical $k$-means clustering problem} is
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:def-emp-k-means}
	\begin{cases}
		\textnormal{minimize} & \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X_i\|^2 \\
		\textnormal{over} & \pointset\subseteq \Rbb^d \\
		\textnormal{with} & 1 \le \#\pointset \le k,
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
any solution of which is called a \textit{set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers}.
When $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$, it is natural to expect that \eqref{eqn:def-emp-k-means} should converge (in some suitable sense) to the \textit{population $k$-means clustering problem} defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:def-pop-k-means}
	\begin{cases}
		\textnormal{minimize} & \Ebb\left[\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2\right] \\
		\textnormal{over} & \pointset\subseteq \Rbb^d \\
		\textnormal{with} & 1 \le \#\pointset \le k,
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
any solution of which is called a \textit{set of population $k$-means cluster centers}.
The value of \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means} at $\pointset$ is called its \textit{distortion}, denoted $D_X(\pointset)$.

\subsection{Problem Statement}

While it may seem that $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$ is necessary in order to define the problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means}, it turns out that there is a canonical extension to the case where we have $\Ebb\|X\|^2=\infty$ but $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.  
That is, we may consider the ``renormalized'' problem
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal}
	\begin{cases}
		\textnormal{minimize} & \Ebb\left[\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2-\|X\|^2\right] \\
		\textnormal{over} & \pointset\subseteq \Rbb^d \\
		\textnormal{with} & 1 \le \#\pointset \le k,
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and we say that any of its solution are a set of population $k$-means cluster centers.
Also, the value of \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} at $\pointset$ is called its \textit{excess distortion} relative to the set $\{0\}$, denoted $D_X(\pointset\,|\,\{0\})$.
(We will later see that one can similarly define excess distortion relative to other sets.)
Two remarks help to explain why this definition is indeed canonical.
First, note that problems~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means} and \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} are equivalent whenever $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$, and that \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} is well-defined whenever $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.
Second, note that problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} captures, in the $k=1$ case, the notion that $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ suffices to define $\Ebb[X]$, whereas  problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means} does not.
(Note that such renormalization is not required for the empirical $k$-means clustering problem \eqref{eqn:def-emp-k-means}.)
We note that reductions from distortion to excess distortion have already appeared in the finite-sample analyses of the papers \cite{BiauDevroyeLugosi,Zhivotovskiy}, but neither of these works covers our regime of interest, namely $\Ebb\|X\|^2=\infty$.


The excess distortion $D_X(\pointset\,|\,\{0\})$ being well-defined does not necessarily guarantee that the problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} admits a solution, and we indeed show that solutions may not exist.
Even more delicately, we show that a wide range of population-level behaviors can occur (Section~\ref{sec:cases}).
That is, we show that there exist distributions of a random variable $X$ such that
\begin{itemize}
	\item[(i)] problem \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has a solution hence finite optimal excess distortion (Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}),
	\item[(ii)] problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has a finite optimal excess distortion but no solution (Proposition~\ref{prop:1-sided-pareto-pop}), and
	\item[(iii)] problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has infinite optimal excess distortion hence no solution (Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj}).
\end{itemize}
We emphasize that the landscape of these cases is only visible when $k\ge 2$ and $\Ebb\|X\|^2 = \infty$; if $k= 1$ or $\Ebb\|X\|^2 < \infty$, then Kolmogorov's SLLN and Pollard's result in \cite{Pollard} imply that case (i) always obtains and that strong convergence of the empirical cluster centers to the population cluster centers always holds.

As we will illustrate in a variety of negative results below, various notions of consistency for the empirical $k$-means problem may fail even when the requisite population-level objects are well-defined.
For an example in case (i), we have already seen Figure~\ref{fig:two_sided_pareto} which shows that convergence of the empirical $k$-means cluster centers may fail even when the population $k$-means cluster centers are well-defined.
A careful analysis of this example and related examples reveals the exact mechanism by which this inconsistency occurs.
Namely, for heavy-tailed distributions $X$, there is a non-negligible probability that the largest value among $X_1,\ldots, X_n$ is so much larger than the other values that it must be placed into a cluster alone.
This can be observed directly in Figure~\ref{fig:two_sided_pareto}, where jumps in either cluster center are due to extreme values of samples.

Because inconsistency in $k$-means clustering occurs due to cluster imbalance, it is natural to consider an alternative procedure in which we impose some a priori constraint on the balance of the empirical clusters.
To precisely introduce such a procedure, recall \cite[Chapter~9.1]{Bishop} that problem \eqref{eqn:def-emp-k-means} is an optimization over \textit{cluster centers} but it may be equivalently stated as an optimization over \textit{clusters} themselves; mathematically, we regard the set of clusters as the \textit{Voronoi partition} determined by the cluster centers, i.e., the partition of $\Rbb^d$ into finitely-many regions determined by which cluster centers is the closest.
Then, for some integer $0\le \gamma\le n$, we consider the empirical clustering problem
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:def-emp-k-means-balance}
	\begin{cases}
		\textnormal{minimize} & \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\sum_{X_i\in\Vcal_{\ell}}\|\bar m_n(\Vcal_{\ell})-X_i\|^2 \\
		\textnormal{over} & \textnormal{Voronoi partitions } \Vcal_1,\ldots, \Vcal_k \textnormal{ of } \Rbb^d \\
		\textnormal{with} & \#\{1\le i \le n: X_i\in\Vcal_{\ell}\} \ge \gamma \textnormal{ for all } 1 \le \ell \le k,
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\bar m_n(\Vcal):=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}/\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}$ is the conditional sample mean on a measurable region $\Vcal\subseteq\Rbb^d$.
Then, we are interested in the convergence of the set of points $\{\bar m_n(\Vcal_1),\ldots, \bar m_n(\Vcal_k)\}$, in a suitable sense, when $\gamma$ grows with $n$ at some carefully-chosen rate.
(There are some nuances to this choice of balanced clustering problem, for example that $\{\Vcal_1,\ldots, \Vcal_k\}$ is typically not equivalent to Voronoi partition of the points $\{\bar m_n(\Vcal_1),\ldots, \bar m_n(\Vcal_k)\}$ unless $\gamma \neq 0$. See Subsection~\ref{subsec:balance} for further detail.)

\subsection{Main Results}

Now we may state our positive and negative results concerning the various desired notions of convergence in various cases of interest.

We consider convergence of the empirical cluster centers in the Hausdorff metric (Section~\ref{sec:centers}).
First, we show that the empirical cluster centers may fail to converge even when the population cluster centers exist and are unique (Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}).
Second, we show that imposing a linear balance constraint, that is $\gamma_n = \alpha n$ for some suitable $0<\alpha<1$ depending on the distribution of $X$, suffices for the convergence of the balanced empirical $k$-means cluster centers to the (unconstrained) population $k$-means cluster center (Theorem~\ref{thm:cluster-consistency}).
The choice of $\alpha$ may be viewed as a form of well-specification, and it is typically unknown in practice.

Similarly, we have positive and negative results concerning the convergence of the excess distortion of the empirical cluster cluster centers (Section~\ref{sec:distortion}).
First, we show that the excess distortion of the empirical cluster centers may fail to converge to the excess distortion of the population cluster centers (Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided_imbalanced}).
Second, we show that imposing a polylogarithmic balance constraint, that is $\gamma_n\ge (\log n)^4$, suffices for the convergence of the excess distortion of the balanced empirical $k$-means cluster centers to the optimal (unconstrained) excess distortion (Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_log_points_per_cluster}).

Let us now summarize our main results thus far.
For cases (i) and (ii), we give Table~\ref{tab:summary} which summarizes the form of consistency that is desired, the counterexample showing that such consistency can fail, and the balance constraint that leads to an analogous positive result.
For case (iii) we do not know of a meaningful form of consistency that may be desired, since essentially no interesting population-level object is well-defined; one may desire a set of empirical cluster centers whose (population) excess risk diverges, but we show by example that this notion is actually quite weak (Proposition~\ref{prop:naive_cluster_estimator}).
We also show that there is an intermediate form of consistency in case (i) when polylogarithmic balance is imposed (Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_unified}), whereby we have convergence of the subset of non-diverging cluster centers, and limiting set of points is an optimal set of $k'$-means cluster centers, for $1\le k'\le k$.


