\subsection{Related Literature}

In order to situate our results in the context of related literature, we briefly summarize some relevant literature on $k$-means clustering and its variants.
In addition to the seminal work by Pollard on strong consistency \cite{Pollard}, the statistical theory of $k$-means clustering has seen many recent developments.
For example, strong consistency is known for $k$-means clustering in a variety of geometric settings \cite{Parna1, Parna2, Lember, Thorpe, Laloe, JaffeClustering} and under a variety of adaptive modifications \cite{JiangAriasCastro, JaffeClustering}, but all results rely on some condition at least as strong as finite variance.
For another example, Pollard also proved a central limit theorem for the empirical $k$-means cluster centers \cite{PollardCLT}, and this also relies on the condition of finite variance.
There are also various finite-sample rates of convergence known for $k$-means clustering (typically for the distortion rather than the cluster centers); these all rely on stronger conditions that finite variance \cite{BartlettLinderLugosi, BiauDevroyeLugosi, Levrard, Fefferman, appert2021new} with the exception of \cite{Zhivotovskiy}.

Somewhat related to the present work is existing literature concerning robustness properties of $k$-means clustering and its variants.
For example, in \cite[Section~2]{TrimmedKMeansII} it is shown that $k$-means clustering lacks all of the usual notions of robustness from classical robust statistics.
A primary approach to overcome these difficulties is to employ \textit{$\alpha$-trimmed $k$-means} \cite{TrimmedKMeansI} for some $0<\alpha<1$, which amounts to minimizing the $k$-means clustering objective while omitting up to proportion $\alpha$ of the data points; much is known about trimmed $k$-means clustering, although it is still an active area of research \cite{RobustTrimmed, SoftTrimming, RobustBregman, WassersteinTrimmed, TrimmedCLT}.
Other robust variants of $k$-means clustering have been proposed, for example model-based methods.
\cite{GallegosRitter}; see \cite{RobustClusteringSurvey} for a survey of statistical and computational aspects of robust clustering.

One of our fundamental observation is that cluster imbalance leads to statistical problems, and this has indeed been observed in previous literature.
For example, the works \cite{Ng, CuturiDoucet, GenevayDulacArnoldVert} argue that cluster balance is desirable from a practical point of view, and they discuss computational aspects of implementing such balanced clustering procedures.
For another example, the recent work \cite{Zhivotovskiy} identifies (see \cite[Theorem~3.1 and Theorem 3.10]{Zhivotovskiy}) that it is precisely cluster imbalance that controls the minimax rate of convergence of the distortion, when finite variance is assumed.
Our results contribute to this story by showing that extreme cluster imbalance can even lead to inconsistency.

Our various positive and negative results suggest an interesting open problem on identifying the sharpest possible balance constraint $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ leading to asymptotic consistency in balanced $k$-means clustering.
In case (i) we have seen that $\gamma_n = \alpha n$ is sufficient for certain $0<\alpha<1$, and in case (ii) we have seen that $\gamma_n=(\log n)^4$ is sufficient.
However it is not clear whether either of these rates is sharp.
It would be interesting to try to identify the sharpest possible balance constraint for each case, and also to address the analogous questions for convergence in probability rather than convergence almost surely.

\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

In this section we establish our notation and give some preliminaries that will be used in the main results.


\paragraph{Notation.} Throughout the paper, we fix $d\in\Nbb$ and for $k\in\Nbb$ we write $\allpointsets_k:=\{\pointset\subseteq \R^d:1\le\#\pointset \le k\}$ for the collection of all non-empty sets of at most $k$ points in $\R^d$.
We write $(\Omega,\F,\P)$ for a probability space, with expectation $\E$, supporting an i.i.d. sequence of random variables $X,X_1,X_2,\ldots$ taking values in $\R^d$.
For $p\ge 1$, we write $W_p$ for the $p$-Wasserstein distance between probability measures on $\Rbb^d$; we refer the reader to \cite{Villani} for further detail, but we emphasize \cite[Definition~6.8]{Villani} that convergence in $W_p$ is equivalent to weak convergence plus convergence of the $p$th moment.
We write $\kl$ for the Kullback-Liebler divergence (i.e., relative entropy) between two probability measures, and by a slight abuse of notation we also write $\kl(x\,\|\,y):=x \log (x/y) + (1-x)\log((1-x)/(1-y))$ for the relative entropy between Bernoulli distributions with parameters $0\le x,y\le 1$.
We use standard notation for asymptotic comparisons of sequences, e.g. $a_n = O(b_n)$ to mean $\limsup_{n\to\infty}a_n/b_n < \infty$ and $a_n\ll b_n$ to mean $\lim_{n\to\infty}a_n/b_n = 0$.

\subsection{Clustering Definitions}\label{subsec:basic}

We begin with some precise notions that will allow us to describe the population- and empirical-level $k$-means clustering problems under the minimal moment assumption of $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.
For instance, we have the following elementary observation which follows from some casework; the proof is hence omitted for the sake of brevity:

\begin{lemma}
	If $X$ satisfies $\E\|X\|<\infty$, then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\E\left|\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-X\|^2\right|<\infty
	\end{equation*}
	for any $\pointset,\pointset'\in \allpointsets_k$. 
\end{lemma}

The preceding integrability trick is known in probability theory as \textit{renormalization}; although the tails of the random variable $\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2$ are too heavy to be integrable under the condition $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$, the random variable of the difference $\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2 - \min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-X\|^2$ experiences a great deal of cancellation and hence its tails are light enough to be integrable under the condition $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.
See \cite{SchoetzSLLN, FrechetMeanInfDim} for further explanation of the renormalization trick in the context of Fr\'echet means, which corresponds to $k$-means clustering with $k=1$ in a general metric space. 

The preceding result allows us to make sense of the following.

\begin{definition}\label{def:k-means-pop}
	Suppose that $X$ satisfies $\E\|X\|<\infty$.
	The \textit{(population) excess distortion} of $\pointset$ over $\pointset'$ is defined to be
	\begin{equation*}
		D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset'):=\E\left[\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-X\|^2\right].
	\end{equation*}
	A set $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$ is called a \textit{set of population $k$-means cluster centers} if it satisfies $D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset')\le 0$ for all $\pointset'\in\allpointsets_k$.
	We write $\allpointsets_k(X)$ for the collection of all sets of population $k$-means cluster centers.
\end{definition}

We can also formulate a definition similar to equation \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means} under the assumption of $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.
Namely, for any fixed $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$, it turns out that $\allpointsets_k(X)$ is the equal to the set of solutions to the optimization problem
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:extended-def}
	\begin{cases}
		\textnormal{minimize} &\E\left[\min_{c\in \pointset}\|c-X\|^2-\min_{c_0\in\pointset_0}\|c_0-X\|^2\right] \\
		\textnormal{over} &\pointset\in\allpointsets_k.
	\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Interestingly, note that the resultant solution set does not depend on the choice of $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$, so we may simply take $\pointset_0 = \{0\}$.
This can be easily proven from the elementary identity $D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset'') = D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset') + D_X(\pointset'\,|\,\pointset'')$ for all $\pointset, \pointset', \pointset''\in\allpointsets_k$.

We now define these notions at empirical level, as follows.
\begin{definition}\label{def:k-means-emp}
	The \textit{empirical excess distortion} of $\pointset$ over $\pointset'$ is defined to be
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar D_{n}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset'):=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-X_i\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-X_i\|^2\right).
	\end{equation*}
	A set $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$ is called a \textit{set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers} if it satisfies $\bar D_n(\pointset\,|\,\pointset')\le 0$ for all $\pointset'\in\allpointsets_k$.
	We write $\bar \allpointsets_{n,k}$ for the collection of all sets of empirical $k$-means cluster centers.
\end{definition}

The definition above is equivalent to \eqref{eqn:def-emp-k-means}, and we emphasize no renormalization is needed.
However, we choose to formulate the problem in terms of empirical excess distortion because the parallel with (population) excess distortion will be useful in our later proofs.

Note that there is a slight abuse of notation in the preceding definitions.
That is, $D_X$ depends not on the random variable $X$ but rather on its distribution.
Similarly, $\bar D_n$ depends only on the empirical measure $\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\delta_{X_i}$ of $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.
Later we will see that there is some analytical advantage to expressing the excess distortion in terms of an arbitrary probability measure. (See Lemma~\ref{lem:D-cts}.)

\subsection{Voronoi Regions}

Next we describe a following geometric aspect of $k$-means clustering which features in our results.

\begin{definition}
	Fix any $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$.
	For $c\in\pointset$, the \textit{open Voronoi region of $c$ in $\pointset$} is the set
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathcal{V}_{\pointset}^{\circ}(c) :=\{x\in\R^d: \|c-x\|< \|c'-x\| \textnormal{ for all }c'\in\pointset\setminus\{c\}\},
	\end{equation*}
	consisting of all points in $\R^d$ which are (strictly) closer to $c\in\pointset$ than to any other point of $\pointset$.
	A \textit{Voronoi partition for $\pointset$} is any partition $\{\mathcal{V}_{\pointset}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ of $\R^d$ into measurable sets such that we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathcal{V}_{\pointset}(c) \supseteq\mathcal{V}^{\circ}_{\pointset}(c)
	\end{equation*}
	for all $c\in\pointset$.
	In words, each $\mathcal{V}_{\pointset}(c)$ contains the set of points in $\R^d$ which are (strictly) closer to $c\in\pointset$ than to any other point of $\pointset$, and ties may be broken in an arbitrary (but measurable) way.
\end{definition}

The interpretation is that sets of points represent \textit{cluster centers} while Voronoi regions correspond to \textit{clusters} themselves. 
Also, we emphasize that the collection of open Voronoi regions $\{\Vcal_{\pointset}^{\circ}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ (which is not a partition, since it does not cover $\Rbb^d$) is determined uniquely by $\pointset$, but that there are typically many possible Voronoi partitions $\{\Vcal_{\pointset}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ corresponding to $\pointset$ since there is ambiguity about the boundary points.
However, this is not a problem in most cases:
It is well-known (see \cite[Theorem~1.5]{GrafLuschgy}) that the boundary of each Voronoi region has zero Lebesgue measure, and (see \cite[Theorem~4.2]{GrafLuschgy}) that the boundary of each Voronoi region has probability zero under $X$ whenever $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k(X)$.

Next we introduce some notation for the conditional mean of $X$ given a Voronoi partition.
In the following, when we write \textit{a Voronoi partition} $\mathscr{V} := \{\Vcal_{\ell}\}_{1\le\ell\le k}$ we mean a Voronoi partition corresponding to \textit{some} set of cluster centers, i.e., $\{\Vcal_{\ell}\}_{1\le\ell\le k} = \{\Vcal_{\pointset}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ for some $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$.

\begin{definition}
	If $X$ satisfies $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ then for any measurable set $\Vcal\subseteq\Rbb^d$, we write
	\begin{equation*}
		m_{X}(\Vcal) :=\Ebb[X\,|\,X\in\Vcal]
	\end{equation*}
	and for any Voronoi partition $\mathscr{V}=\{\Vcal_{\ell}\}_{1\le \ell \le k},$ we write
	\begin{equation*}
		m_X(\mathscr{V}) := \{m_X(\Vcal_{\ell})\}_{1\le \ell \le k},
	\end{equation*}
	which is an element of $\allpointsets_k$.
	We also write
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar m_{n}(\Vcal) :=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}}
	\end{equation*}
	and $\bar m_n(\mathscr{V})$ for the corresponding conditional means with respect to the empirical distribution of $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.
\end{definition}

The preceding definition does not make sense if $\Vcal$ has zero probability under the distribution of $X$ or if $\Vcal$ contains none of the data points $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.
In such cases, the conditional means $m_X(\Vcal)$ and $\bar m_n(\Vcal)$ may be arbitrarily set to $0$.

Lastly, we give a fundamental result relating a given Voronoi partition with the cluster centers to which it corresponds, in the case that the cluster centers are an optimal set of population $k$-means cluster centers.
This is usually referred to as the \textit{centroid condition}, and a proof can be found in \cite[Lemma~A]{PollardCLT} or \cite[Lemma~4.10]{GrafLuschgy}; note that both proofs rely only on $\E\|X\|<\infty$ although they have assumed the stronger $\E\|X\|^2<\infty$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:centroid-condition}
	If $X$ satisfies $\E\|X\|<\infty$ and $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k(X)$, then $\mathscr{V}:=\{\Vcal_{\pointset}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ satisfies $m_X(\mathscr{V}) = \pointset$.
	In other words, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		c = \E[X\,|\,X\in \mathcal{V}_{\pointset}(c)]
	\end{equation*}
	for all $c\in\pointset$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Balance and Constraints}\label{subsec:balance}

Our positive results concern a modification of $k$-means clustering in which the clusters are constrained to have some degree of balance, so we now give precise definitions for various notions related to balance.
Because of Lemma~\ref{lem:centroid-condition}, it is well-known that we may equivalently formulate the (unconstrained) $k$-means clustering problem in terms of either the cluster centers or the clusters.
However, adding balance constraints means that the resulting two problems need not agree, and we must choose which one to take as our definition; in this work, we choose to consider the problem of optimizing over Voronoi partitions satisfying some balance constraint.

We begin by introducing some further notation.
First, we write $\mathscr{P}_{k}$ for the set of all Voronoi partitions of $\Rbb^d$ into at most $k$ regions; in other words, $\mathscr{V}\in \mathscr{P}_{k}$ if and only if we have $\mathscr{V} = \{\Vcal_{\pointset}(c)\}_{c\in\pointset}$ for some $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$.
Then we give the following:

\begin{definition}\label{def:Voronoi-distortion}
	Suppose that $X$ satisfies $\E\|X\|<\infty$.
	The \textit{(population) excess distortion} of $\mathscr{V}\in\mathscr{P}_k$ is
	\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Voronoi-distortion}
		D_X(\mathscr{V}) :=\Ebb\sqb{\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \ind\{X\in\Vcal_{\ell}\} \|m_X(\Vcal_{\ell})-X\|^2  - \|X\|^2 }=-\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \Pbb(X\in\Vcal_{\ell}) \|m_X(\Vcal_{\ell})\|^2.
	\end{equation}
	(More precisely, $D_X(\mathscr{V})$ is the excess distortion of $\mathscr{V}$ relative to the partition $\mathscr{V}_0 := \{\Rbb^d\}$, which is generated by $\{0\}$.
	However, in this paper, we will not consider the excess distortion with respect to more general Voronoi partitions.)
\end{definition}

Note that~\eqref{eqn:Voronoi-distortion} provides two equivalent formulations of the excess distortion of $\mathscr{V}$.
Although the equivalence of these definitions is elementary, we will see throughout the paper that it turns out to be very useful to have both formulations.
This observation has been made in previous works under the stronger condition of $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$ (e.g., \cite[Remark~4.6(c)]{GrafLuschgy}), but we emphasize that it also holds under the weaker $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.

We also note that, although $\mathscr{V}$ need not be equal to the Voronoi partition generated by the conditional means $m_X(\mathscr{V})$, we nonetheless have the following well-known result which states that the corresponding unconstrained problems have the same value.
We emphasize that this may be regarded as an extension of Lemma~\ref{lem:centroid-condition} to the case where optimal clusters need not exist.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:equivalence_distortions}
	If $X$ satisfies $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$, then $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) = \inf_{\mathscr{V}\in\mathscr{P}_{k}} D_X(\mathscr{V})$ for  $\pointset_0=\{0\}$.
\end{lemma}

Similarly, we define analogous notions for the empirical-level problem.
As usual, this can be seen as a special case of the population-level problem when the distribution of $X$ is assumed to be equal to the empirical measure of the samples $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.

\begin{definition}
	The \textit{empirical excess distortion} of $\mathscr{V}\in\mathscr{P}_k$ is defined as
	\begin{equation}
		\bar D_n(\mathscr{V}) :=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \ind\{X\in\Vcal_{\ell}\}\left( \|\bar m_n(\Vcal_{\ell})-X_i\|^2-\|X_i\|^2\right)  = -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \ind\{X_i\in\Vcal_{\ell}\} \|\bar m_n(\Vcal_{\ell})\|^2.
	\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Now we may define the central notion of balanced $k$-means clustering.
Because we will use empirical balanced $k$-means clustering to consistently estimate the cluster centers in the unconstrained population $k$-means clustering problem, we focus for now on balance constraints at the empirical level.
\begin{definition}\label{def:balance-k-means-emp}
	Fix some $0 \le \gamma\le n$.
	A Voronoi partition $\mathscr{V}$ is called a \textit{$\gamma$-balanced Voronoi partition} if it satisfies $\#\{1\le i \le n:X_i\in \Vcal\}\ge \gamma$ for all $\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}$, and we write $\bar{\mathscr{P}}_{n,k,\gamma}$ for the set of all $\gamma$-balanced Voronoi partitions with at most $k$ regions.
	An element $\mathscr{V}\in\bar{\mathscr{P}}_{n,k,\alpha}$ is called an \textit{empirical $\gamma$-balanced $k$-means clustering Voronoi partition} if it satisfies $\bar D_n(\mathscr{V}) \le \bar D_n(\mathscr{V}')$ for all $\mathscr{V}'\in\bar{\mathscr{P}}_{n,k,\gamma}$.
	We write $\bar \allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma}:=\{\bar m_n(\mathscr{V}): \mathscr{V}\in\bar{\mathscr{P}}_{n,k,\gamma}\}$, any element of which is called a \textit{$\gamma$-balanced set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers}.
\end{definition}

Although the preceding definition is a bit involved, the interpretation is straightforward:
A set of empirical $\gamma$-balanced $k$-means cluster centers is nothing more than the set of conditional means of the empirical distribution of the data, with respect to some empirical $\gamma$-balanced $k$-means clustering Voronoi partition.
Also, we note that there always exist some set of $\gamma$-balanced empirical $k$-means cluster centers, since there are only finitely many partitions of the $n$ data points.
We lastly note that there are some subtleties to this definition, explained in the following:

\begin{remark}\label{rem:def-balance}
	The Voronoi partition generated by $\bar m_n(\mathscr{V})$ need not be $\gamma$-balanced, even though $\mathscr{V}$ is required to be $\gamma$-balanced.
	This discrepancy highlights the fact that there is not a canonical way to impose the balance constraint; while we have chosen one particular formalism of the balanced clustering problem for convenience in our proofs, we note that other choices may also be interesting.
\end{remark}

\section{The Three Cases}\label{sec:cases}

In this section, we show that the population-level $k$-means clustering problem \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} is naturally divided into three cases, as outlined in the introduction.
That is, the distribution of $X$ may be such that problem \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has (i) a solution hence finite optimal excess distortion, (ii) a finite optimal excess distortion but no solution, or (iii) infinite optimal excess distortion hence no solution.
Throughout this section, $X$ always denotes a random variable satisfying $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$.

Most of our explicit examples involve distributions with power-law tails.
The basic starting point is a random variable $X$ said to have a \textit{Pareto distribution with parameter $\alpha>0$}, denoted $\Pareto(\alpha)$, meaning
\begin{equation*}
	\Pbb(X\ge t) = t^{-\alpha} \qquad \textnormal{ for all } t\ge 1.
\end{equation*}
(We always take the support of $X$ to be $[1,\infty)$ but other definitions of Pareto distribution may involve support of the form $[x_0,\infty)$ for some $x_0>0$.)
Our examples generally take place in the simplest possible setting of $m=1$ and $k=2$ because of some explicit calculations that can be done with Pareto distributions, although we note that it is easy to generalize these examples to higher dimensions, higher numbers of clusters, regularly-varying tails, and more.        

First we consider case (i), that problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} admits a solution, and hence the optimal excess distortion is finite.
By the general results of \cite{Pollard}, this case obtains whenever we have $\Ebb\|X\|^2<\infty$; as we show next, it can also obtain in cases with $\Ebb\|X\|^2 = \infty$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}
	Suppose $X$ is a real-valued random variable whose distribution is symmetric and satisfies
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(|X|\ge t) = t^{-2} \qquad\textnormal{for all}\qquad t \ge 1. 
	\end{equation*}
	(In other words, $X$ is symmetric and $|X|\sim \Pareto(2)$.)
	Then $\allpointsets_2(X) = \{\{-2,2\}\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	By Lemma~\ref{lem:centroid-condition}, we may parameterize every putative $\pointset\in\allpointsets_2(X)$ by the set of possible Voronoi regions.
	Since $d=1$ and $k=2$, the only possible Voronoi regions are of the form $\{(-\infty,r),[r,\infty)\}$ for $r\in\Rbb$.
	(Recall by \cite[Theorem~4.2]{GrafLuschgy} that we may assign the boundary $\{r\}$ arbitrarily to either region; we choose the right region for no particular reason.)
	Thus it follows that, if there exists some $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k(X)$, then it may be written $\pointset = \pointset(r)$, where
	\begin{equation*}
		\min (\pointset(r)) := \E[X\,|X< r]\qquad \textnormal{ and }\qquad\max (\pointset(r)) = \Ebb[X\,|X\ge r].
	\end{equation*}
	We can directly calculate these values, by considering cases for $r\in\Rbb$.
	If $-1\le r\le 1$, then we have $\min (\pointset(r)) = -2$ and $\max (\pointset(r)) = 2$.
	If $r>1$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\min (\pointset(r)) =\frac{r^{-1}}{1-\frac{1}{2}r^{-2}} \qquad\textnormal{ and }\qquad\max (\pointset(r)) = 2r,
	\end{equation*}
	and the $r<-1$ case can be handled by symmetry.
	Therefore, the function $D:\Rbb\to\R$ defined via
	\begin{equation*}
		D(r) := \E\left[\min_{c\in \pointset(r)}\|c-X\|^2 - \|X\|^2\right]
	\end{equation*}
	is given by
	\begin{equation*}
		D(r)=\begin{cases}
			-4 &\textnormal{ if } |r|\le 1 \\
			-2r^2/(r^2-\frac{1}{2}) &\textnormal{ if } |r|> 1. \\
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	This function achieves its global minimum on all of $-1\le r\le 1$, hence we have $\allpointsets_2(X) = \{\{-2,2\}\}$ as claimed.
\end{proof}

When case (i) holds, it is natural to hope that each set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers should be close to some set of population $k$-means cluster centers.
This question is taken up in detail Section~\ref{sec:centers}.

Second, we consider case (ii), that problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has a finite optimal excess distortion but no solution.
To see that this is possible, we consider the following.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:1-sided-pareto-pop}
	Suppose $X$ is a real-valued random variable whose distribution satisfies
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(X\ge t) = t^{-2} \qquad\textnormal{for all}\qquad t \ge 1. 
	\end{equation*}
	(In other words, $X$ $\sim \Pareto(2)$.)
	Then $\allpointsets_2(X) = \varnothing$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	The proof is similar to the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop} above.
	By Lemma~\ref{lem:centroid-condition}, we parameterize putative $\pointset\in\allpointsets_2(X)$ by $\{(-\infty,r),[r,\infty)\}$ for $r\in\Rbb$.
	If $r<1$ then $\Pbb(X<r) = 0$ meaning one cluster center is not well-defined, so we restrict our attention to the case of $r\ge 1$.
	By direct calculation for $r\ge 1$, we get            
	\begin{equation*}
		\min (\pointset(r)) := \E[X\,|X\le r] = \frac{2(1-r^{-1})}{1-r^{-2}}\qquad \textnormal{ and }\qquad\max (\pointset(r)) := \E[X\,|X>r] = 2r.
	\end{equation*}
	Next, we calculate that the function $D:[1,\infty)\to\R$ defined via
	\begin{equation*}
		D(r) := \E\left[\min_{c\in \pointset(r)}\|c-X\|^2 - \|X\|^2\right]
	\end{equation*}
	is just equal to $D(r) = -8r/(r+1)$.
	Note that this decreases monotonically to $-8$ as $r\to\infty$ but that there exists no $r\in [1,\infty)$ which achieves value $-8$.
	This shows that no Voronoi partition is optimal, hence proves the claim.
\end{proof}

When case (ii) holds, it is natural to hope that each set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers should achieve a (population) excess distortion which is similar to the infimal excess distortion.
This question is taken up in detail in Section~\ref{sec:distortion}.

Some remarks about the preceding two examples are due.
First, notice that both examples involved a random variable $X$ satisfying $\Ebb|X|^2 = \infty$ but $\Ebb|X|^p<\infty$ for all $1\le p < 2$; thus, cases (i) and (ii) can obtain even in settings where the finite-variance condition $\Ebb|X|^2<\infty$ \textit{just barely} fails.
Second, we emphasize that we do not know a simple way to determine whether problem \eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} admits a solution (equivalently, which of (i) or (ii) holds); in both previous examples our analysis relied on exactly calculating the excess distortion, but this is not possible in more complicated examples.

Last, we consider case (iii) that problem~\eqref{eqn:def-pop-k-means-minimal} has infinite optimal excess distortion hence no solution.
Rather than just showing by example that this case is possible, we exactly characterize when it occurs; the answer is that it occurs if and only if $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by a scalar multiple of $\Pareto(2)$.   
This requires the following simple result, which will also be used later.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:computation_bound_norm}
	Let $X$ be a random variable such that $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by $hZ$ where $h>0$ and $Z\sim\Pareto(2)$.
	Then, for any measurable region $A\subseteq\Rbb^d$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\|\Ebb[X\mid X\in A] \| \leq \Ebb[\|X\|\mid X\in A]  \leq \frac{2h}{\sqrt{\Pbb(X\in A)}}.
	\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	The first inequality is immediate by convexity.
	The second is true if $\Pbb(X\in A) = 0$ since then there is nothing to prove, so let us assume $\Pbb(X\in A) > 0$.
	To do this, let $X$ and $Z$ be coupled onto the same probability space in such a way that we have $\|X\|\le hZ$ almost surely, so that
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(X\in A)\cdot \Ebb[\|X\|\mid X\in A] =\Ebb[\|X\|\cdot\ind\{ X\in A\}] \le h\Ebb[Z\cdot\ind\{ X\in A\}].
	\end{equation*}
	Now consider maximizing the random variable $Z\cdot\ind_{\Ecal}$ over all $\Ecal\in\sigma(X,Z)$ with $\Pbb(\Ecal) = r$, for fixed $0<r<1$.
	It is clearly maximized when $Z$ takes on its largest possible value, i.e., $\Ecal = \{Z\ge F_c^{-1}(r)\}$ where $F_c(x)=\Pbb(Z\geq x)=x^{-2}$ the complementary cumulative distribution function of $Z$.
	Thus we take $r:=\Pbb(X\in A)$ and get
	\begin{align*}
		\Pbb(X\in A)\cdot \Ebb[\|X\|\mid X\in A] &\leq h \Ebb[Z \cdot \ind\{X\in A\}]\\
		&\leq h\Ebb[Z \cdot \ind\{Z \geq F_c^{-1}(r)\}]\\
		&=h\Ebb\sqb{Z \cdot \ind\{Z \geq r^{-1/2}\}} = 2h\sqrt{r}
	\end{align*}
	This ends the proof.
\end{proof}

The next result shows the desired claim, namely that $X$ has finite optimal excess distortion if and only if it is stochastically dominated by some scalar multiple of $\Pareto(2)$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:charact_finite_obj}
	For $k\geq 2$ and $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$, the following are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(a)] For some (equivalently, all) $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$, we have $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0) >-\infty$.
		\item[(b)] There exists a constant $r>0$ such that we have $\Pbb(\|X\|\geq t) \leq rt^{-2}$ for all $t\ge 0$
	\end{enumerate}
	Consequently, if $\Ebb\|X\|^p=\infty$ for some $1\le p < 2$, then $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0) =-\infty$ for all $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	We start by proving that (a) implies (b).
	Fix $\pointset_0=\{0\}$ and consider a random variable $X$ for which property (a) holds.
	For convenience, denote
	\begin{equation*}
		M := -\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0)<\infty,
	\end{equation*}
	and note $D_X(\pointset_0\,|\, \pointset_0)=0$ hence $M\geq 0$.
	Now for any vector $v\in \Rbb^d$ with $\|v\|=1$ and any $t\geq 0$, we consider the set of cluster centers $\pointset(v,t):=\{0,2t v\}$, and we note that the corresponding Voronoi partition is given by the pair of halfspaces $\{x\in\Rbb^d: v^\top X<t\}$ and $\{x\in\Rbb^d:v^\top  X\geq t\}$.
	Consequently, we can compute the distortion as follows:        
	\begin{equation*}
		D_X(\pointset(v,t) \,|\,\pointset_0) = \Ebb\sqb{ (\|2tv\|^2-4tv^\top X)\ind\{\|X-2tv\|<\|X\|\}} \leq -4t^2\Pbb(v^\top X \geq t) \\
	\end{equation*}
	Rearranging this and using the definition of $M$, we get
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(v^\top X\geq t) \leq \frac{M}{t^2}.
	\end{equation*}
	By applying this bound to all the standard basis vectors in $\Rbb^d$, we get
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(\|X\|\geq t) \leq 
		\Pbb\paren{\|X\|_\infty \geq \frac{t}{\sqrt d}} \leq \frac{2d^2M}{t^2}.
	\end{equation*}
	for all $t\ge 0$.
	This proves the claim, with $r=2d^2M$.

	Next we show that (b) implies (a), so let $X$ be any random variable satisfying condition (b). Fix $\pointset_0=\{0\}$ and any set of points $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$, and compute:
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower_bound_distortion_1}
		\begin{split}
			D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0) &=\sum_{c\in\pointset}            
			\Ebb\sqb{(\|c-X\|^2 - \|X\|^2)\cdot\ind\{X\in\Vcal_\pointset(c)\}} \notag \\
			&=\sum_{c\in\pointset} \Pbb(X\in\Vcal_\pointset(c)) \paren{\|c\|^2 - 2c^\top  m_X(\Vcal_\pointset(c))} \notag \\
			&\geq - \sum_{c\in\pointset} \Pbb(X\in\Vcal_\pointset(c)) \|m_X(\Vcal_\pointset(c))\|^2.
		\end{split}
	\end{equation}
	Next, fix any $c\in\pointset$ such that $\Pbb(X\in\Vcal_\pointset(c))>0$.
	By condition (b), there exists a constant $r>0$ such that the random variable $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by $\sqrt{r}\cdot Z$ where $Z$ is a random variable defined via $\Pbb(Z\ge t) = t^{-2}$ for all $t\ge 1$. Therefore, Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm} implies
	\begin{align*}
		\|m_X(\Vcal_\pointset(c))\| \leq \Ebb\left[\|X\| \mid  X\in\Vcal_\pointset(c)\right] 
		\leq 2\sqrt{\frac{r}{\Pbb(X\in\Vcal_{\pointset}(c))}},
	\end{align*}
	and this gives
	\begin{equation*}
		D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0) \geq -4kr.
	\end{equation*}
	Because this holds for all $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$, we have shown that condition (a) holds.

	Lastly, we show that $\Ebb\|X\|^p = \infty$ for some $1\le p<2$ implies that conditions (a) and (b) both fail.
	To do this, we observe the contrapositive, namely that condition (b) implies $\Ebb\|X\|^p < \infty$ for all $1\le p<2$.
	This follows immediately from the tail formula, since
	\begin{equation*}
		\Ebb \|X\|^p = \int_0^\infty \Pbb(\|X\|^p \geq t) \diff t \leq \frac{r}{2-p}<\infty.
	\end{equation*}
	for any $p>0$.
	This completes the proof.
\end{proof}

When case (iii) holds, it remains unclear what a natural form of consistency should be.
A naive guess would be to require that the empirical $k$-means cluster centers achieve a (population) excess distortion which diverges, but we can show in a simple example that this notion is quite weak.
Indeed, consider the following ``clustering procedure'' which, given samples $X_1,\ldots,X_n$, outputs the cluster centers $\{0,\bar v_n\}$, where $\bar v_n\in\Rbb^d$ is any vector which minimizes the sample variance of the subset of the data falling in the region $H_v:=\{x\in\Rbb^d: v^\top x\ge \|v\|^2\}$, provided that $H_v$ does not contain too few points.

More precisely, for some integer $\beta_n\ge 0$, we let
\begin{equation*}
	\bar v_n\in\arg\max\set{ \|v\|^2 \sum_{i=1}^n \ind\{v^\top X_i\geq \|v\|^2\} : v\in\Rbb^d,\, \sum_{i=1}^n \ind\{v^\top X_i\geq \|v\|^2\} \geq \beta_n},
\end{equation*}
and the following result shows $\{0,\bar v_n\}$ achieves the putative form of consistency in case (iii).
This is not a meaningful set of cluster centers, however, since it always yields two clusters although it is ``consistent'' even for $k\ge 3$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:naive_cluster_estimator}
	If $X$ satisfies $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ and $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)=-\infty$ for some $k\in\Nbb$, and if $\log n \ll \beta_n\ll n$, then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\lim_{n\to\infty} D_X(\{0, \bar v_n\} \,|\,\pointset_0)=-\infty
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	We first give an upper bound on the excess distortion of clusters of the form $\{0,v\}$ for $v\in \Rbb^d$:
	\begin{align}\label{eq:upper_bound_excess_distortion_naive}
		D_X(\{0,v\} \,|\,\pointset_0) = \Ebb\sqb{ (\|v\|^2-2v^\top X)\ind\{\|X-v\|<\|X\|\}} \leq -\|v\|^2 \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2)=:-f(v).
	\end{align}
	Next, let $\Hcal$ be the set of halfspaces in $\Rbb^d$.
	Since linear separators in $\Rbb^d$ have VC dimension $d+1$, 
	classical uniform convergence theorem for VC classes (e.g., \cite[Theorem 5.1]{boucheron2005theory}) imply that there is a constant $c_1>0$ for which the following holds. On an event $\Ecal_n$ of probability at least $1-n^{-2}$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\min\left\{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in H\}, \Pbb(X\in H)\right\} \geq c_1 \frac{d\log n}{n}
	\end{equation*}
	implies
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{1}{2}\Pbb(X\in H) \le \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in H\}\le 2\Pbb(X\in H)
	\end{equation*}
	for any $H\in\Hcal$.
	Now use $\log n = o(\gamma_n)$ to get $n$ sufficiently large so that $\gamma_n\geq c_1 d\log n$, and suppose that $\Ecal_n$ holds. By equation~\eqref{eq:upper_bound_excess_distortion_naive} and the definition of $\Ecal_n$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		-D_X(\{0,\bar v_n\} \,|\,\pointset_0)\ge f(\bar v_n)\geq \frac{\|\bar v_n\|^2}{2} \sum_{i=1}^n \ind\{\bar v_n^\top X_i\geq \|\bar v_n\|^2\}.
	\end{equation*}
	Also, the VC bound, the definition of $\bar v_n$, and the definition of $\Ecal_n$ imply
	\begin{align*}
		\|\bar v_n\|^2\sum_{i=1}^n \ind\{\bar v_n^\top X_i\geq \|\bar v_n\|^2\} &\geq \max\set{ \|v\|^2 \sum_{i=1}^n\ind\{v^\top X_i\geq \|v\|^2\} : v\in\Rbb^n,\, \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) \geq \frac{2\beta_n}{n}}\\
		&\geq \frac{1}{2} \sup\set{ \|v\|^2 \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) : v\in\Rbb^n,\, \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) \geq \frac{2\beta_n}{n}}.
	\end{align*}
	Since $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)=-\infty$, the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj} precisely shows that $\sup\set{ \|v\|^2 \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) : v\in\Rbb^n} = \infty$.
	In other words, the function
	\begin{equation*}
		M(\epsilon):= \sup\set{ \|v\|^2 \Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) : v\in\Rbb^n,\,\Pbb(v^\top X\geq \|v\|^2) \geq \epsilon},
	\end{equation*}
	satisfies $M(\epsilon)\to\infty$ as $\varepsilon\to 0$.
	Thus, combining the two previous inequalities, we have shown
	\begin{equation*}
		\Ecal_n\subseteq\left\{D_X(\{0,\bar v_n\} \,|\,\pointset_0) \leq -\frac{1}{4}M\paren{\frac{2\beta_n}{n}}\right\}
	\end{equation*}
	for sufficiently large $n$.
	Since $\Pbb(\Ecal_n) \ge 1-n^{-2}$ by construction and since $\beta_n = o(n)$ by assumption, Borel-Cantelli implies $D_X(\{0,\bar v_n\} \,|\,\pointset_0)\to-\infty$ as $n\to\infty$ almost surely.
\end{proof}



\section{Convergence of Cluster Centers}\label{sec:centers}

In this section we consider the problem of whether the empirical $k$-means cluster centers converge, in some sense, to some population $k$-means cluster centers in case (i).
We will show that this naive guess can fail (Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}), but also that imposing a certain linear balance constraint indeed guarantees such convergence (Theorem~\ref{thm:cluster-consistency}).
Throughout this section, we let $X$ denote a random variable such that case (i) holds; that is, there exists a set of population $k$-means cluster centers hence the optimal excess distortion is finite.

Notationally, it is slightly complicated to precisely state a notion of convergence for $k$-means clustering.
One reason is that the objects of interest are sets of points, so we need a notion of convergence for sets that is strong enough to be practically meaningful.
Another reason is that there typically does not exist a unique set of $k$-means cluster centers for either the empirical or population problems, so we need a notion of convergence which accommodates this ill-posedness.
In order to overcome these challenges, we follow the form of consistency given in most existing positive results \cite{Pollard, JaffeClustering, Lember, Parna1, Parna2}, which we now define.

This form of consistency requires the following notion of distance between non-empty compact subsets of $\Rbb^d$, which was used in the earliest consistency results for $k$-means clustering by Pollard \cite{Pollard}.

\begin{definition}
	For any non-empty compact sets $\pointset,\pointset'\subseteq\Rbb^d$, we define
	\begin{equation*}
		\dhaus(\pointset,\pointset') := \max\left\{\max_{c\in \pointset}\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c-c'\|,\max_{c'\in \pointset'}\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-c'\|\right\},
	\end{equation*}
	called their \textit{Hausdorff distance}.
\end{definition}

It is worth mentioning that there are several equivalent formulations of the Hausdorff distance.
For instance, if $\#\pointset = \#\pointset' = k<\infty$, then we have $\dhaus(\pointset,\pointset') \le \varepsilon$ if and only if there exist labelings $\pointset = \{c_1,\ldots, c_k\}$ and $\pointset' = \{c_1',\ldots, c_k'\}$ satisfying $\|c_{\ell}-c_{\ell}'\|\le \varepsilon$ for all $1\le \ell\le k$.
Alternatively, if we define the \textit{$\varepsilon$-thickening} of $\pointset$ via
\begin{equation*}
	\pointset^{\varepsilon}:=\bigcup_{c\in\pointset}\{x\in\Rbb^d: \|c-x\|\le \varepsilon\}
\end{equation*}
then $\dhaus(\pointset,\pointset')$ is exactly the smallest $\varepsilon\ge 0$ such that we have both $\pointset^{\varepsilon}\supseteq\pointset'$ and $(\pointset')^{\varepsilon}\supseteq\pointset$.

Now we may state that a natural form of consistency, following \cite{Pollard, JaffeClustering, Lember, Parna1, Parna2}, is
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:centers-consistent}
	\max_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k}}\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{n,k}(X)}\dhaus(\bar\pointset_n,\pointset)\to 0
\end{equation}
almost surely, as $n\to\infty$.
We think of~\eqref{eqn:centers-consistent} as a ``no false positives'' property, since it shows that every set of empirical $k$-means cluster centers must be close, with respect to the Hausdorff distance $\dhaus$, to some set of population $k$-means cluster centers.
If there indeed exist uniquely defined sets of empirical and population $k$-means cluster centers, denoted $\{\bar \pointset_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ and $\pointset$ respectively, then~\eqref{eqn:centers-consistent} is equivalent to $\dhaus(\bar \pointset_n,\pointset)\to 0$ almost surely, as $n\to\infty$.

Unfortunately, we show in an example that~\eqref{eqn:centers-consistent} can plainly fail in case (i); see Figure~\ref{fig:two_sided_pareto} for a visualization of the sample paths in this example.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}
	Suppose $X$ is a real-valued random variable whose distribution is symmetric and satisfies
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(|X|\ge t) = t^{-2} \qquad\textnormal{for all}\qquad t \ge 1. 
	\end{equation*}
	(In other words, $X$ is symmetric and $|X|\sim \Pareto(2)$, which was studied at the population-level in Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}.)
	Then, there exists a universal constant $c>0$ such that almost surely, for infinitely many $n\in\Nbb$, for any $\bar\pointset_n\in \bar \allpointsets_{n,2}$ we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\max(\bar{\pointset}_n) \ge c\,\frac{\sqrt{n}}{\log n} \quad \textnormal{or} \quad \min(\bar{\pointset}_n) \le -c\,\frac{\sqrt{n}}{\log n}.
	\end{equation*}
	Consequently, we have almost surely that, for infinitely many $n\in\Nbb$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\min_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{n,2}(X)} \dhaus(\bar\pointset_n,\pointset) \geq c\frac{\sqrt n}{\log n}-2.
	\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}
	We introduce some notation for convenience.
	First, write $X_{(1)}^{n},\ldots, X_{(n)}^{n}$ for the order statistics of the samples $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.
	Then, for integers $1\le i<j\le n$, write 
	\begin{equation*}
		S_n{[\,:i]} := \sum_{\ell=1}^{i}X_{(\ell)}^{n}, \qquad S_n[i:\,] := \sum_{\ell=i+1}^{n}X_{(\ell)}^{n}, \qquad \textnormal{ and} \qquad S_n[i:j]:=\sum_{\ell=i+1}^{j}X_{(\ell)}^{n}
	\end{equation*}
	for the partial sums of contiguous blocks of $X_{(1)}^{n},\ldots, X_{(n)}^{n}$, and
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar{X}_n{[\,:i]} := \frac{1}{i}S_n{[\,:i]}, \qquad \bar{X}_n[i:\,] := \frac{1}{n-i}S_n{[i:\,]}\qquad \textnormal{ and} \qquad \bar{X}_n[i:j]:=\frac{1}{j-i}S_n[i:j]
	\end{equation*}
	for the corresponding partial averages.
	(Note that we use non-standard conventions regarding inclusivity and exclusivity of the endpoints.)
	As in the proofs of Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop} and Proposition~\ref{prop:1-sided-pareto-pop}, we apply Lemma~\ref{lem:centroid-condition} to see that $\bar{\pointset}_n\in\bar\pointset_{n,2}$ is uniquely described by its Voronoi regions; moreover, we may assume that the Voronoi regions are simply
	\begin{equation*}
		\left\{\big(-\infty, X_{(i)}^{n}\big),\big[X_{(i)}^{n},\infty\big)\right\},
	\end{equation*}
	for some integer $1\le i\le n$.
	Now define $\bar{D}_{n,2}:\{1,\ldots, n\}\to\R$ via
	\begin{align*}
		\bar{D}_{n,2}(i) &:= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{i} \left(X_{(\ell)}^{n}-\bar{X}_n[\,:i]\right)^2 +  \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\ell=i}^{n}\left(X_{(\ell)}^n-\bar{X}_n[i:\,]\right)^2 \\
		&= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\ell=1}^{n} X_{\ell}^2 - \frac{i}{n} \left(\bar{X}_n[\,:i]\right)^2 - \frac{n-i}{n} \left(\bar{X}_n[i:\,]\right)^2,
	\end{align*}
	which is simply the (absolute, not excess) empirical distortion of the empirical $k$-means cluster centers when the Voronoi regions are as above.

	The first step of the proof is to show
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:useful_comparison}
		\left\{\bar{D}_{n,2}(i)>\bar{D}_{n,2}(j)\right\} = \left\{\bar{X}_n[j:\,] - \bar{X}_n[i:j] > \sqrt{\frac{i(n-i)}{j(n-j)}} \left(\bar{X}_n[i:j] - \bar{X}_n[\,:i]\right)\right\}
	\end{equation}
	for any $1\le i<j\le n$.
	Indeed, this follows by expanding
	\begin{align*}
		&n(\bar{D}_{n,2}(i) - \bar{D}_{n,2}(j) )\\
		&=\frac{1}{j}(S_n[\,:j])^2 + \frac{1}{n-j}(S_n[j:\,])^2 - \frac{1}{i}(S_n[\,:i])^2 - \frac{1}{n-i}(S_n[i:\,])^2\\
		&=\left(\frac{1}{j}-\frac{1}{i}\right)(S_n[\,:i])^2 + \left(\frac{1}{j}-\frac{1}{n-i}\right)(S_n[i:j])^2 + \left(\frac{1}{n-j}-\frac{1}{n-i}\right)(S_n[j:\,])^2 \\
		& \qquad \qquad + \frac{2}{j} S_n[\,:i]S_n[i:j] - \frac{2}{n-i} S_n[i:j]S_n[j:\,] \\
		&= (j-i) \frac{n-j}{n-i}\left( \frac{1}{n-j}S_n[j:\,]
		- \frac{1}{j-i}S_n[i:j]\right)^2
		-  (j-i) \frac{i}{j}\left( \frac{1}{j-i}S_n[i:j] - \frac{1}{i}S_n[\,:i]\right)^2.
	\end{align*}
	and rearranging.

	Next, we introduce some events and bound their probability of failure.
	To do this, we introduce the notation
	\begin{equation*}
		N_+ := \#\set{1\le i\le n: X_i>0}\quad \text{and} \quad N_- := n-N_+,
	\end{equation*}
	to count the number of samples with either sign.
	Then, by Hoeffding's inequality, the event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Ecal_n:=\set{\abs{ N_+ - \frac{n}{2}} \leq \frac{n}{4}} 
	\end{equation*}
	has probability at least $1-2e^{-n/8}$.
	Next, for $p\ge 1$, we consider i.i.d. samples $Z_1,\ldots,Z_p$ from the one-sided Pareto distribution from Proposition~\ref{prop:1-sided-pareto-pop}, and, as above, we use the notation $Z^p_{(1)},\ldots,Z^p_{(p)}$ for the order statistics of the samples $Z_1,\ldots, Z_p$; by convention, let $Z^p_{(0)}:=1$.
	We claim that for any integers $0<a\leq b\leq p$ and any $C\geq 1$, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_pareto_os}
		\Pbb\left(\frac{Z^p_{(p-a)} }{Z^p_{(p-b)}} \leq \sqrt{\frac{b}{Ca}}\right) \leq e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}a}.
	\end{equation}
	To see this, first use the fact that, conditionally on $X_{(n-b:n)}$, the ratios $X_{(n-b+1:n)}/X_{(n-b:n)},\ldots,X_{(n:n)}/X_{(n-b:n)}$ are distributed exactly as $Z^b_{(1)},\ldots, Z^b_{(b)}$.
	Then, apply Chernoff's inequality to the random variable $Y\sim\Binom(b,Ca/b)$, and use the well-known relative entropy bound $D(x\parallel y) \ge (y-x)^2/(2y)$ for $x\leq y$.
	This yields
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb\left(\frac{Z^p_{(p-a)} }{Z^p_{(p-b)}} \leq \sqrt{\frac{b}{Ca}}\right) = \Pbb\left(Z^b_{(b-a)} \leq \sqrt{\frac{b}{Ca}}\right) =\Pbb(Y\leq a) \leq e^{-b D( \frac{a}{b} \parallel \frac{Ca}{b})} \leq e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}a}.
	\end{equation*}
	as claimed.
	Note also that in particular we have
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_proba_event_E}
		\Pbb\left(Z^p_{(n-a)} >  \sqrt{\frac{p}{Ca}}\right) \geq 1-e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}a}.
	\end{equation}
	by taking $b=p$.
	Next note that, conditionally on $\{N_+=n_+\}$, the samples $\set{X_i:X_i>0,1\le i \le n}$ and $\set{-X_i:X_i<0,1\le i \le n}$ are, respectively, independent sequences of $n_+$ and $n_-:=n-n_+$ i.i.d.\ samples from $\Pareto(2)$. Therefore, equation~\eqref{eq:bound_pareto_os} and the union bound imply that for any $1\le a\le n$, the event
	\begin{multline*}
		\Fcal_n(a;C) := \set{0<X_{(n-b)}^n <  \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{b}} \cdot X_{(n-a)}^n  \textnormal{ for all }a \le b \le N_+-1\}} \\
		\cap \set{0 >X_{(b)}^n > \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{b}} \cdot X_{(a+1)}^n\textnormal{ for all } a+1 \le b \le\ N_{-}}
	\end{multline*}
	has probability
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:proba_event_F}
		\Pbb(\Fcal_n(a;C))\geq 1 - n \cdot e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}a}.
	\end{equation}
	We also introduce the following event for $C\geq 1$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\Gcal_n(a;C):=\set{X_{(a+1)}^n \geq -\sqrt{\frac{Cn}{a}}}\cap \set{X_{(n-a)}^n \leq \sqrt{\frac{Cn}{a}}}.
	\end{equation*}
	To bound the probability of failure, apply Chernoff's inequality to the random variable $Y\sim \Binom(n,a/(2Cn))$ to get for $C\geq 8$, 
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:proba_event_G}
		\Pbb(\Gcal_n^c(a;C)) \leq 2\Pbb(Y\geq a) \leq 2e^{-nD(\frac{a}{n}\parallel \frac{a}{2Cn})} \leq \frac{2}{(2C-1)^{a/4}} \leq \frac{2}{C^{a/4}},
	\end{equation}
	where we used the bound $D(x+y\parallel x) \geq \frac{y}{2}\log\frac{y}{x}$ whenever $y\geq 8x$ and $x,y\leq \sfrac{1}{4}$ (e.g., see \cite[Lemma~16]{blanchard2024tight}).

	Now we return to the main proof, where the bulk of the work is to develop estimates on the value of $\bar D_{n,2}(i)$ for moderate values of the index $i$.
	We begin with the positive side.
	Suppose that the events $\Ecal_n$, $\Fcal_n(a;C)$, and $\Gcal_n(a;C)$ hold for some $1\le a \le n$ and $C\geq 8$. If $a+1\le i\le N_+$, then we use $\Fcal_n(a;C)$, an elementary inequality, and $\Gcal_n(a;C)$ to get:        
	\begin{align}
		\bar X_n[n-i:n-1] &= \frac{a-1}{i-1}\bar X_n[n-a:n-1] + \frac{1}{i-1} S_n[n-i:n-a]\notag \\
		&\leq \frac{a}{i}X_{(n)}^n + \frac{1}{i-1} X_{(n-a)}^n \sum_{j=a}^{i-1} \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{j}} \notag\\
		&\leq \frac{a}{i}X_{(n)}^n + 2\sqrt{\frac{2Ca}{i}} X_{(n-a)}^n, \notag\\
		&\leq \frac{a}{i}X_{(n)}^n + 2C\sqrt{\frac{2n}{i}}.\label{eq:upper_bound_avg}
	\end{align}
	Similarly, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\bar X_n[:n-i] \geq \bar X_n[:N_-] &\geq\frac{a}{N_-} \bar X_n[:a] + \frac{1}{N_-} X_{(a+1)}^n \sum_{j=a+1}^{N_-} \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{j}} \notag\\
		&\geq \frac{a}{n_-} \bar X_n[:a] + 2\sqrt{\frac{2Ca}{N_-}}  X_{(a+1)}^n \notag \\
		&\geq \frac{4a}{n} X_{(1)}^n - 4C\sqrt 2. 
	\end{align*}
	If we additionally have the event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Zcal_n^-(a;C):=\set{-X_{(1)}^n \leq \frac{Cn}{2a} },
	\end{equation*}
	then we have obtained
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower_bound_mean-}
		\bar X_n[:n-i] \geq \bar X_n[:N_-] \geq -8C.
	\end{equation}
	Next, using equation~\eqref{eq:useful_comparison} we obtain for $\ell \geq a$,
	\begin{align*}
		\Acal_n(\ell)&:=\set{\bar D_{n,2}(i)>\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) \textnormal{ for all } n-N_{+}+1\le i \le n-\ell} \\
		&= \set{ X_{(n)}^n>\bar X_n[n-i:n-1] + \sqrt{i}\cdot  (\bar X_n[n-i:n-1] - \bar X_n[:n-i]) \textnormal{ for all } \ell \le i \le N_{+}-1} \\
		&\supseteq \set{ X_{(n)}^n>2\sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[n-i:n-1]  - \sqrt i\cdot  \bar X_n[:n-i]) \textnormal{ for all } \ell \le i \le N_{+}-1}
	\end{align*}
	Combining this with equation~\eqref{eq:upper_bound_avg} and \eqref{eq:lower_bound_mean-} shows that, if additionally $\ell\geq 16a^2$ (i.e., $\ell\ge 4a$), then
	\begin{align}
		\Acal_n(\ell) \supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)&\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap\Zcal_n^-(a;C) \\
		&\cap  \set{X_{(n)}^n>\frac{2a}{\sqrt i} X_{(n)}^n + 4C\sqrt{2n} +8C\sqrt{i} \textnormal{ for all } \ell \le i \le N_{+}-1}  \notag\\
		\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n&(a;C) \cap \Zcal_n^-(a;C)\cap  \set{X_{(n)}^n >25C\sqrt{n} }. \label{eq:decomposition_positive_side}
	\end{align}
	We use a similar argument for the negative side.
	Suppose that $\Ecal_n$, $\Fcal_n(a;C)$, and $\Gcal_n(a;C)$ hold. Then, for $a+1\le i\le N_-$, we compute
	\begin{align}
		\bar X_n[:i]  &= \frac{a}{i} \bar X_n[:a] + \frac{1}{i} S_n[a:i]\notag \\
		&\geq \frac{a}{i} \bar X_n[:a] + \frac{1}{i} X_{(a+1)}^n \sum_{j=a+1}^i \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{j}} \notag\\
		&\geq \frac{a}{i} X_{(1)}^n + 2\sqrt{\frac{2Ca}{i}} X_{(a+1)}^n \notag\\
		&\geq \frac{a}{i} X_{(1)}^n - 2C\sqrt{\frac{2n}{i}}.\label{eq:lower_bound_avg-}
	\end{align}
	Equation~\eqref{eq:useful_comparison} shows that, any $\ell\geq a$, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\Bcal_n(\ell) &:= \set{\bar D_{n,2}(i)>\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) \textnormal{ for all } \ell+1 \le i \le N_{-}}\\
		&\supseteq \set{X_{(n)}^n> 2\sqrt i\cdot  \bar X_n[n-N_+:n-1] - \sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[:i]\textnormal{ for all } \ell+1 \le i \le N_{-}}.
	\end{align*}
	Last, we introduce the event $\Ycal_n:=\{X_{(n)}^n \geq - X_{(1)}^n\}$.
	Then, for $n$ sufficiently large, equations~ \eqref{eq:upper_bound_avg} and~\eqref{eq:lower_bound_avg-} yield the following, for any $a<\sqrt n/8$ and $\ell\geq 16a^2$,
	\begin{align}
		\Bcal_n(\ell) &\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C)  \cap \Ycal_n \notag\\
		&\qquad \cap\set{ X_{(n)}^n> \frac{2a\sqrt i}{N_+} X_{(n)}^n +4C \sqrt{\frac{2ni}{N_+}}   - \frac{a}{\sqrt i} X_{(1)}^n + 2C\sqrt{2n} \textnormal{ for all }\ell \le i \le N_{+}-1}  \notag\\
		&\supseteq\Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C) \cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n\cap  \set{\frac{1}{2}X_{(n)}^n > - \frac{a}{\sqrt \ell} X_{(1)}^n + 6C\sqrt{2n} } \notag \\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n\cap  \set{X_{(n)}^n > 24C\sqrt{2n} }. 
		\label{eq:decomposition_negative_side}
	\end{align}
	We now combine equations~\eqref{eq:decomposition_positive_side} and ~\eqref{eq:decomposition_negative_side} to obtain, for $n$ sufficiently large and $a<\sqrt n/8$ and $\ell\geq 16a^2$, that
	\begin{align}
		\Dcal_n(\ell) &:= \set{\max(\bar{\pointset}_{n}) \ge X_{(n-\ell+1)}^n \textnormal{ for all }\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \cup \set{\min(\bar{\pointset}_{n}) \le X_{(\ell)}^n \textnormal{ for all } \bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \notag\\
		&\supseteq \set{\min_{1\le i \le n-1} \bar D_{n,2}(i) < \min_{\ell+1\le i\le n-\ell} \bar D_{n,2}(i) } \notag\\
		&\supseteq \set{\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) < \min_{\ell+1\le i \le n-\ell} \bar D_{n,2}(i) } = \Acal_n(\ell)\cap\Bcal_n(\ell) \notag\\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n\cap \Zcal_n^-(a;C) \cap  \set{X_{(n)}^n > 35C\sqrt{n} } \label{eq:final_bound+}
	\end{align}
	By applying the symmetry $X_{(i)}^n\mapsto -X_{(n-i)}^n$, we also have
	\begin{align}
		\Dcal_n(\ell) &\supseteq \set{\bar D_{n,2}(1) > \min_{\ell+1 \le i\le n-\ell} \bar D_{n,2}(i) } \notag\\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n^c \cap \Zcal_n^+(a;C) \cap \set{-X_{(1)}^n >35C\sqrt n},\label{eq:final_bound-}
	\end{align}
	where 
	\begin{equation*}
		\Zcal_n^+(a;C) := \set{X_{(n)}^n \leq \frac{Cn}{2a} }.
	\end{equation*}
	For convenience, set $\Zcal_n(a;C):= \Zcal_n^+(a;C) \cup \Zcal_n^-(a;C)$.
	We will next put together equations~\eqref{eq:final_bound+} and~\eqref{eq:final_bound-}, but first we claim that
	\begin{align*}
		\Zcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n^c &= \Zcal_n^+(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n^c\\
		\Zcal_n(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n &= \Zcal_n^-(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n
	\end{align*}
	Indeed, for the first equality, if $\Zcal_n^+(a;C)$ does not hold, because of $\Ycal_n^c$ we have $-X_{(1)}^n > X_{(n)}^n > \frac{Cn}{2a}$, that is $\Zcal_n^-(a;C)$ also does not hold.
    This contradicts $\Zcal_n(a;C)$. Similarly, for the second equality, if $\Zcal_n^-(a;C)$ did not hold, as would have $X_{(n)}^n \geq -X_{(1)}^n > \frac{Cn}{2a}$ contradicting $\Zcal_n(a;C)$. Therefore,
	\begin{align}
		\Zcal_n(a;C) \cap &\set{X_{(n)}^n>35C\sqrt n} \notag \\
		&= \paren{\Zcal_n^-(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n \cap \set{X_{(n)}^n>35C\sqrt n} } \cup \paren{\Zcal_n^+(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n^c \cap \set{X_{(n)}^n>35C\sqrt n} } \notag \\
		&\subseteq \paren{\Zcal_n^-(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n \cap \set{X_{(n)}^n>35C\sqrt n} } \cup \paren{\Zcal_n^+(a;C) \cap \Ycal_n^c \cap \set{-X_{(1)}^n>35C\sqrt n} }.\label{eq:useful_event_property}
	\end{align}
	We also introduce the last event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Hcal_n(\ell;C) = \set{X_{(n-\ell+1)}^n \geq \sqrt{\frac{n}{4C\ell}}} \cap \set{X_{(\ell)}^n \leq -\sqrt{\frac{n}{4C\ell}}}.
	\end{equation*}
	Using equation~\eqref{eq:bound_proba_event_E},  we obtain
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:proba_event_H}
		\Pbb(\Hcal_n(\ell;C)\mid\Ecal_n) \geq 1-2e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}\ell}
	\end{equation}
	provided that $\ell<n/4$.

	Now we put everything together.
	Combining equations~\eqref{eq:final_bound+},~\eqref{eq:final_bound-}, and~\eqref{eq:proba_event_H}, we proved
	\begin{align}
		\Ccal_n(\ell) &:=\set{\max(\bar{\pointset}_{n}) \ge \sqrt{\frac{n}{4C\ell}} \textnormal{ for all }\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \cup \set{\min(\bar{\pointset}_{n}) \le -\sqrt{\frac{n}{4C\ell}} \textnormal{ for all }\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \notag \\
		&\supseteq \Dcal_n(\ell) \cap \Hcal_n(\ell;C) \notag\\
		&\supseteq\Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C)\cap\Hcal_n(\ell;C)\cap\Zcal_n(a;C) \cap \set{X_{(n)}^n>35C\sqrt n}, \label{eq:final_decomposition}
	\end{align}
	To bound the probability of the right-hand side, we first compute
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:proba_event_Z}
		\Pbb(\Zcal_n^c(a;C)) \leq \Pbb(Y\geq 2) \leq \frac{16a^4}{C^4n^2}.
	\end{equation}
	where $Y\sim\Binom(n,4a^2/(C^2n^2))$.
	Then, using all the failure probability bounds from \eqref{eq:proba_event_F}, \eqref{eq:proba_event_G}, \eqref{eq:proba_event_H}, and~\eqref{eq:proba_event_Z}, there exists a constant $c_0>0$ such that choosing $a_n=\ceil{c_0\log n}$ and $\ell_n=16 a_n^2$, and $C=8$, yields
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{n= 1}^{\infty} \bigg(\Pbb(\Ecal_n^c) + \Pbb(\Fcal_n^c(a_n;C)\mid \Ecal_n) + \Pbb(\Gcal_n^c(a_n;C)) + \Pbb(\Hcal_n^c(l_n;C)\mid \Ecal_n) + \Pbb(\Zcal_n^c(a_n;C))\bigg) <\infty.
	\end{equation*}
	Hence, by the Borel-Cantelli lemma, the event $\Ecal^{(0)}$ that for large enough $n$, the events $\Ecal_n$, $\Fcal_n(a_n;C)$, $\Gcal_n(a_n;C)$, $\Hcal_n(\ell_n;C)$, $\Zcal_n(a_n;C)$ are all satisfied, has probability one.
	Next, let $n_k:=2^k$ and note that for any $k\geq 1$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb\left(\max_{n_{k-1}<i\le n_k} X_i> 35C\sqrt{n_k} \right) =1-\paren{1-\frac{1}{(35C)^2 n_k}}^{n_k-n_{k-1}} \geq 1-e^{-1/(2(35C)^2)} \geq \frac{1}{2^{11}C^2}.
	\end{equation*}
	Further, all these events are independent. Hence, using the Borel-Cantelli lemma, the event $\Ecal^{(1)}$ where these occur infinitely often has probability one as well.
	Combining this with equation~\eqref{eq:final_decomposition} implies
	\begin{align*}
		\Ecal^{(0)}\cap\Ecal^{(1)} &\subseteq \Ecal^{(0)}\cap \set{X_{(n)}^n >35C\sqrt n \text{ for infinitely many }n\in\Nbb}\\
		&\subseteq\set{\Ccal_n(\ell_n) \text{ holds for infinitely many }n\in\Nbb}.
	\end{align*}
	Because $\Ecal^{(0)}$ and $\Ecal^{(1)}$ have full probability and $\ell_n=O((\log n)^2)$, this ends the proof.
\end{proof}

Lastly, we turn to positive results that can be achieved by imposing some balance constraint on the empirical $k$-means clusters.
To set this up precisely, we need the following:

\begin{definition}
	For any Voronoi partition $\mathscr{V}$, write
	\begin{equation*}
		p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}) := \min_{\Vcal\in \mathscr{V}}\Pbb(X\in \Vcal)
	\end{equation*}
	for the smallest probability assigned to any of its regions.
\end{definition}

The quantity $p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V})$ can be interpreted as the degree of imbalance of the clusters in $\mathscr{V}$ at the population level.
In fact, it is easy to see that we always have $0 \le p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V})\le 1/\#\mathscr{V}$, and the upper bound is achieved if and only if all clusters have the same mass.

Before giving our positive result, it is instructive to state and prove a fundamental continuity property for the excess distortion function.
Towards this, observe that the quantity $D_X(\pointset\,|\pointset')$ depends not on the random variable $X$ but rather on its distribution; as such, the next result shows that the excess distortion is jointly continuous as a function of this distribution and its two sets of points.
(In other words, this is an extension of \cite[Lemma~4]{JaffeClustering} from $W_2$ to $W_1$ convergence.) 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:D-cts}
	The excess distortion $D$ is a continuous function $(\Wcal_1(\Rbb^d)\times \allpointsets_k\times \allpointsets_k,W_1\times \dhaus\times\dhaus)\to\Rbb$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	For convenience, we write $D_{\mu}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset')$ for the excess distortion when $X$ has distribution $\mu$.
	Now, suppose that $\{(\mu_n,\pointset_n,\pointset_n')\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ and $(\mu,\pointset,\pointset')$ in $\Wcal_1(\Rbb^d)\times \allpointsets_k\times \allpointsets_k$ satisfy $W_1(\mu_n,\mu)\to 0$, $\dhaus(\pointset_n,\pointset)\to 0$, and $\dhaus(\pointset_n',\pointset')\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.
	Then construct a probability space  $(\tilde{\Omega},\tilde{\mathscr{F}},\tilde{\Pbb})$ on which are defined random variables $\{Z_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ and $Z$ such that $Z_n$ has distribution $\mu_n$ for all $n\in\Nbb$ and $Z$ has distribution $\mu$, and such that we have $Z_n\to Z$ holding $\tilde{\Pbb}$-almost surely as well as $\tilde{\Ebb}\|Z_n-Z\|\to 0$.
	(This is essentially an application of Skorokhod's representation theorem; see \cite[Lemma~3]{JaffeClustering} for details.)
	We now claim that
	\begin{equation}\label{eqn:min-diffs}
		\left\{\min_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n-Z_n\|^2-\min_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'-Z_n\|^2\right\}_{n\in\Nbb}
	\end{equation}
	is uniformly integrable.
	To show this, note that the assumptions $\dhaus(\pointset_n,\pointset)\to 0$ and $\dhaus(\pointset_n',\pointset')\to 0$ imply that
	\begin{equation*}
		\left\{\sum_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n\|^2\right\}_{n\in\Nbb},\qquad\left\{\sum_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'\|^2\right\}_{n\in\Nbb},\qquad \textnormal{ and } \qquad\left\{\dhaus(\pointset_n,\pointset_n')\right\}_{n\in\Nbb}
	\end{equation*}
	are uniformly bounded, and observe that we have
	\begin{align*}
		\left|\min_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n-Z_n\|^2-\min_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'-Z_n\|^2\right| \le \sum_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n\|^2 + \sum_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'\|^2 + 2\dhaus(\pointset_n,\pointset_n')\|Z_n\|.
	\end{align*}
	Since $\{Z_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ is uniformly integrable by construction, we conclude that \eqref{eqn:min-diffs} is indeed uniformly integrable.
	Since we also have
	\begin{equation*}
		\min_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n-Z_n\|^2-\min_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'-Z_n\|^2\to \min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-Z\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-Z\|^2
	\end{equation*}
	holding $\tilde{\Pbb}$-almost surely, it follows that we can compute:
	\begin{align*}
		D_{\mu_n}(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_n') &= \int_{\Rbb^d}\left(\min_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n-x\|^2-\min_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'-x\|^2\right)\diff \mu_n(x) \\
		&= \tilde{\Ebb}\left[\min_{c_n\in\pointset_n}\|c_n-Z_n\|^2-\min_{c_n'\in\pointset_n'}\|c_n'-Z_n\|^2\right] \\
		&= \tilde{\Ebb}\left[\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-Z\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-Z\|^2\right] \\
		&= \int_{\Rbb^d}\left(\min_{c\in\pointset}\|c-x\|^2-\min_{c'\in\pointset'}\|c'-x\|^2\right)\diff \mu(x) = D_{\mu}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset').
	\end{align*}
	This finishes the proof.
\end{proof}

Finally we get to our positive result.
Recall that we are in case (i) so we are assuming that an optimal set of (unconstrained) $k$-means cluster centers exists.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:cluster-consistency}
	Suppose $X$ has density with respect to Lebesgue measure, $\E\|X\|<\infty$, and $\#\supp(X)\ge k$, and let
	\begin{equation*}
		\alpha_0(X):=\sup\left\{p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}): \begin{matrix}
			\mathscr{V} \textnormal{ is the Voronoi partition for some}\\
			\textnormal{optimal set of $k$-means cluster centers}
		\end{matrix}\right\} > 0.
	\end{equation*}
	If $0 < \alpha < \alpha_0(X)$, then we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\max_{\bar{\pointset}_n\in \bar{\allpointsets}_{n,k,\alpha n}}\min_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_{k}(X)}\dhaus(\bar\pointset_n,\pointset)\to 0
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely as $n\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	It suffices to show that, if $\{\bar \pointset_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ is any sequence in $\allpointsets_k$, with $\bar \pointset_n\in\bar \allpointsets_{n,k,\alpha n}$, then $\{\bar \pointset_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ is $\dhaus$-precompact and every $\dhaus$-subsequential limit lies in $\allpointsets_{k}(X)$, almost surely.
	For notation, let us write $\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n$ for each $n\in\Nbb$ for the Voronoi partition such that $\{\bar m_n(\Vcal)\}_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n}=\bar \pointset_n$.


	First, we show the required precompactness.
	To do this, we make the following simple calculation for any $\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n$, which is similar to \cite[equation~(14)]{Zhivotovskiy}:

	\begin{equation*}
		\|\bar m_n(\Vcal)\| = \frac{\|\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}\|}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}} \le  \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|X_i\|\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}} \le  \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|X_i\|}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ind\{X_i\in\Vcal\}}
	\end{equation*}
	Since $\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n$ is $\alpha n$-balanced by assumption, we have shown
	\begin{equation*}
		\max_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n}
		\|\bar m_n(\Vcal)\| \le  \frac{1}{\alpha n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|X_i\|=:Y_n
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely.
	But $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ and the strong law of large numbers imply $Y_n\to \Ebb\|X\|$ almost surely, so we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\sup_{n\in\Nbb}\max_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n}
		\|\bar m_n(\Vcal)\| \le \sup_{n\in\Nbb}Y_n < \infty
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely.
	This shows that $\{\bar \pointset_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ are almost surely contained in a bounded set, hence $\dhaus$-compactness follows from $k$ iterated applications of the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem.
	(Alternatively, one may directly apply Blaschke's selection theorem \cite[Theorem~7.3.8]{BuragoBuragoIvanov}.)

	Next we suppose that we have a subsequence $\{n_j\}_{j\in\Nbb}$ and a compact set $\pointset\subseteq \Rbb^d$ such that $\dhaus(\bar \pointset_{n_j},\pointset)\to 0$ almost surely, and let us show $\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k}(X)$.
	To do this, write $\mathscr{V}=\{\Vcal_{\ell}\}_{1\le \ell \le k}$ for the Voronoi partition generated by the cluster centers $\pointset$, and also write $\bar{\mathscr{V}}_{n_j} = \{\Vcal_{j,\ell}\}_{1\le \ell\le k}$.
	We claim that we have

	\begin{align*}
		\Ebb\sqb{\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \ind\{X\in\Vcal_{\ell}\} \|m_X(\Vcal_{\ell})-X\|^2  - \|X\|^2 } \le \liminf_{j\to\infty}\frac{1}{n_j}\sum_{i=1}^{n_j}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\ind\{X\in\Vcal_{j,\ell}\}\left(\|\bar m_{n_j}(\Vcal_{j,\ell})-X_i\|^2  - \|X_i\|^2 \right).
	\end{align*}
	To see this, we apply the same uniform integrability trick as in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:D-cts}, where we also use the fact that $X$ possessing a density implies that we have $\ind\{X\in \Vcal_{j,\ell}\}\to \ind\{X\in \Vcal_{\ell}\}$ holding almost surely, after passing to a subsequence and relabeling, if necessary.
	Then we let $\mathscr{V}':=\{\Vcal'_{\ell}\}_{1\le \ell \le k}$ be an arbitrary Voronoi partition with $p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}') \ge \alpha_0(X)$, and we note that $\alpha<\alpha_0(X)$ implies that $\mathscr{V}'$ is $\alpha n$-balanced for sufficiently large $n\in\Nbb$, almost surely.
	Thus, we apply the displayed equation above, the optimality of $\mathscr{V}$, and the strong law of large numbers, to get:
	\begin{align*}
		D_X(\mathscr{V}) &=\Ebb\sqb{\sum_{\ell=1}^{k} \ind\{X\in\Vcal_{\ell}\} \|m_X(\Vcal_{\ell})-X\|^2  - \|X\|^2 } \\
		&\le \liminf_{j\to\infty}\frac{1}{n_j}\sum_{i=1}^{n_j}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\ind\{X\in\Vcal_{j,\ell}\}\left(\|\bar m_{n_j}(\Vcal_{j,\ell})-X_i\|^2  - \|X_i\|^2 \right) \\
		&= \liminf_{j\to\infty}-\frac{1}{n_j}\sum_{i=1}^{n_j}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\ind\{X\in\Vcal_{j,\ell}\}\|\bar m_{n_j}(\Vcal_{j,\ell})\|^2 \\
		&\le \liminf_{j\to\infty}-\frac{1}{n_j}\sum_{i=1}^{n_j}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\ind\{X\in\Vcal'_{\ell}\}\|\bar m_{n_j}(\Vcal'_{\ell})\|^2 \\
		&=-\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\Pbb(X\in\Vcal'_{\ell})\| m_X(\Vcal'_{\ell})\|^2 \\
		&= D_X(\mathscr{V}').
	\end{align*}
	We have shown that $\mathscr{V}$ minimizes $D_X$ among all Voronoi partitions whose value of $p_{\min}$ is at least $\alpha_0(X)$, and by Lemma~\ref{lemma:equivalence_distortions} this shows $\pointset\in \allpointsets_k(X)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
	The condition that the constant $0<\alpha<1$ is sufficiently small should be interpreted as a type of well-specification.
	However, note that we require $\alpha$ to be smaller than
	\begin{equation*}
		\sup\left\{p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}): \begin{matrix}
			\mathscr{V} \textnormal{ is the Voronoi partition for some}\\
			\textnormal{optimal set of $k$-means cluster centers}
		\end{matrix}\right\}
	\end{equation*}
	rather than
	\begin{equation*}
		\inf\left\{p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}): \begin{matrix}
			\mathscr{V} \textnormal{ is the Voronoi partition for some}\\
			\textnormal{optimal set of $k$-means cluster centers}
		\end{matrix}\right\}.
	\end{equation*}
	These values may disagree in general, since there need not be a unique set of population $k$-means cluster centers for $X$.
	In other words, the well-specification depends on the population-level ``best-case'' imbalance rather than the ``worst-case'' imbalance.
	(Relatedly, see the comments before \cite[Theorem~3.1]{Zhivotovskiy})
\end{remark}


This result combines with the continuity of Lemma~\ref{lem:D-cts} to also yield the following:

\begin{corollary}
	If $X$ has a density with respect to Lebesgue measure, $\E\|X\|<\infty$, $\#\supp(X)\ge k$, and $0 < \alpha < \alpha_0(X)$, then for any fixed $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$ we have
	\begin{equation}
		\max_{\bar{\pointset}_n\in \bar{\allpointsets}_{n,k,\alpha n}}  D_X(\bar{\pointset}_n\,|\,\pointset_0) \to\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)
	\end{equation}
	almost surely as $n\to\infty$.
\end{corollary}



\section{Convergence of Excess Distortion}\label{sec:distortion}

In this section we consider whether the (population-level) excess distortion of the empirical $k$-means cluster centers converge to the infimum of the (population-level) excess distortion in case (ii).
We will show that this guess can fail (Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided_imbalanced}), but also that imposing a certain logarithmic balance constraint recovers such convergence.
We let $X$ denote a random variable such that (ii) holds throughout this section; as a reminder, this means that the optimal excess distortion is finite but there exists no set of optimal $k$-means cluster centers.
We emphasize that, while, convergence of the distortion is well-understood in many settings \cite{BartlettLinderLugosi, BiauDevroyeLugosi, Levrard, Zhivotovskiy}, no literature so far has considered the case of $\Ebb\|X\|^2= \infty$, let alone $\allpointsets_k(X) = \varnothing$.

We begin with a negative result which is similar in spirit to that of Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}. Precisely, we consider the following asymmetric version of the two-sided $\Pareto(2)$ distribution.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:population_level_imbalanced_2_sided}
	For $0 \le p \le 1$, let $X$ be a real-valued random variable which satisfies
	\begin{align*}
		\Pbb(X\geq t) = pt^{-2} &\qquad\textnormal{for all} \qquad t\geq 1,\\
		\Pbb(X\leq -t) = (1-p)t^{-2} &\qquad\textnormal{for all} \qquad t\geq 1.
	\end{align*}
	(In other words, $X=SZ$ where $Z$ and $S$ are independent, $Z\sim\Pareto(2)$, and $S= +1$ with probability $p$ and $S=-1$ with probability $1-p$.)
	For $0 \le p < \sfrac{1}{4}$ or $\sfrac{3}{4}<p\le 1$, we have $\allpointsets_2(X)=\varnothing$ and with $\pointset_0=\{0\}$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_2} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) = -4\paren{\max(p,1-p)+(2p-1)^2}.
	\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	As in Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}, it suffices to consider Voronoi regions of the form $\mathscr{V}(r)=\{(-\infty,r),[r,\infty)\}$, which are generated for instance by the cluster $\pointset(r):=\{r-1,r+1\}$. For convenience, we denote by 
	\begin{equation*}
		D(r):=-\sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}(r)} \Pbb(X\in\Vcal) m_X(\Vcal)^2,
	\end{equation*}
	the corresponding excess distortion with respect to the cluster center $\pointset_0=\{0\}$.
	Using some of the computations from Proposition~\ref{prop:2-sided-pareto-pop}, by direct calculation, we find
	\begin{equation}
		D(r)=\begin{cases}
			-4p - 4(2p-1)^2\frac{\paren{1-\frac{p}{(2p-1)r}}^2}{1-pr^{-2}} &r\geq 1,\\
			-4(1-p) - 4(2p-1)^2\frac{\paren{1-\frac{1-p}{(2p-1)r}}^2}{1-(1-p)r^{-2}} &r\leq -1.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	As a result, we can use that the support of $X$ is on $(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)$ to get:
	\begin{equation*}
		\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_2} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) \overset{(i)}{=} \inf_{r\in(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)}D(r) = -4\max\paren{1,\max(p,1-p)+(2p-1)^2}.
	\end{equation*}            
	Further, $\pointset_2(X)=\varnothing$ whenever the above infimum $\inf_{r\in(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)]}D(r)$ is not attained. This holds for $p\in[0,1/4)\cup(3/4,1]$, for which we obtain the desired formula for the infimum excess distortion.
\end{proof}

In the following result (which is similar to Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}), we show that convergence in excess distortion can fail.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:inconsistency_two_sided_imbalanced}
	For $0 \le p < \sfrac{1}{4}$, let $X$ be a real-valued random variable which satisfies
	\begin{align*}
		\Pbb(X\geq t) = pt^{-2} &\qquad\textnormal{for all} \qquad t\geq 1,\\
		\Pbb(X\leq -t) = (1-p)t^{-2} &\qquad\textnormal{for all} \qquad t\geq 1.
	\end{align*}
	(In other words, $X$ has the asymmetric $\Pareto(2)$ distribution from Proposition~\ref{prop:population_level_imbalanced_2_sided}.)
	Then, there exists a universal constant $c>0$ such that almost surely, for infinitely many $n\in\Nbb$, for any $\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}$ we have $$\max(\bar\pointset_n)\geq c\frac{\sqrt{pn}}{\log n}.$$
	Consequently, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\limsup_{n\to\infty} \min_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} D_X(\bar\pointset_{n}\,|\, \pointset_0) \geq \inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_2} D_X(\pointset\,|\, \pointset_0) +4(1-2p).
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	The proof uses the same tools as in the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}. We also use the same notation for the order statistics $X_{(1)}^n,\ldots,X_{(n)}^n$ and the quantities $S_n$, $\bar X_n$, $\bar D_{n,2}$, and $N_+=\#\set{1\le i \le n:X_i>0}=n-N_-$. Since the distribution of $N_+$ is now a Binomial of parameter $n$ and $p$, we introduce the event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Ecal_n:=\set{\abs{N_+-pn}\leq \frac{pn}{2}},
	\end{equation*}
	which by Hoeffding's inequality has probability at least $1-2e^{-(1-p)^2 n/4}$. Next, for $1\le a\le n$ and $C\geq 1$, we introduce the same event as in Proposition~\ref{prop:inconsistency_two_sided}:
	\begin{multline*}
		\Fcal_n(a;C) := \set{0<X_{(n-b)}^n <  \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{b}} \cdot X_{(n-a)}^n  \textnormal{ for all } a\le b\le N_+ - 1} \\
		\cap \set{0 >X_{(b)}^n > \sqrt{\frac{Ca}{b}} \cdot X_{(a+1)}^n \textnormal{ for all } a+1\le b \le N_-},
	\end{multline*}
	which has probability at least $1-n e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}a}$ using the same arguments (see equation~\eqref{eq:proba_event_F}).
	Next, let
	\begin{equation*}
		\Gcal_n(a;C):=\set{X_{(a+1)}^n \geq -\sqrt{\frac{2(1-p)Cn}{a}}}\cap \set{X_{(n-a)}^n \leq \sqrt{\frac{2pCn}{a}}}.
	\end{equation*}
	Then, equation~\eqref{eq:proba_event_G} still holds, that is, $\Pbb(\Gcal_n(a;C))\geq 1-2C^{-a/4}$.

	We suppose from now that $\Ecal_n$, $\Fcal_n(a;C)$, $\Gcal_n(a;C)$, and $$\Zcal_n^-(a;C):=\set{-X_{(1)}^n \leq \frac{C(1-p)n}{2a} }$$ hold. 
	Then fix $a+1\le i\le N_+$.
	Slightly adjusting the arguments for equation~\eqref{eq:upper_bound_avg}, we obtain
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:upper_n_v2}
		\bar X_n[n-i:n-1] \leq \frac{a}{i}X_{(n)}^n + 4C\sqrt{\frac{pn}{i}}.
	\end{equation}
	Adjusting the arguments for equation~\eqref{eq:lower_bound_mean-} and using $\Ecal_n$, we also obtain
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar X_n[:n-i] \geq \bar X_n[:N_-] \geq \frac{a}{N_-}X_{(1)}^n - 4C\sqrt{\frac{(1-p)n}{N_-}}\overset{(i)}{\geq} \frac{4a}{n}X_{(1)}^n - 8C \geq -10C,
	\end{equation*}
	Therefore, following the arguments from equation~\eqref{eq:decomposition_positive_side} we have for $\ell\geq 16a^2$,
	\begin{align*}
		\Acal_n(\ell)&:=\set{\bar D_{n,2}(i)>\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) \textnormal{ for all } n-N_+ + 1\le i \le n-\ell} \\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Zcal_n^-(a;C) \\
		&\qquad\cap\set{X_{(n)}^n >\frac{2a}{\sqrt i} X_{(n)}^n + 8C\sqrt{pn} + 10C\sqrt i \textnormal{ for all } \ell \le i \le N_+-1}\\
		&\subseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap \Zcal_n^-(a;C)\cap  \set{X_{(n)}^n >50C\sqrt{pn}}.
	\end{align*}
	Next, we turn to indices $i\leq N_-$.
	For $\ell\le i\in N_-$, equation~\eqref{eq:lower_bound_avg-} becomes
	\begin{equation*}
		-\sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[:i]  \leq -\frac{a}{\sqrt i} X_{(1)}^n + 4C\sqrt{(1-p)n} \leq -\frac{1}{2} X_{(1)}^n + 4C\sqrt{n} ,
	\end{equation*}
	where in the last inequality we used $\ell\geq 16a^2$.
	Next, we introduce the event 
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:defnition_tilde_G_n}
		\tilde\Gcal_n(\ell;C):= \set{X_{(b)}^n \geq -\sqrt{\frac{2(1-p)Cn}{b}} \textnormal{ for all }1\le b\le \ell}\supseteq \set{ -\sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[:i] \leq 2\sqrt{2Cn} \textnormal{ for all } 1 \le i \le \ell}
	\end{equation}
	for $C\geq 8$,
	which by Chernoff's inequality and the union bound satisfies
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_tilde_G_event}
		\Pbb(\tilde\Gcal_n^c(\ell;C)) \leq \sum_{1\le b\le \ell}e^{-nD(\frac{b}{n}\parallel \frac{b}{2Cn})} \leq \frac{2}{C^{1/4}-1},
	\end{equation}
	where in the last inequality we used the same bound as in equation~\eqref{eq:proba_event_G}.
	In particular, under $\tilde\Gcal_n(\ell;C)$ the two previous equations show that for $\ell\geq 16a^2$, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_last_term_i}
		-\sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[:i] \leq 5C\sqrt n\textnormal{ for all } 1\le i\le N_{-}.
	\end{equation}
	For $n$ sufficiently large, $a<p\sqrt n/8$, and $\ell\geq 16a^2$, we obtain the following, by using \eqref{eq:useful_comparison},~\eqref{eq:upper_n_v2},~\eqref{eq:bound_last_term_i}, and $\Ecal_n$:
	\begin{align*}
		\Bcal_n(\ell) &:= \set{\bar D_{n,2}(i)>\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) \textnormal{ for all }1 \le i \le N_-}\\
		&\supseteq \set{X_{(n)}^n> 2\sqrt i\cdot  \bar X_n[n-N_+:n-1] - \sqrt i\cdot \bar X_n[:i] \textnormal{ for all }1 \le i \le N_-}\\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C)\cap\tilde \Gcal_n(\ell;C) \cap\set{ X_{(n)}^n >  \frac{2a\sqrt n}{N_+} X_{(n)}^n +8C \sqrt{\frac{pn^2}{N_+}}   +5C\sqrt{n}  \textnormal{ for all } 1\le i \le N_-}\\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap\tilde \Gcal_n(\ell;C)\cap \set{X_{(n)}^n >   40C\sqrt{n}  }.
	\end{align*}
	Finally, we define the event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Hcal_n(\ell;C):=\set{X_{(n-\ell+1)}^n \geq \sqrt{\frac{pn}{2C\ell}}},
	\end{equation*}
	which, using the same arguments as in equation~\eqref{eq:proba_event_H}, satisfies $\Pbb(\Hcal_n(\ell;C)\mid\Ecal_n) \geq 1-e^{-\frac{(C-1)^2}{C}\ell}$ provided that $\ell<pn/2$.

	In summary, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\Ccal_n(\ell)&:=\set{\max(\bar\pointset_n)\geq \sqrt{\frac{pn}{2C\ell}} \textnormal{ for all } \bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}}\\
		&\supseteq\set{\max(\bar\pointset_n)\geq X_{(n-\ell+1)}^n \textnormal{ for all } \bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} \cap \Hcal_n(\ell;C)\\
		&\supseteq \set{\bar D_{n,2}(n-1) < \min_{1\le i \le n-\ell} \bar D_{n,2}(i) }\cap \Hcal_n(\ell;C) = \Acal_n(\ell)\cap\Bcal_n(\ell)\cap \Hcal_n(\ell;C) \\
		&\supseteq \Ecal_n\cap\Fcal_n(a;C)\cap\Gcal_n(a;C) \cap\tilde \Gcal_n(\ell;C)\cap \Hcal_n(\ell;C)\cap \Zcal_n^-(a;C)\cap\set{X_{(n)}^n >   50C\sqrt{n}  }.
	\end{align*}
	Using the failure probability bounds for each event, there exists $c_0>0$ such that choosing $a_n=\ceil{c_0\log n}$, $\ell_n=16a_n^2$, and $C=8$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{n\in\Nbb} \bigg(\Pbb(\Ecal_n^c) + \Pbb(\Fcal_n^{c}(a_n;C)\,|\,\Ecal_n) + \Pbb(\Gcal_n^c(a_n;C)) + \Pbb(\Hcal_n^c(\ell_n;C)\mid \Ecal_n)\bigg) <\infty.
	\end{equation*}
	Hence, by the Borel-Cantelli lemma, there is an event $\Ecal^{(0)}$ of probability one such that for large enough $n$, all of the events $\Ecal_n$, $\Fcal_n(a_n;C)$, $\Gcal_n(a_n;C)$, $\Hcal_n(\ell_n;C)$ are satisfied.
	Next, for $n$ sufficiently large, we have $\tilde \Gcal_n(\ell;C)\subseteq \Zcal_n^-(a;C)$. Therefore, we will focus on the events $\Ical(n):=\tilde \Gcal_n(\ell_n;C) \cap\set{X_{(n)}^n >   50C\sqrt{n}  }$ from now onward.
	We construct by induction a random sequence $\{N_k\}_{k\in\Nbb}$, where $N_1=1$, and, having defined $N_{k-1}$, we let $N_k$ be the smallest integer such that $N_k\geq 4N_{k-1}$ and
	\begin{equation*}
		X_{(1)}^{2N_{k-1}} > -\sqrt{\frac{2(1-p)CN_k}{\ell_{N_k}}}.
	\end{equation*}
	Such an integer $N_k$ exists since the right-hand side diverges to $-\infty$ as $N_k\to\infty$.

	By construction, we use equation~\eqref{eq:bound_tilde_G_event} to bound, for any $k\in\Nbb$:
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb\left(\tilde \Gcal_{N_k}(\ell_{n_k};C)  \mid N_k,X_1,\ldots,X_{2N_{k-1}}\right) \geq \Pbb\left(\tilde \Gcal_{N_k}(\ell_{N_k};C)\mid N_k\right) \geq 1-\frac{2}{C^{1/4}-1},
	\end{equation*}
	Next, for $k\in\Nbb$ sufficiently large, use $N_k/2\geq 2N_{k-1}$ to get
	\begin{align*}
		&\Pbb\left( X_{(N_k)}^{N_k}>50C\sqrt {N_k} \mid N_k,X_1,\ldots,X_{2N_{k-1}},\tilde \Gcal_{N_k}(\ell_{N_k};C)\right) \\
		&\geq\Pbb\left( \max_{N_k/2<i\leq N_k} X_i>50C\sqrt {N_k} \mid N_k,X_1,\ldots,X_{2N_{k-1}},\tilde \Gcal_{N_k}(\ell_{N_k};C)\right)\\
		&= \Pbb\left( \max_{N_k/2<i\leq N_k} X_i>50C\sqrt {N_k} \mid N_k\right)  =\paren{1-\frac{p}{(50C)^2 N_k}}^{N_k/2} \geq \frac{p}{2^{16}C^2}.
	\end{align*}
	As a result, for $k$ sufficiently large, we obtained
	\begin{equation*}
		\Pbb(\Ical(N_k)\mid N_k,X_1,\ldots,X_{2N_{k-1}}) \geq c_0p
	\end{equation*}
	for some universal constant $c_0>0$. Note that all the events $\Ical(N_1),\ldots,\Ical(N_{k-1})$ are $\sigma(N_k,X_1,\ldots,X_{2N_{k-1}})$-measurable.
	Hence, using a variant of Borel-Cantelli (or the Azuma-Hoeffding inequality) implies that the event $\Ecal^{(1)}$ on which the events $\Ical(N_k)$ occur for infinitely many $k\in\Nbb$ has probability one.
	On $\Ecal^{(0)}\cap\Ecal^{(1)}$ which has probability one, the event $\Ccal_n(\ell_n)$ occurs infinitely often.
	Consequently, writing $D(r)$ for the excess distortion for the partition $\{(-\infty,r),[r,\infty)\}$, as defined in Proposition~\ref{prop:population_level_imbalanced_2_sided}, and using the computations from the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:population_level_imbalanced_2_sided}, we get that the following holds on $\Ecal^{(0)}\cap\Ecal^{(1)}$:
	\begin{equation*}
		\limsup_{n\to\infty} \min_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,2}} D_X(\bar\pointset_{n}\,|\,\pointset_0) \geq \lim_{r\to\infty} D(r)=-4(p+(2p-1)^2) = \inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_2}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) + 4(1-2p),
	\end{equation*}
	This ends the proof.
\end{proof}

Next we turn to positive results, which will essentially be consequences of the following uniform convergence result for the distortion on any Voronoi partition.
The proof of this result uses the fact that any Voronoi region in a Voronoi partition into $k$ pieces can be written as an intersections of at most $k-1$ half-spaces, which have finite VC dimension $O(dk\log k)$ \cite{blumer1989learnability}.
We recall the definition of $\bar{\mathscr{P}}_{n,k,\gamma}$ given in Definition~\ref{def:balance-k-means-emp}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:uniform_convergence_voronoi}
	There exists a universal constant $c>0$ such that the following holds.
	If $X$ satisfies $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ and $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)>-\infty$ for some (equivalently all) $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$, then there exists a constant $C>0$, depending only on the distribution of $X$, such that for any $n\in\Nbb$, $k\geq 2$, $\delta\in(0,1]$, and $\gamma\geq \frac{c}{\delta}(dk\log k\log n+\log \frac{1}{\delta})^2$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V}):=\max\paren{  \abs{\bar D_n(\mathscr{V}) -  D_X(\mathscr{V}) }, \sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}} \mu_X(\Vcal)\|\bar m_n(\Vcal)-m_X(\Vcal)\|^2  }\leq Ck \frac{\sqrt{dk\log k\log n + \log\frac{1}{\delta}}}{(\gamma\delta)^{1/4}},
	\end{equation*}
	for all  $\mathscr{V}\in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma}$, with probability at least $1-\delta$.
	Further, suppose that $\{\beta_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ satisfies $\sum_{n\in\Nbb}(n\beta_n)^{-1}<\infty$, and that $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ satisfies $\gamma_n\geq \beta_n(\log n)^2$ for all $n\in\Nbb$; then, almost surely, there exists (random) $N$ such that $n\ge N$ implies
	\begin{equation*}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V})  \leq C \frac{k\beta_n^{1/4}\sqrt{\log n}}{\gamma_n^{1/4}}
	\end{equation*}
	for all $\mathscr{V}\in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	By assumption, we have $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D(\pointset\, | \, \pointset_0)>-\infty$.
	From Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj} this implies that there exists a constant $r>0$ (depending only on the distribution of $X$, and, specifically, not on $k$) such that we have $\Pbb(\|X\|\geq t)\leq r t^{-2}$ for all $t\geq 0$.

	Let $\Hcal_k$ be the set of all intersections of at most $k$ half-spaces in $\Rbb^d$.
	From \cite{blumer1989learnability}, the VC dimension of $\Hcal_k$ is bounded above by $c_0 dk\log k$ for some universal constant $c_0>0$.
	Also, let $\bar \mu_n$ denote the empirical measure of the samples $X_1,\ldots, X_n$.
	Then, the classical uniform convergence theorem for VC classes (e.g. \cite[Theorem 5.1]{boucheron2005theory}) implies that, with probability $1-\delta$,
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:main_H_event}
		\abs{\Pbb(X\in H) - \bar \mu_n(H)} \leq c_1\sqrt{\bar\mu_n(H) \frac{dk\log k \log n  + \log\frac{1}{\delta}}{n} } + c_1 \frac{dk\log k \log n + \log \frac{1}{\delta}}{n},\quad H\in\Hcal_k,
	\end{equation}
	for some universal constant $c_1>0$. We denote this event by $\Ecal_n(\delta)$. For convenience, we denote $C_\delta(n):=dk\log k\log n+\log\frac{1}{\delta}$. In particular, there exists a sufficiently large universal constant $c_2\geq 1$ such that the following holds. Under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, any $H\in\Hcal_k$ with $\bar\mu_n(H)\geq c_2 \frac{C_\delta(n)}{n}$ satisfies
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:uniform_concentration_H_k}
		\frac{\Pbb(X\in H)}{1+ 2c_1\sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_n(H)}}}\leq \bar\mu_n(H) \leq \paren{1+ 2c_1\sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_n(H)}}} \Pbb(X\in H).
	\end{equation}
	We use equation~\eqref{eq:uniform_concentration_H_k} to bound the deviation of the excess distortion for any Voronoi partition $\mathscr{V}$ with $1\le \#\mathscr{V}\le k$. Indeed, denoting by $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$ a set of clusters points associated with the Voronoi partition $\mathscr{V}$, note that for any Voronoi region $\Vcal=\Vcal_\pointset(c)$ for $c\in\pointset$ is an intersection of at most $k-1$ half-spaces:
	\begin{equation*}
		\Vcal = \bigcap_{c'\in\pointset\setminus\{c\}} \set{x\in\Rbb^d: 2(c'-c)^\top x \leq \|c'\|^2 - \|c\|^2}\in\Hcal_{k-1}.
	\end{equation*}
	We now compare $\bar D_n(\mathscr{V})$ and $D_X(\mathscr{V})$ under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$. Suppose that each Voronoi region from $\mathscr{V}$ contains at least $\max(c_2,4c_1^2)C_\delta(n)$ samples, that is, $\mathscr{V}\in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma}$ for $\gamma = \max(c_2,4c_1^2)C_\delta(n)$.
	Then, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, since $\Vcal \in \Hcal_k$, equation~\eqref{eq:uniform_concentration_H_k} gives
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower_bound_nb_points_per_cluster}
		\frac{\Pbb(X\in \Vcal)}{1+ 2c_1\sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)}}} \leq \bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \leq  \paren{1+ 2c_1\sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)}}} \Pbb(X\in \Vcal).
	\end{equation}
	In particular, since $\Vcal$ contains at least $4c_1^2 C_\delta(n)$ samples, we also have $\Pbb(X\in\Vcal)/2 \leq \bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \leq 2\Pbb(X\in\Vcal)$. 

	Most of the work to compare $\bar D_n(\mathscr{V})$ and $D_X(\mathscr{V})$ will be in comparing $m_X(\Vcal)$ and $\bar m_n(\Vcal)$ for any fixed $\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}$.
	This requires some preliminary estimates.
	To do so, consider a unit-norm vector $e\in \Rbb^d$ to be fixed later, and divide the samples in $\Vcal$ into the two following sets:
	\begin{align*}
		\Scal^+(\Vcal;e)&:= \{e^\top X_i: 1\le i \le n,\, X_i\in \Vcal,\, e^\top X_i> 0\},\\
		\Scal^-(\Vcal;e)&:= \{-e^\top X_i: 1\le i \le n,\, X_i\in \Vcal,\, e^\top X_i<0\}.
	\end{align*}
	For convenience, we order these sets writing $\Scal^+(\Vcal;e)=:\{z_1^+<\ldots<z_{\ell^+}^+\}$ and $\Scal^-(\Vcal;e)=:\{z_1^-<\ldots<z_{\ell^-}^-\}$, where $\ell^+:=|\Scal^+(\Vcal;e)|$ and $\ell^-:=|\Scal^-(\Vcal;e)|$. In words, we order the samples falling in the Voronoi partition of $c$ by their projection onto the unit-vector $e$.
	The upshot of these definitions is that
	\begin{equation*}
		e^\top  \bar m_n(\Vcal) = \frac{1}{n\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)} \sum_{i=1}^{n} e^\top X_i \cdot \ind\{X_i\in \Vcal\},
	\end{equation*}
	and as a result, letting $z_0^+=z_0^-=0$, we have
	\begin{multline}\label{eq:bar_m_expression_useful}
		\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)\cdot e^\top \bar m_n(\Vcal)
		= \underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{\ell^+} (z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+) \cdot \bar\mu_n\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x \geq z_j^+\} }}_{:=\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e)}\\
		-\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{l^-} (z_j^--z_{j-1}^-) \cdot \bar\mu_n\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: -e^\top x \geq z_j^-\} }}_{:=\bar A^-_n(\Vcal;e)}.
	\end{multline}
	By construction, each term of both sums is non-negative. Further, all sets on the right-hand side belong to $\Hcal_k$. We treat each sum separately. Fix a parameter $\alpha\geq \max(1,4c_1^2/c_2)$.
	Next, we treat separately the sets in the sum that do not contain at least $\alpha c_2 C_\delta(n)$ samples; for convenience, we denote by $\tilde  \ell^+$ the largest index $j$ for which $n\bar\mu_n\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x \geq z_j^+\} }\geq \alpha c_2C_\delta(n)$. Then, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, equation~\eqref{eq:uniform_concentration_H_k} yields
	\begin{align*}
		\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e)\leq \sum_{j=1}^{\tilde \ell^+} (z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+)\paren{1+\frac{2c_1}{\sqrt{c_2\alpha}}} \mu\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x \geq z_j^+\} }   + z_{l^+}^+ \cdot \alpha\frac{c_2C_\delta(n)}{n}.
	\end{align*}
	Now note that
	\begin{align*}
		\sum_{j=1}^{\tilde \ell^+} (z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+) \mu\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x \geq z_j^+\} } \leq  \Ebb\sqb{e ^\top X \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\} \ind\{e^\top X> 0\}}.
	\end{align*}
	On the other hand, equation~\eqref{eq:uniform_concentration_H_k} implies that we have the following under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$:
	\begin{align*}
		\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e) =\sum_{j=1}^{\ell^+} &(z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+) \cdot \bar\mu_n\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x > z_{j-1}^+\} } \notag \\
		&\geq \sum_{j=1}^{\tilde \ell^+} (z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+)\paren{1-\frac{2c_1}{\sqrt{c_2\alpha}}} \mu\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x > z_{j-1}^+\} }.
	\end{align*}
	Also, letting $y^+:= z^+_{\tilde \ell^+}$ we have
	\begin{align*}
		\sum_{j=1}^{\tilde \ell^+} (z_j^+-z_{j-1}^+) \mu\paren{\Vcal\cap \{x\in\Rbb^d: e^\top x > z_{j-1}^+\} } \geq  \Ebb\sqb{e ^\top X \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\} \ind\{0< e^\top X\leq y^+\}}.
	\end{align*}
	Therefore, defining $A^+(\Vcal;e):= \Ebb\sqb{e ^\top X \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\}\ind\{e^\top X > 0\}}$ and putting all four previous equations together shows that, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, we have
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_A+_term_1}
		\abs{\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e) -  A^+(\Vcal;e) } \leq \frac{4c_1}{\sqrt{c_2\alpha}} A^+(\Vcal;e) + \max_{1\le i\le n} \|X_i\| \cdot \alpha\frac{c_2C_\delta(n)}{n} + \Ebb\sqb{\|X\| \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\}\ind\{e^\top X> y^+\}},
	\end{equation}
	where we upper-bounded some terms $|e^\top X_i|$ with $\|X_i\|$ for $1\le i \le n$
	We continue bounding the terms on the right-hand side. First, we recall by Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj} that $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by $\sqrt{r}\cdot Z$, where $Z\sim\Pareto(2)$.
	Then, Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm} implies
	\begin{align}\label{eq:upper_bound_Pareto_argument}
		A^+(\Vcal;e) \leq \Ebb\sqb{ \|X\| \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\} } \leq 2\sqrt{r \Pbb(X\in \Vcal)}.
	\end{align}
	Next, by construction of $y^+$, the region $\Vcal\cap\{x:e^\top x>y^+\}$ contains at most $\alpha c_2C_\delta(n)$ samples. Therefore, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, equation~\eqref{eq:main_H_event} implies that
	\begin{equation*}
		\mu\paren{\Vcal\cap\{x\in\Rbb^d:e^\top x>y^+\} } \leq c_3 \frac{C_\delta(n)}{n},
	\end{equation*}
	for some universal constant $c_3>0$, and where $\mu$ denotes the law of $X$.
	Then, we again use Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm} to obtain
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_edge_case}
		\Ebb\sqb{\|X\| \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\}\ind\{e^\top X> y^+\}} \leq 2\sqrt{r \cdot \mu\paren{\Vcal\cap\{x\in\Rbb^d:e^\top x>y^+\} }} \leq 2 \sqrt{\frac{c_3 r C_\delta(n)}{n}}.
	\end{equation}
	In summary, combining equations~\eqref{eq:bound_A+_term_1},~\eqref{eq:upper_bound_Pareto_argument}, and~\eqref{eq:bound_edge_case} we obtained
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_A+_term}
		\abs{\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e) -  A^+(\Vcal;e) } \leq 4c_1\sqrt{\frac{r \Pbb(X\in\Vcal)}{c_2\alpha}} + 2 \sqrt{\frac{c_3 r C_\delta(n)}{n}} + \max_{1\le i \le n} \|X_i\| \cdot \alpha\frac{c_2C_\delta(n)}{n}.
	\end{equation}
	under the event $\Ecal_n(\delta)$.
	If we define $A^-(\Vcal;e):= \Ebb\sqb{-e ^\top X \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\}\ind\{e^\top X < 0\}}$, then, by symmetry, the same arguments as above show that we have
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_A-_term}
		\abs{\bar A^-_n(\Vcal;e) -  A^-(\Vcal;e) } \leq 4c_1\sqrt{\frac{r\Pbb(X\in\Vcal)}{c_2\alpha}} + 2 \sqrt{\frac{c_3 r C_\delta(n)}{n}} + \max_{1\le i \le n} \|X_i\| \cdot \alpha\frac{c_2C_\delta(n)}{n}.
	\end{equation}
	under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$.

	Now we turn to comparing $m_X(\Vcal)$ and $\bar m_n(\Vcal)$, for fixed $\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}$.
	Now note that by construction and from equation~\eqref{eq:bar_m_expression_useful}, we have
	\begin{align}\label{eq:relate_to_original_sums}
		\begin{cases}
			A^+(\Vcal;e) - A^-(\Vcal;e) =\Ebb\sqb{e ^\top X \cdot\ind\{X\in \Vcal\}} = \Pbb(X\in\Vcal)\cdot e ^\top m_X(\Vcal); \\
			\bar A^+_n(\Vcal;e) - \bar A^-_n(\Vcal;e) = \bar \mu_n(\Vcal)\cdot e ^\top \bar m_n(\Vcal).
		\end{cases}
	\end{align}
	We next define $e:=\frac{\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal) }{\|\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal) \|}$ if $\bar m_n(\Vcal) \neq  m_X(\Vcal) $ and otherwise fix $e\in \Rbb^d$ arbitrarily.
	Then, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$ and for any $\alpha\geq 1$, we may use equations~\eqref{eq:lower_bound_nb_points_per_cluster},~\eqref{eq:relate_to_original_sums}, ~\eqref{eq:bound_A+_term}, and~\eqref{eq:bound_A-_term} to get 
	\begin{equation*}
		|\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) - \mu_X(\Vcal)|\leq \frac{4c_1}{\sqrt n} \frac{\mu_X(\Vcal)}{\sqrt{\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)}}\leq \frac{4c_1}{\sqrt n} \sqrt{2\mu(\Vcal)}.
	\end{equation*}       
	Hence
	\begin{align}
		\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) &\norm{\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal) }
		=\abs{\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \cdot e^\top \paren{\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal) }} \notag \\
		&\leq \abs{\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \cdot e^\top \bar m_n(\Vcal) - \mu(\Vcal)\cdot e ^\top m_X(\Vcal) }+ |\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) - \mu(\Vcal)|\cdot \|m_X(\Vcal)\|\notag \\
		&\leq 8c_1\sqrt{\frac{r \mu_X(\Vcal) }{c_2\alpha}} + 4 \sqrt{\frac{c_3 r C_\delta(n)}{n}} + \max_{1\le i \le n} \|X_i\| \cdot \alpha\frac{2c_2C_\delta(n)}{n} + 8c_1 \sqrt{\frac{\mu_X(\Vcal)}{n}}\cdot \|m_X(\Vcal)\|. \label{eq:bound_deviation_m}
	\end{align}
	Now let $\gamma\geq \max(c_2,4c_1^2)C_\delta(n)$. Then, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, for any $\mathscr{V}\in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma}$ and $\alpha\geq 1$,
	\begin{align*}
		&\abs{ \bar D_n(\mathscr{V}) -  D_X(\mathscr{V})} \\
		&\leq  \sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}} \abs{\mu(\Vcal) \|m_X(\Vcal)\|^2 - \bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \|\bar m_n(\Vcal)\|^2}\\
		&\leq \sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}} \paren{|\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) - \mu(\Vcal)| \cdot \|m_X(\Vcal)\|^2 + \bar\mu_n(\Vcal) \|m_X(\Vcal)\|\cdot \|\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal)\| + \bar\mu_n(\Vcal)  \|\bar m_n(\Vcal) - m_X(\Vcal)\|^2}
	\end{align*}
	Now use equation~\eqref{eq:bound_deviation_m}, the fact that $ \mu(\Vcal)/2 \leq \bar\mu_n(\Vcal)\leq 2 \mu(\Vcal)$ from \eqref{eq:lower_bound_nb_points_per_cluster}, and the previous bound on $|\bar\mu_n(\Vcal) - \mu(\Vcal)|$, to get that there exists a universal constant $c_4>0$ with
	\begin{multline*}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V})\leq c_4\sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}} \left(\sqrt{\frac{\bar \mu_n(\Vcal)}{n}}  \|m_X(\Vcal)\|^2 + \sqrt{r\paren{\frac{\bar \mu_n(\Vcal)}{\alpha} + \frac{C_\delta(n)}{n}}}\|m_X(\Vcal)\|+ \frac{r}{\alpha} + \frac{r C_\delta(n)}{n\mu(\Vcal)} \right.  \\
		\left. + \frac{\|m_X(\Vcal)\|^2}{n} +\max_{1\le i \le n}\|X_i\|\cdot \frac{\alpha C_\delta(n)}{n}  \|m_X(\Vcal)\|+ \max_{1\le i\le n}\|X_i\|^2\cdot \frac{\alpha^2 C_\delta(n)^2}{n^2\mu(\Vcal)} \right).
	\end{multline*}
	Next, since $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by $\sqrt{r}\cdot Z$ where $Z\sim\Pareto(2)$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm} implies that for any $c\in\pointset$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\|m_X(\Vcal)\| \leq 2\sqrt{\frac{r}{\mu(\Vcal)}}.
	\end{equation*}
	Together with the identity $ \mu(\Vcal)/2 \leq \bar\mu_n(\Vcal)\leq 2 \mu(\Vcal)$, this implies that under $\Ecal_n(\delta)$, letting $Z_n^{\max}:=\max_{1\le i\le n}\|X_i\|/\sqrt{r}$, we have
	\begin{align}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V})
		&\leq c_5 r\sum_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}} \paren{\sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_X(\Vcal)}} +\frac{C_\delta(n)}{n\bar\mu_X(\Vcal)} +\frac{1}{\alpha} + \frac{\alpha C_\delta(n) Z_n^{\max}}{n\sqrt{\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)}} +\frac{\alpha^2 (C_\delta(n))^2 (Z_n^{\max})^2}{n^2\bar\mu_n(\Vcal)}} \notag \\
		&\leq c_6 kr\paren{ \sqrt{\frac{C_\delta(n)}{ \gamma}} + \frac{1}{\alpha} +\frac{\alpha C_\delta(n) Z_n^{\max}}{\sqrt{\gamma n}} + \paren{\frac{\alpha C_\delta(n) Z_n^{\max}}{\sqrt{\gamma n}}}^2 }, \label{eq:distortion_bound_v2}
	\end{align}
	for some universal constants $c_5,c_6>0$. It now only remains to bound $Z_n^{\max}$ which is stochastically dominated by the maximum of $n$ i.i.d. Pareto random variables.
	To bound these, we introduce the following event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Fcal_n(\delta):=\set{Z_n^{\max} \leq \sqrt{\frac{n}{\delta}}},
	\end{equation*}
	Since Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj} implies $\Pbb(\|X\|\geq \sqrt{r x})\leq 1/x$, we have $\Pbb(\Fcal_n(\delta))\geq 1-\delta$. In summary, under $\Ecal_n(\delta)\cap\Fcal_n(\delta)$ which holds with probability $1-2\delta$, we can further the bound from equation~\eqref{eq:distortion_bound_v2} as follows. There exists universal constants $c_8,c_9>0$ such that for $\gamma \geq c_8 C_\delta(n)^2/\delta$, using $\alpha = (\gamma\delta/C_\delta(n)^2)^{1/4}$, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V}) \leq \frac{c_9}{2}kr \paren{\frac{1}{\alpha} + \frac{\alpha C_\delta(n)}{\sqrt{\gamma\delta}}} = c_9 k r \paren{\frac{(C_\delta(n))^2}{\gamma\delta}}^{1/4}
	\end{align*}
	for all $\mathscr{V} \in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma}$.
	This ends the proof of the first claim.

	We next turn to almost-sure bounds.
	To do so, let $\{\beta_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ be any increasing and non-negative sequence such that $\sum_{n\in\Nbb} (n\beta_n)^{-1}<\infty$.
	By assumption,
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{n\in\Nbb} \Pbb\left(\|X_n\| \geq \sqrt{r n\beta_n}\right) = \sum_{n\in\Nbb} \frac{1}{n\beta_n}<\infty.
	\end{equation*}
	So, Borel-Cantelli implies that, on an event $\Gcal$ of probability one, there exists $N$ such that for all $n\geq N$, we have $\|X_n\|\leq \sqrt{rn\beta_n}$ and $\Ecal_n(n^{-2})$ holds. Note that under $\Gcal$, there exists $N_1$ such that we have
	\begin{equation*}
		Z_n^{\max} \leq  \sqrt{n\beta_n} +Z_{\hat n}^{\max} \leq 2\sqrt{n\beta_n},\qquad \textnormal{ for all } n\geq N_1.
	\end{equation*}
	Therefore, there exists universal constants $c_{10},c_{11}>0$ such that for any sequence $\gamma_n\geq \beta_n(\log n)^2$, using $\alpha_n = \gamma_n^{1/4}\beta_n^{-1/4}(\log n)^{1/2}$, the following holds. Under $\Gcal$, for $n$ sufficiently large, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\Delta(\mathscr{V}) \leq c_{10} kr \paren{\frac{1}{\alpha_n} + \frac{\alpha_n C_{1/n^2}(n)\sqrt{\beta_n}}{\sqrt{\gamma_n}}} \leq c_{11} k r \paren{\frac{\beta_n (\log n)^2}{\gamma_n}}^{1/4}
	\end{equation*}
	for all $\mathscr{V} \in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}$.
	This ends the proof.
\end{proof}

Now we can state our main positive result in this section.
Recall that we are assuming to be in case (i) or (ii), meaning $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)>-\infty$.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:consistency_log_points_per_cluster}
	Suppose that $\E\|X\|<\infty$ and $\#\supp(X)\ge k$. Then, for any sequence $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ such that $(\log n)^2\ll \gamma_n\ll n$ as $n\to\infty$, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\max_{\bar{\pointset}_n\in \bar{\allpointsets}_{n,k,\gamma_n}}  D_X(\bar{\pointset}_n\,|\,\pointset_0) \overset{\Pbb}{\to}\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0).
	\end{equation*}
	Further, for any sequence $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ such that $(\log n)^4\le \gamma_n\ll n$, we have            \begin{equation*}
		\max_{\bar{\pointset}_n\in \bar{\allpointsets}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\bar{\pointset}_n\,|\,\pointset_0) \to\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0).
	\end{equation*}
	almost surely, for each $\pointset_0\in\allpointsets_k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
	Let $\pointset_0=\{0\}$. Fix a sequence $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ such that $\gamma_n=o(n)$. We define the event
	\begin{equation*}
		\Ecal:=\set{\lim_{n\to\infty} \inf_{\mathscr{V} \in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_{X}(\mathscr V) =\inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)}.
	\end{equation*}
	We briefly argue that $\Ecal$ has probability one. First, from Lemma~\ref{lemma:equivalence_distortions}, the right-hand side in the definition of $\Ecal$ is equal to the infimum excess distortion $D_X(\mathscr{V})$ over Voronoi partitions $\mathscr{V}$ with $1\le\#\mathscr{V}\le k$.
	Then, fix any sequence of partitions $\mathscr{V}_{\ell} $ for $\ell\geq 1$ with $1\le\#\mathscr{V}_{\ell}\le k$ such that $D_X(\mathscr{V}_{\ell})\to \inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} \widetilde D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)$ as $\ell\to\infty$. Up to deleting and merging some Voronoi regions, we may also assume without loss of generality that, for each $\ell\geq 1$, each region of $\mathscr{V}_{\ell}$ satisfies $p_{\min}(X,\mathscr{V}_{\ell}) > 0$.
	Then, by the strong law of large numbers, with probability one, for each $\ell\geq 1$ we have we have $\liminf_{n\to\infty}\min_{\Vcal\in\mathscr{V}_{\ell}} \bar\mu_n(\mathscr{V}_{\ell})>0$.
	Therefore, since $\gamma_n=o(n)$, for any $\ell\geq 1$ for $n$ sufficiently large we have $\mathscr{V}_{\ell}\in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}$. Under this event of probability one, the event $\Ecal$ then holds.

	For convenience, we now define
	\begin{equation*}
		\Delta_{\max}(n):=\sup_{\mathscr{V}\in\bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} \Delta(\mathscr{V}),
	\end{equation*}
	where $\Delta(\mathscr{V})$ is defined in Lemma~\ref{lemma:uniform_convergence_voronoi}. We denote $\Fcal=\{\lim_{n\to\infty}\Delta_{\max}(n)=0\}$.
	Now consider any cluster $\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma_n}$ and denote by $\bar{\mathscr V}_n$ a $\gamma_n$-balanced $k$-means clustering Voronoi partition for which $\bar\pointset_n=\bar m_n(\bar{\mathscr V}_n)$. Then, we have
	\begin{align*}
		D_X(\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n)-\Delta_{\max}(n) \leq \bar D_n(\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n) = \inf_{\mathscr{V} \in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} \bar D_n(\mathscr{V}) \leq  \inf_{\mathscr{V} \in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\mathscr{V}) +\Delta_{\max}(n).
	\end{align*}
	Since $\bar{\mathscr V}_n\in  \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}$, we obtained
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:bound_distortion_emp}
		\abs{D_X(\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n) -\inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)} \leq 2\Delta_{\max}(n) + \abs{\inf_{\mathscr{V} \in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\mathscr{V}) - \inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)}.
	\end{equation}
	Next, note that
	\begin{align*}
		D_X(\bar\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) &= \Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in \bar m_n(\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n)} \|c-X\|^2-\|X\|^2}\\
		&\leq \Ebb\sqb{\sum_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr V}_n} \paren{\|\bar m_n(\Vcal)-X\|^2-\|X\|^2}\ind\{X\in\Vcal\}}\\
		&=\Ebb\sqb{\sum_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr V}_n} \paren{\|m_X(\Vcal)-X\|^2-\|X\|^2}\ind\{X\in\Vcal\}} + \sum_{\Vcal\in\bar{\mathscr V}_n} \Pbb(X\in \Vcal) \|\bar m_n(\Vcal)-m_X(\Vcal)\|^2\\
		&\leq D_X(\bar{\mathscr{V}}_n) + \Delta_{\max}(n).
	\end{align*}
	Together with equation~\eqref{eq:bound_distortion_emp}, we showed that
	\begin{equation}\label{eq:final_bound_deviation}
		\max_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\bar\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) -\inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) \leq  3\Delta_{\max}(n) + \abs{\inf_{\mathscr{V} \in \bar{\mathscr P}_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\mathscr{V}) - \inf_{\pointset\in \allpointsets_k} D_{X}(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)}
	\end{equation}
	On $\Ecal\cap\Fcal$, the right-hand side converges to $0$ as $n\to\infty$. We recall that $\Ecal$ has probability one. Also, from Lemma~\ref{lemma:uniform_convergence_voronoi}, we have $\Pbb(\Fcal)=1$ whenever $\gamma_n\geq (\log n)^4$, which gives the desired almost-sure result.

	For the result in convergence in probability, Lemma~\ref{lemma:uniform_convergence_voronoi} shows that whenever $\gamma_n\gg (\log n)^2$, we have $\Delta_{\max}(n)\to 0$ in probability as $n\to\infty$.
	Hence, since $\Ecal$ has probability one, the right-hand side of equation~\eqref{eq:final_bound_deviation} converges in probability to $0$.
	This ends the proof.
\end{proof}

The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_log_points_per_cluster} reveals that the condition $\gamma\ge (\log n)^4$ guaranteeing almost sure convergence can be reduced at least to $\gamma_n\ge (\log n)^3(\log \log n)^2$.
It is not clear what to expect the sharpest possible rate for $\gamma_n$ to be, but this would be an interesting question for future work.


Lastly, we note that Theorem~\ref{thm:cluster-consistency} and Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_log_points_per_cluster} can, in some sense, be unified.
This leads us to the following:

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:consistency_unified}
Suppose that $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ and $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)>-\infty$.
For any sequence $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ such that $(\log n)^4\le \gamma_n \ll n$, the following holds almost surely:
For any sequence $\{\bar\pointset_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ such that $\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma_n}$ for all $n\in \Nbb$, we can decompose $\bar\pointset_n = \Acal_n\sqcup \Bcal_n$ so that $\Acal_n\neq\varnothing$ and
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{1\le k'\le k}\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus( \Acal_n,\pointset)\to 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal_n}\|c\|\to\infty
\end{equation*}
as $n\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

Most of the heavy-lifting is done in the following result, which we remark is completely deterministic.
It states that, among any sequence of sets of cluster centers which approximate the optimal excess distortion, the collection of all bounded centers must converge to a set of population-level $k'$-means cluster centers, for some $1\le k'\le k$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:can_delete_diverging_clusters}
Suppose that $\Ebb\|X\|<\infty$ and $\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)>-\infty$. Consider a sequence of clusters $\pointset_n\in\allpointsets_k$ for $n\in\Nbb$ such that
\begin{equation*}
	\lim_{n\to\infty}D_X(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0)= \inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0).
\end{equation*}
Then, there exists a decomposition $\pointset_n=\Acal_n\sqcup \Bcal_n$ for $n\in\Nbb$ so that $\Acal_n\neq\varnothing$ and
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{1\le k'\le k}\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus(\Acal_n,\pointset)\to 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal_n}\|c\|\to\infty
\end{equation*}
as $n\to\infty$.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
In this proof, we denote by $B_0(M):=\{x\in\Rbb^d: \|x\|\leq M\}$ the centered ball of radius $M$.
By continuity of the distortion objective Lemma~\ref{lem:D-cts}, for any $M\geq 1$ and $1\le k'\le k$, and for any $\epsilon>0$ there exists $\eta(M;\epsilon,k')\in(0,1]$ such that for any $\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}$ with $\pointset\subseteq B_0(M)$,
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{\pointset'\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus(\pointset,\pointset')\geq \epsilon \implies  D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) \geq \inf_{\pointset'\in\allpointsets_{k'}} D_X(\pointset'\,|\,\pointset_0) + \eta(M;\epsilon,k').
\end{equation*}
We denote $\eta(M;\epsilon):=\min_{1\le k'\le k}\eta(M;\epsilon,k')/2$.
Then, the previous equation implies that there exists $\phi(M;\epsilon)\geq M$ such that for any $1\le k'\le k$ and $\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}$ with $\pointset\subseteq B_0(M)$, the condition
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{\pointset'\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus(\pointset,\pointset')\geq \epsilon
\end{equation*}
implies that there exists
$\tilde\pointset \in \allpointsets_{k'}$ satisfying $\tilde\pointset\subseteq B_0(\phi(M;\epsilon))$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:construction_better_cluster_candidate}
	D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) \geq  D_X(\tilde \pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)+ \eta(M;\epsilon).
\end{equation}
Without loss of generality, we may also assume that $\eta(M;\epsilon)$ is non-increasing in $M$. Next, we define $\psi(M;\epsilon)\geq \phi(M;\epsilon)+1$ such that denoting by $\mu$ the law of $X$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:def_psi_function}
    \mu\paren{\Rbb^d\setminus B_0\paren{\frac{\psi(M;\epsilon)-\phi(M;\epsilon)-1}{2}}}\leq \frac{(\eta(M;\epsilon)/3)^2}{20r\phi(M;\epsilon)^2}.
\end{equation}
Again, without loss of generality, assume that $\psi(M;\epsilon)$ is non-increasing in $M$.

Note that for any set of points $\Acal\in\allpointsets_k$ and measurable region $B\subseteq\Rbb^d$, we have
\begin{align*}
	\abs{\Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in\Acal}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\notin B\}} - D_X(\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0)}
	&\leq \max_{a\in\Acal}\|a\|^2 \Pbb(X\in B) + 2\max_{a\in\Acal}\|a\|\cdot \Ebb\sqb{\|X\|  \ind\{X\in B\}} \notag\\
	&\leq \max_{a\in\Acal}\|a\|^2 \Pbb(X\in B) + 4\max_{a\in\Acal}\|a\|\cdot \sqrt{r \Pbb(X\in B)}.
\end{align*}
In the last inequality, we used the fact that $\|X\|$ is stochastically dominated by $\sqrt{r} \cdot Z$ where $Z\sim\Pareto(2)$ from Lemma~\ref{lemma:charact_finite_obj} and Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm}. Without loss of generality, we suppose $r\geq 1$. In particular, for any $\Acal\sqcup\Bcal\in \pointset_k$ such that $\Acal\subseteq B_0(M)$ and $\Pbb(X\in B)\leq \eta^2/(20rM^2)$ for some $\eta\in(0,1]$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:comparison_deleting_far_clusters}
	\abs{\Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in\Acal}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\notin B\}} - D_X(\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) } \leq \eta.
\end{equation}
Now let $\pointset\in\allpointsets_k$. We suppose that we can decompose $\pointset=\Acal\sqcup\Bcal$ with $\Acal\neq\emptyset$ such that letting $k'=\#\Acal$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:assumptions_intermediary}
	\min_{\pointset'\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)} \dhaus(\Acal,\pointset')\geq \epsilon ,\quad \max_{c\in\Acal} \|c\|\leq M,\quad \text{and} \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal} \|c\|\geq \psi(\epsilon;M).
\end{equation}
Since $\Acal\neq\emptyset$, we can fix $a\in\Acal$. Next, let $c\in\Bcal$. In particular, by \eqref{eq:assumptions_intermediary} we have $\|a\|\leq M$ and $\|b\|\geq \psi(M;\epsilon)$. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \Vcal_\pointset(c)&\subseteq\{x\in\Rbb^d: \|x-c\|\leq \|x-a\|\} \\
    &\subseteq \{x\in\Rbb^d: \psi(\epsilon;M) - \|x\|\leq \|x\|+M\} \subseteq \Rbb^d\setminus B_0\paren{\frac{\psi(M;\epsilon)-M-1}{2}}.
\end{align*}
Hence, using \eqref{eq:def_psi_function} and the fact that $\phi(M;\epsilon)\geq M$, we obtained
\begin{equation*}
	\mu\paren{\bigcup_{b\in\Bcal}\Vcal_\pointset(b)} \leq  \mu\paren{\Rbb^d\setminus B_0\paren{\frac{\psi(M;\epsilon)-\phi(M;\epsilon)-1}{2}}} \leq \frac{(\eta(M;\epsilon)/3)^2}{20 r M^2}.
\end{equation*}
Then, equation~\eqref{eq:comparison_deleting_far_clusters} applied to $\Acal$ and $B:=\bigcup_{b\in\Bcal}\Vcal_\pointset(b)$ shows that
\begin{align*}
	D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) &- \Ebb\sqb{\sum_{b\in\Bcal}(\|X-b\|^2 -\|X\|^2)\ind\{X\in\Vcal_\pointset(b)\}} - D_X( \Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) \\
	&=\Ebb\sqb{\sum_{a\in\Acal}(\|X-a\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\in\Vcal_\pointset(a)\}} - D_X( \Acal\,|\,\pointset_0)\\
	&\geq 
	\Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in \Acal}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\notin B\}} - D_X( \Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) \\
	&\geq -\frac{\eta(M;\epsilon)}{3}.
\end{align*}
Next, from equation~\eqref{eq:construction_better_cluster_candidate} there exists $\tilde\Acal\in\allpointsets_{k'}$ such that $\tilde\Acal\subseteq B_0(\phi(M;\epsilon))$ and $D_X(\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) \geq  D_X( \tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0)+ \eta(\epsilon;M)$. We now define $\tilde\pointset:=\tilde\Acal\cup \Bcal$.
Applying equation~\eqref{eq:comparison_deleting_far_clusters} to $\tilde\Acal$ and the same set $B\subseteq \tilde{\pointset}$, we get
\begin{align*}
	D_X(\tilde \pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) &- \Ebb\sqb{\sum_{b\in\Bcal}(\|X-b\|^2 -\|X\|^2)\ind\{X\in\Vcal_\pointset(b)\}} - D_X( \tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0)\\
	&= \Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in \tilde \pointset}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2)} - \Ebb\sqb{\sum_{b\in\Bcal}(\|X-b\|^2 -\|X\|^2)\ind\{X\in\Vcal_\pointset(b)\}} - D_X( \tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0)\\
	&\leq 
	\Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in \tilde\pointset}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\notin B\}} - D_X( \tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) \\
	&\leq \Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in \tilde\Acal}(\|X-c\|^2-\|X\|^2) \ind\{X\notin B\}} - D_X( \tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) \leq \frac{\eta(M;\epsilon)}{3}.
\end{align*}
In summary, we obtained
\begin{align}\label{eq:conclusion_for_recursion}
	D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) -  \inf_{\pointset'\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset'\,|\,\pointset_0) &\geq D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) -  D_X(\tilde \pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) \notag\\
	&\geq D_X(\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) - D_X(\tilde\Acal\,|\,\pointset_0) -\frac{2\eta(M;\epsilon)}{3}\geq \frac{\eta(M;\epsilon)}{3}.
\end{align}
by combining the two previous inequalities.

With this identity, we are now ready to show the following for some arbitrary $\epsilon>0$. Fix a sequence of cluster sets $\{\pointset_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ satisfying the assumptions for this result.
We aim to show that there is a decomposition of $\pointset_n = \Acal_n(\epsilon)\sqcup\Bcal_n(\epsilon)$ for $n\in\Nbb$ so that for sufficiently large $n$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:desired_decomposition_epsilon}
	\min_{1\le k'\le k}\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus(\Acal_n(\epsilon),\pointset)\leq \epsilon \quad \text{and} \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal_n(\epsilon)}\|c\|\geq \frac{1}{\epsilon}.
\end{equation}
First, by assumption, there exists $n_\epsilon$ such that for all $n\geq n_\epsilon$, we have
\begin{equation*}
	D_X(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) \leq \inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) + \frac{1}{4}\eta\paren{\psi^{(k)}(1/\epsilon;\epsilon) ;\epsilon},
\end{equation*}
where we used the notation where $\psi^{(0)}$ is the identity function and $\psi^{({\ell}+1)}(M;\epsilon):=\psi\paren{\psi^{(\ell)}(M;\epsilon);\epsilon}$ for $\ell\in\Nbb$ and any $M\geq 0$. For convenience, we denote
\begin{equation*}
	M_{\ell}:= \psi^{(\ell)}(1/\epsilon;\epsilon)\,\quad 1\le \ell \le k.
\end{equation*}
We can check that since $\psi(M;\epsilon)\geq \phi(M;\epsilon)\geq M$, for all $M\geq 0$, we have $M_{k}\geq M_{k-1}\geq\cdots \geq M_1\geq M_0:=1/\epsilon$. Now fix $n\geq n_\epsilon$. First, if we have $\min_{c\in \pointset_n} \|c\| \geq M_0$
then, the desired result \eqref{eq:desired_decomposition_epsilon} is immediate by taking $\Bcal_n(\epsilon)=\pointset_n$.
We now suppose that there is at least one element $c\in \pointset_n$ with $\|c\|<M_0$.
Then, by the pigeonhole principle, there must be some $1\le \ell \le k$ for which 
\begin{equation*}
	\#\set{c\in \pointset_n: \|c\| \in (M_{\ell-1},M_\ell)} = 0.
\end{equation*}
We now pose
\begin{equation*}
	\Acal_n(\epsilon):=  \pointset_n\cap B_0(M_{\ell-1}) \quad \text{and} \quad \Bcal_n(\epsilon) =  \pointset_n\setminus B_0(M_{\ell-1}).
\end{equation*}
By construction, we have $\Acal_n(\epsilon)\neq\emptyset$ and 
\begin{equation*}
	\max_{c\in\Acal_n(\epsilon)}\|c\| \leq M_{\ell-1}, \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal_n(\epsilon)} \|c\| \geq M_{\ell},\quad \text{and} \quad D_X(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) \leq \inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k}D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) + \frac{\eta(M_{\ell-1};\epsilon)}{4}.
\end{equation*}
Also, by construction, $M_{\ell} = \psi(\epsilon;M_{\ell-1})\geq M_{\ell-1} \geq M_0 =1/\epsilon$. Hence, equations~\eqref{eq:assumptions_intermediary} and~\eqref{eq:conclusion_for_recursion} imply that
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)} \dhaus(\Acal_n(\epsilon),\pointset) \leq \epsilon,
\end{equation*}
where $k'=\#\Acal_n(\epsilon)$. This ends the proof of~\eqref{eq:desired_decomposition_epsilon}. 

Because this holds for any $\epsilon>0$, this implies that there exists a decomposition $\pointset_n=\Acal_n\sqcup\Bcal_n$ for which
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{1\le k'\le k}\min_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_{k'}(X)}\dhaus(\Acal_n,\pointset)\to 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \min_{c\in\Bcal_n}\|c\|\to \infty
\end{equation*}
as $n\to\infty$. It only remains to check that we can take $\Acal_n$ such that $\Acal_n\neq\varnothing$. To prove that this holds, it suffices to show that
$\min_{c\in \pointset_n}\|c\|$
remains bounded.
Indeed, there exists $c_n\in\pointset_n$ for which $\mu(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\geq 1/k$. From Lemma~\ref{lemma:computation_bound_norm}, we have $\|m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\| \leq 2\sqrt{kr}$.
Next, consider the cluster centers $\pointset'_n=(\pointset_n\setminus\{c_n\})\cup\{m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\}$, where we replaced the point $c_n$ with $m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))$. Then,
\begin{align*}
	D_X(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) -D_X(\pointset_n'\,|\,\pointset_0) &= \Ebb\sqb{\min_{c\in\pointset_n}\|X-c\|^2 - \min_{c\in\pointset_n'}\|X-c\|^2}\\
	&\geq \Ebb\sqb{\paren{\|X-c_n\|^2 - \|X-m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\|^2} \ind\{X\in\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n)\}}\\
	&=\mu(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\|c_n-m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\|^2\\
	&\geq \frac{\|c_n-m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\|^2}{k}.
\end{align*}
As a result, we obtained
\begin{equation*}
	\min_{c\in \pointset_n}\|c\| \leq \|m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\|  + \|c_n-m_X(\Vcal_{\pointset_n}(c_n))\| \leq  2\sqrt{kr} + \sqrt{k\paren{D_X(\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) -\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0) }},
\end{equation*}
which remains bounded since the right-hand side converges to $2\sqrt{kr}$ as $n\to\infty$.
This ends the proof.
\end{proof}

We are now ready to prove our unified convergence result.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_unified}]
If the sequence $\{\gamma_n\}_{n\in\Nbb}$ satisfies the given assumptions, then Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_log_points_per_cluster} implies that the event
\begin{equation*}
	\Ecal:=\set{ \lim_{n\to\infty} \max_{\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma_n}} D_X(\bar\pointset_n\,|\,\pointset_0) =\inf_{\pointset\in\allpointsets_k} D_X(\pointset\,|\,\pointset_0)}
\end{equation*}
has probability one. Then, on $\Ecal$, from Lemma~\ref{lemma:can_delete_diverging_clusters} we can decompose any $\bar\pointset_n\in\bar\allpointsets_{n,k,\gamma_n}$ into $\pointset_n=\Acal_n\sqcup\Bcal_n$ to satisfy the desired result.
\end{proof}


Although the conclusion of Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_unified} is, strictly speaking, weaker than the conclusion of Theorem~\ref{thm:cluster-consistency}, we find that it is advantageous in two senses.
First, the balance constraint it requires is strictly weaker, being polylogarithmic rather than linear.
Second (and more importantly) there is no risk of misspecification.
An interesting avenue for future work would be to understand the methodological implications of Theorem~\ref{thm:consistency_unified}, and more generally the way that our theory affects practical usages of balanced variants of $k$-means clustering.

