This is somewhat expected as even classically, \emph{late fusion}-like approaches were more effective when dealing with heterogenous modalities~\citep{varshney1997distributed, ramachandram2017deep}. 
However, with the rise of powerful foundation models, this paradigm is changing, as works increasingly learn shared semantic space embeddings for different modalities. For example, vision transformers \cite{dosovitskiy2021image} have a natural unimodal token space per transformer layer,  motivating a prompt-based multimodal fusion layer to fuse the unimodal tokens at each layer \cite{Li_2023_CVPR}. Similar methods have been developed to generalize vision transformers to spectrograms \citep{gong2022ssast}. However, most such methods use a fixed fusion model wherein the fused representation is computed only once. Note that this refers not to the number of fusion layers, but rather that a fused representation is not revisited or recomputed. These fixed fusion designs however create an \textbf{information loss issue}, where any information that is not integrated into the fused representations \emph{is at risk of getting lost} deeper in the network pipeline. This creates a \textbf{fuse-it or lose-it} problem; which means that information can only be fused or lost. This information loss hinders these networks from doing conditional fusion where the network can use or revise the information present in the same embedding conditioned on the other pieces of information already extracted.


