\label{sec:prelim}

% \sidong{$\mathbf{x}$: a vector; $\mathbf{X}$: a random variable; $\mathcal{F}$: a function; $\mathbb{X}$: a space; c, K: scalars }

% \sidong{plots and figures need to be updated to reflect the notation changes}



\begin{comment}
Multimodal learning is a specific type of supervised learning problem with different types of input modalities. We are provided with a dataset of $N$ observations $\mathbb{D} = \{ (\mathbf{x}^j, \mathbf{y}^j)_{j=1}^{N}\} $, where all observed vector $\mathbf{x}^j$ come from a space $\mathbb{X}$ and $\mathbf{y}^j$ from $\mathbb{Y}$, and a loss function $\mathcal{L} : \mathbb{Y} \times \mathbb{Y} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ which is the task loss. Our goal is to learn a parametric function $\mathcal{M}: \mathbb{X} \rightarrow \mathbb{Y}$ such that the total loss  $L =  \sum_j \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x}^j),\mathbf{y}^j)$ is minimized. In multimodal fusion the space of inputs $\mathbb{X}$ naturally decomposes into $K$ different modalities $\mathbb{X} = \prod_{i=1}^K \mathbb{X}_i$. We denote the random variable of ith modality sampled from the $\mathbb{X}_i$ as $\mathbf{X}_i$, and the random variable sampled from target space $\mathbb{Y}$ as $\mathbf{Y}$. Correspondingly any observation $\mathbf{x}^j$ also decomposes into modality specific components $\mathbf{x}^j_i$ i.e. $\mathbf{x}^j = (\mathbf{x}^j_1, \mathbf{x}^j_2, \dots \mathbf{x}^j_K) $. 

A natural way to learn such a function $\mathcal{M}$ with a multimodal input is to have an \emph{embedding component}  which fuses information into a high dimensional vector in $\mathbb{R}^d$, where $d$ is the size of the embedding, and a \emph{predictive component} $\mathcal{P}$ which maps the embedding vector from $\mathbb{R}^d$ to $\mathbb{Y}$. Furthermore, since different modalities are often of distinct nature and cannot be processed by similar networks (e.g. text and image), the embedding generator is decomposed into (a) unimodal feature generators $\mathcal{G}_i : \mathbb{X}_i \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{d_i}$ which are specifically designed for each individual modality $\mathbb{X}_i$ and (b) a fusion component $\mathcal{F} : \prod_i \mathbb{R}^{d_i} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d$ which fuses information from each individual unimodal vector. $\mathcal{F}$ is provided with unimodal representations of the input $\mathbf{x}^j$ obtained through embedding networks $\mathcal{G}_i$. The unimodal feature generators $\mathcal{G}_i$ can have different kinds of layers including 2D convolution, 3D convolution and fully connected layers. $\mathcal{F}$ is the layer where the embeddings obtained from different modalities are fused. $\mathcal{F}$ is called the \emph{fusion} or \emph{shared representation} layer. $\mathcal{F}$ has to capture both unimodal dependencies (i.e. relations between features that span only one modality) and multimodal dependencies (i.e. relationships between features across multiple modalities). A vanilla $\mathcal{M}$ would fuse all the K unimodal features generated by each $\mathcal{G}_i$ from $\mathbf{x}_i$, 

defined as:

\begin{align} \label{eqn:vanilla_fusion}
\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x}) \coloneq  \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}(\mathcal{G}_1(\mathbf{x}_1),\mathcal{G}_2(\mathbf{x}_2), \cdots, \mathcal{G}_K(\mathbf{x}_K)))
\end{align}

