Classically, fusion techniques were separated into two categories, \emph{early fusion} and \emph{late fusion}, depending on where the information from each modality is integrated into the feature pipeline  \citep{varshney1997distributed, ramachandram2017deep}. While theoretically, early fusion models tend to be more expressive, in practice, they are more commonly used for homogeneous or similar modalities \citep{ramachandram2017deep}. On the other hand, late fusion models are more effective in combining diverse modalities. This has generally been attributed to the challenges like feature shifts, cross-modal distributional changes, differences in dimensionality, etc., when dealing with heterogeneities across diverse modalities such as text and image \citep{mogadala2021trends, yan2021deep}. To break the expressivity limits in early and late fusion, researchers have explored more flexible fusion techniques. A typical approach is to set a gating function at every fusion position controlled by deep neural networks \citep{xue2023dynamic}. \citet{pmlr-v202-zhang23ar} gives a theoretical analysis on a general dynamic fusion approach. \citet{multilevel} conducts multimodal fusion at every layer. 

% \ssh{the lines from here till fusion techniques need to be made coherent}

In recent years, with the wide success of transformer \citep{vaswani2017attention} based models, researchers are increasingly focusing on incorporating multimodal information in transformers. Since transformers work on abstract sequence of tokens, multiple input modalities can be given jointly to a transformer \citep{li2019visualbert}; typically using first a modality specific tokenizer. Also, inspired by the natural unimodal token space per transformer layer, vision transformer-based \cite{dosovitskiy2021image} method brings up a prompt-based multimodal fusion layer to fuse the unimodal tokens at each layer for multimodal computer vision tasks \cite{Li_2023_CVPR}. The Perceiver model \citep{jaegle2021perceiver} introduces an iterative attention mechanism which takes concatenated raw multimodal signals as inputs. Multimodal transformers have been applied to various tasks including audio enhancement \citep{ephrat2018looking, tzinis2020into}, speech recognition \citep{harwath2016unsupervised}, cross-modal sequence generation \citep{li2020learning} etc. 

