Consider the scenario where we want to identify the clean audio tracks of two speakers from a video recording them talking to each other in a noisy environment. In this case, the task is to separate the clean audio utterances of the two speakers from the combined audio-track which potentially can include additional background noise. In principle, this audio separation task is solvable with the unimodal audio mixture input. However, due to factors including additional noise, recording qualities, accents and tones of the speakers,  certain utterances can be difficult to identify without the assistance of other cues such as from the facial movements. For example, a female speaker says "WE MUST ADOPT THAT WAY OF" while the other female speaker says "THEY'RE LIKE THE BASEBALL SCOUTS 20 YEARS", but because of the distortion from the noise and the tones of the two speakers, the two phrases sound close to each other at the beginning words "WE MUST" and "THEY'RE". Only when a model gains access to the lip regions of the two speakers, which have significantly different moves, can it identify the two phrases correctly. The task then becomes an AVSS task.

Now assume we are modeling the AVSS task of separating the utterances of "WE MUST ADOPT THAT WAY OF" and "THEY'RE LIKE THE BASEBALL SCOUTS 20 YEARS" via multimodal fusion from the noisy audio record and the video frames of the speakers lip movements. 
In a standard fusion architecture for this problem, the modalities are first embedded into high-dimensional vector spaces, before using a neural network to fuse the vectors. A significant part of the representation capacity of the latent audio feature embedders (i.e. the networks that produce the latent embeddings) might be devoted to improving the signal-to-distortion ratio between the separated audio and the clean audio before possibly receiving any facial information. 
If the utterances have high similarity, the fusion model loses the key information about the correct phrases and might be using its layers to improve latent embeddings corresponding to "THEY'RE ADOPT THAT WAY OF" and "WE MUST LIKE THE BASEBALL SCOUTS 20 YEARS", instead of the correct "WE MUST ADOPT THAT WAY OF " and "THEY'RE LIKE THE BASEBALL SCOUTS 20 YEARS" phrases. More generally, if the unimodal layers are bottlenecked or not powerful enough, the required information for the task might be lost or compressed too much to be recovered correctly. Note that even if the fusion was done at a comparatively earlier stage, the problem remains; although now it depends on whether the fusion layer keeps retaining the relevant information. This is what we label the \textbf{fuse-it or lose-it} problem. Furthermore, the earlier such a fusion layer, the more challenging it might be to incorporate information from the two heterogeneous modalities in this task. No guarantee exists that fusing visual representation to the audio modality at an early stage can efficiently deliver the information that "WE MUST" comes before "ADOPT". For instance, if the lip region video frames are processed by several convolutional layers, the early latent representations are still carrying irrelevant visual information such as the speaker's nose and the background. Fusing such visual representations of high nuisance with the audio modality introduces more high-level noise and can lead to representing an incorrect utterance structure. 

Figure \ref{fig:motiv} shows an abstract graphical model for the AVSS task. $\mathbf{S}$ represents the entire input mixture audio, $\mathbf{V}$ represents the lip region video frames of the two speakers, and $\mathbf{Z}$ represents an abstract state of the environment (audio and visual features of the two speakers). $\mathbf{S}$ and $\mathbf{V}$ are examples of $\mathbf{X}_1$ and $\mathbf{X}_2$ stated in section \ref{sec:prelim}. The output $(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_1, \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2)$ (e.g., clean separated audios) serves as the target variable $\mathbf{Y}$ in section \ref{sec:prelim} the and is determined by the feature function $h$, so $(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_1, \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2) \leftarrow h(\mathbf{Z})$ ( i.e., $h(\mathbf{Z})$ contains sufficient statistics to generate the mask layers for the two speakers' audio tracks separately). The information about these features is present in $\mathbf{S}$ mapped by a function $f$, i.e. $\exists f, s.t. f(\mathbf{S}) = h(\mathbf{Z})$; however, $\mathbf{S}$ has nuisance variables (e.g., background noise) or a corrupted version of $h(\mathbf{Z})$. $g(\mathbf{Z})$ represents visual information of the two speakers' lip region. The separation mask layer $h(\mathbf{Z})$ and visual characters $g(\mathbf{Z})$ combined identify (part of) the mixture audio $\mathbf{S}$.  
% \sidong{It is still weird that we say a mask layer and a video form part of the audio mixture. Can we phrase it better?}
In this case, $(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_1, \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2)$ may not be identifiable purely via $\mathbf{S}$ . For the audio separation example, this is because $\mathbf{S}$ contains utterances that sound close to each other due to noise and tones of the speaker. But in the presence of input $\mathbf{V}$  (in this case lip region video frames), the target is identifiable \textit{even} if $\mathbf{V}$ by itself is not informative about $(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_1, \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2)$. In late fusion scenario, if the modeling from $\mathbf{S}$ to $h(\mathbf{Z})$ is not sufficiently expressive 
% \sidong{I remove $G_1(X)$ and f, seems introducing new notation here is redundant and confusing}
, the overall networks may not be able to learn sufficiently informative latent representations from unimodal $\mathbf{S}$ and fuse with lip region visual modality $\mathbf{V}$ during late fusion, when the fusion network has already pooled together $h$ and $g$ in a non-invertible manner. While in the case of early fusion, due to introducing highly noised visual representations to the latent space of $\mathbf{S}$, the model may even fail to recover $h(\mathbf{Z})$ from $\mathbf{S}$.  

% \sidong{The motivation is using early \& late fusion as negative examples, so I think we still keep them?}

% \sidong{We need to emphasize profusion's flexibility here, instead of the old paragraph that say put late fusion to early fusion input}

To summarize, fusion at higher-level features runs into a “fuse it or lose it” situation where relevant information – especially conditionally relevant information – introduced to the main modality in a fixed position via model design can either be informative but not fused with main modality at the correct position to prevent information loss, or be too overwhelmed with nuisance to guide the learning process of the main modality.
% that is fused too late by the fusion layer is at risk of being lost, while being fused too early is at risk of carrying too much nuisance. 
This leads us to our basic problem: designing a generic approach with a flexible fusion mechanism that can prevent information loss and extract informative multimodal information relevant to the task. To this end, we propose a scheme that automatically connects cross-stage multimodal fusion features for informative unimodal feature generators.
% late-stage multi-modal fusion features to the early stages of unimodal feature generators.

\subsection{Pro-Fusion}
\label{sec: pro-fusion}


% \sidong{Notation fixed, but need to be reviewed. Also need to consider any better phrase to deliver the idea of flexible fusion}

We build a scheme based on backprojective connections which can be applied to any given base architecture. 
Our scheme considers any given base design as a single step of an iterative process. The base design is augmented to take an additional context vector as input, which serves to provide information from `late' fused representations.
At each iteration, the current output representations of the base model are provided via the context vector as an additional input for the next step.  Formally, given a base model $\mathcal{M}$ with input $\mathbf{x}^j = (\mathbf{x}_1^j, \mathbf{x}_2^j, \cdots, \mathbf{x}_k^j)$, we want to create an augmented model $\hat{\mathcal{M}} : \mathbb{X} \times \mathbb{R}^{d} \rightarrow \mathbb{Y}$ with additional random variable $\mathbf{C} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ such that $ \mathbf{c} = 0 \implies \hat{\mathcal{M}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{c}) = \mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x})$. 

We create the desired network $\hat{\mathcal{M}}$ by providing $\mathbf{c}$ to the unimodal feature generators $\mathcal{G}_i$. We use the output of the fusion layer $\mathcal{F}$ and project it back into the network as $\mathbf{c}_{r+1}$  at iteration $r$ via the matrix/function $\mathcal{W}_r$. This creates an iterative network which we run for $R$ steps. The final vector $\mathbf{c}_R$ after $R$ steps serves as the output of fusion which is then provided to the predictor model $\mathcal{P}$. The R-step iterative approach is formally demonstrated in algorithm (\ref{algo: profusion}), and conceptually visualized in figure \ref{fig:base_fusion}.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Pro-Fusion}\label{algo: profusion}
\begin{algorithmic}
\Require $\mathbf{x}_i$ for $i$ in modality indexing 1 to K  \Comment{The data sample of K modalities}
\Require $\mathcal{G}_i(\cdot)$ for $i$ in modality indexing 1 to K  \Comment{The unimodal feature generator for K modalities}
\Require $\mathcal{F}(\cdot)$  \Comment{The fusion component}
\Require $\mathcal{P}(\cdot)$  \Comment{The predictive component}
\Require $\mathcal{W}_i(\cdot)$ for $i$ ranging from $0$ to $R-1$ 
\Require $R$  \Comment{The number of iteration loops}
\State $\mathbf{c_0} \gets \text{random initialization}$
\State $i \gets 0$
\While{$i < R$}
  \State $\mathbf{c}_{i+1} \gets \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{G}_1(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{c}_i), \cdots, \mathcal{G}_K(\mathbf{x}_K, \mathbf{c}_i))$
  \State $\mathbf{c}_{i+1} \gets \mathcal{W}_i(\mathbf{c}_{i+1})$
  \State $i \gets i+1$
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_R)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}



The ability to send information backward in the network addresses the problem raised earlier in Section~\ref{sec:motiv}. The encoder $\mathcal{G}$ for $\mathbf{S}$ can now gain a handle on $g(\mathbf{Z})$ via the fused output $\mathbf{c}_r$ at iteration $r$. Specifically if the model can compute $g(\mathbf{Z})$ from $\mathcal{W}_{r}(\mathbf{c}_{r+1})$, then in the next iteration step, one can recover from $\mathbf{S}$ the real value of $h(\mathbf{Z})$, which then directly determines the target $\mathbf{Y}$. On the other hand if $\mathbf{V}$ is not useful or if $\mathcal{G}$ cannot process the fused vector efficiently, then $\mathcal{W}(.)$ can be zeroed out and the overall model is no worse than the baseline model.

